\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US lawmakers call to modernize Osha as hundreds die on the job each day\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 824 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2023\par 11:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Reintroduced federal legislation pushes to expand Osha coverage in 24 states not currently covered by the act\par \par \uc2 Every day {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/media/5322"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 343 workers}}}\uc2  die from hazardous working conditions in the US. In 2021, the latest year with data available, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/media/5322"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 5,190 workers}}}\uc2  in the US were killed on the job and an estimated 120,000 deaths were attributed to occupational diseases.\par \par \uc2 Since 1970, more than 429,000 workers have been killed on the job, but only 128 of those cases have been criminally prosecuted under the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/general/aboutdol/history/osha"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Act}}}\uc2  (\uc2 Osha\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Now a new report and reintroduced federal legislation is pushing to modernize Osha.\par \par \uc2 Democratic congressmen Joe Courtney and Bobby Scott have co-authored the Protecting America\u8217\'20\'19s Workers Act, which was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.safetyandhealthmagazine.com/articles/23901-democratic-lawmakers-reintroduce-protecting-americas-workers-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reintroduced}}}\uc2  to Congress on 28 April, on Workers\u8217\'20\'19 Memorial Day.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe history of Osha shows that there really is a systemic problem in terms of that mission of getting the rules aligned with the evolving technology that goes into a whole host of sectors in the US economy, whether it\u8217\'20\'19s home construction, healthcare settings, manufacturing, the new processes, the new chemicals, the new machinery that as part of a very dynamic economy, it\u8217\'20\'19s just kind of rocketed past the Osha rules that are in place to protect people,\u8221\'20\'1D said Courtney.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just really trying to get a system of workforce protection that is really connected to the actual workplaces that people are going to every day in the 21st century.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://courtney.house.gov/sites/evo-subsites/courtney.house.gov/files/evo-media-document/2023-04-28-pawa-fact-sheet.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bill}}}\uc2  includes expanding Osha coverage to the estimated 8 million state and local government workers in 24 states not currently covered by Osha, reinstating an employer record-keeping rule of illnesses and injuries rolled back under the Trump administration, providing authority for increased civil penalties for serious Osha violations, and authorizing felony penalties against employers who knowingly commit Osha violations that result in the death or serious harm of a worker. The bill would also establish rights for families who lose a loved one to a workplace fatality and require Osha to investigate all cases of death or serious injury that occur in a workplace.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Death on the Job 2023 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/sites/default/files/2023-04/2303_DOTJ_2023_final%20%283%29.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2 , released on 26 April, outlines the \u8220\'20\'1Ctoll of neglect\u8221\'20\'1D that comes from inadequately addressing workplace safety issues amid aggressive opposition from industry groups and employers against improving and enforcing workers protections.\par \par \uc2 The report cites low civil penalties for safety violations issued by Osha, understaffing and underfunding at Osha, the millions of workers who are currently not covered under Osha which include independent contractors and federal, state and local public workers, inadequate retaliation protections for workers to speak out and report safety issues, and the need to improve and expand data on worker injuries and illnesses.\par \par \uc2 For Black workers, the workplace fatality rate increased from 3.5 per 100,000 workers in 2020 to 4.0 in 2021, the highest rate in a decade, while Latino workers currently have a worker fatality rate of 4.5 per every 100,000 workers, 25% higher than the national average.\par \par \uc2 Younger and older workers are at higher risk for workplace fatalities. Three hundred and fifty workers under the age of 25 died on the job in 2021, while workers over 65 have a risk of 2.3 times higher than other workers of dying on the job.\par \par \uc2 Courtney cited a 2010 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.csb.gov/kleen-energy-natural-gas-explosion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 incident}}}\uc2  in Connecticut where six workers were killed in an explosion at a Kleen Energy Systems power plant during cleanup of debris in pipes as an example of an avoidable and tragic workplace fatality incident.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs the process of an investigation went on, it was clear that what caused that was really very well known, unsafe practices, that the \uc2 Chemical Safety Board\uc2 , which is a sort of an arm of OshaA, had long identified as being the wrong way to clean the tubes,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The legislation has been introduced numerous times in Congress over the past two decades. Senator Pat Murray of Washington {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ehstoday.com/standards/osha/article/21918039/sen-murray-protecting-americas-workers-act-a-longoverdue-update-to-osh-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called}}}\uc2  the legislation \u8220\'20\'1Clong overdue\u8221\'20\'1D when she was reintroducing it in 2013. Joe Biden was a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.safetyandhealthmagazine.com/articles/23901-democratic-lawmakers-reintroduce-protecting-americas-workers-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 co-sponsor}}}\uc2  of previous versions of the bill while serving in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 In 2009 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.osha.gov/news/testimonies/04272010"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 testimony}}}\uc2  in support of the bill, Osha\u8217\'20\'19s assistant secretary David Michaels said the 1970 Osha Act was \u8220\'20\'1Ctragically outdated and inadequate\u8221\'20\'1D, and yet few to no changes or updates have been made in over 50 years since its passage.\par \par \uc2 Courtney noted the political challenges faced in passing the Protecting America\u8217\'20\'19s Workers Act given the current House is under Republican control. But a companion bill in the Senate will be introduced and Courtney said it was important to keep introducing the bill to keep the issues visible.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think that at some point, the external pressure is going to reach the breaking point in terms of getting this place to move,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230510ej5a001p6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
