\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US employers added 253,000 jobs in April showing jobs market remains robust\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani in New York \par \uc2 619 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2023\par 18:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 47\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 April broke downward trend in growth of labor market, which has been slowing over the last few months\par \par \uc2 US employers added 253,000 jobs in April, a sign that the resilient jobs market remains robust even after a year of \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest rate rises.\par \par \uc2 Labor market growth has been slowing over the last few months, from 472,000 jobs in January to a revised 165,000 in March. April broke that downward trend in growth, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS)}}}\uc2  reported on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate fell slightly to 3.4%, a 0.1% decrease compared to March.\par \par \uc2 The gains were broad-based, with health, education and leisure and hospitality reporting the largest numbers of new jobs. Unemployment for Black Americans dipped below 5% for the first time but, at 4.7%, remained well above the 3.1% experienced by white Americans.\par \par \uc2 The figures further complicate the picture for the Fed which has been raising rates to cool the economy and bring down inflation. The hikes had been expected to dampen US hiring.\par \par \uc2 Economists had expected the US to add 180,000 jobs over the month. Recent data had pointed to signs that jobs growth has been slowing. The monthly Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey, known as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/jolts.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jolt}}}\uc2 , for March was released by the \uc2 BLS\uc2  earlier this week and showed job openings were at their lowest level since April 2021, falling for a third month straight.\par \par \uc2 Layoffs increased to 1.8m, 248,000 more compared with last month and the highest number since December 2020. The construction industry saw the highest number of layoffs as the housing market has cooled with the rise of interest rates.\par \par \uc2 April\u8217\'20\'19s job figures were released just two days after the Fed announced its 10th {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/may/03/interest-rates-increase-federal-reserve-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interest rate hike}}}\uc2  in just over a year, raising rates by a quarter-point to 5% to 5.25%, the highest rate in 16 years.\par \par \uc2 Fed officials on Wednesday had suggested they may stop interest rate rises in the near future as they see the effects play out in the economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are some signs that supply and demand in the labor market are coming back into balance,\u8221\'20\'1D the Fed chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , said at a press conference on Wednesday. He said the \u8220\'20\'1Ceconomy is likely to face further headwinds from tighter credit conditions\u8221\'20\'1D, meaning the full effects of the interest-rate hikes have yet to be seen.\par \par \uc2 This marks a significant change in tone as Fed officials, especially Powell, have in the past reiterated that more rate rises would be needed to reach their target inflation rate of 2%. But now, the economy is \u8220\'20\'1Cseeing the effects of our policy tightening on demand and the most interest-rate-sensitive sectors of the economy, particularly housing and investment\u8221\'20\'1D, Powell said.\par \par \uc2 Rather than signaling future increases, he added, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe will be driven by incoming data, meeting by meeting\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Powell, at the Wednesday press conference, said he believes it is more likely than not that the US will avoid a recession, especially since unemployment is still low and the job market is still growing, but he said there was a possibility \u8220\'20\'1Cthat we will have what I hope would be a mild recession\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Powell also noted that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe severe period of stress\u8221\'20\'1D of the banking crisis has been resolved, but almost immediately after the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s interest rate hike. Shares of PacWest, a mid-sized bank based in California, started to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/may/04/shares-in-california-lender-pacwest-plummet-amid-fears-of-new-us-banking-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tumble}}}\uc2 . Western Alliance, based in Phoenix, also saw its shares fall following news of PacWest. Both banks say they are not experiencing changes in their deposit flows.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230505ej55001s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
