\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 US workers deserve a break. It\u8217\'20\'19s time for a 32-hour working week\b0\par\par\uc2 Bernie Sanders \par \uc2 975 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2023\par 10:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 American workers are more productive than ever, but aren\u8217\'20\'19t feeling the benefit. Let\u8217\'20\'19s learn from Europe and reduce our hours\par \par \uc2 In 1938, as a result of a massive grassroots effort by the trade union movement, the Fair Labor Standards Act was enacted by Congress to reduce the work week to 40 hours. Back then, the American people were sick and tired of working 80, 90, 100 hours a week with very little time for rest, relaxation or quality time with their families. They demanded change and they won a huge victory. That\u8217\'20\'19s the good news.\par \par \uc2 The bad news is that despite an explosion in technology, major increases in worker productivity, and transformational changes in the workplace and American society, the Fair Labor Standards Act has not been reformed in 80 years. The result: millions of Americans are working longer hours for lower wages, with the average worker making nearly $50 a week less than he or she did 50 years ago, after adjusting for inflation. Further, family life is suffering, as parents don\u8217\'20\'19t have adequate time for their kids, life expectancy for working people is in decline, and increased stress is a major factor in the mental health crisis we are now experiencing.\par \par \uc2 Compared with other countries, our workplace record is not good. In 2021, American employees {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://stats.oecd.org/viewhtml.aspx?datasetcode=ANHRS&lang=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worked}}}\uc2  184 more hours than Japanese workers, 294 more hours than British workers, and 442 more hours than German workers. Unbelievably, in 2023 there are millions of Americans who work at jobs with no vacation time.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s time to reduce the work week to 32 hours with no loss in pay. It\u8217\'20\'19s time to reduce the stress level in our country and allow Americans to enjoy a better quality of life. It\u8217\'20\'19s time to make sure that working people benefit from rapidly increasing technology, not just large corporations that are already doing phenomenally well.\par \par \uc2 Think about all of the extraordinary changes that have taken place in the workplace over the past several decades. When I was elected mayor of Burlington, Vermont, in 1981, there were no computers in city hall. There were no chatboxes, no printers, no emails, no calculators, no cellphones, no conference calling or Zoom.\par \par \uc2 In factories and warehouses, robots and sophisticated machinery did not exist or were only used in primitive forms.\par \par \uc2 In grocery stores and shops of all kinds, there were no checkout counters that utilized bar codes.\par \par \uc2 Studies have shown that despite working fewer hours, workers are either more, or just as, productive during a four-day work week\par \par \uc2 As a result of the extraordinary technological transformation that we have seen in recent years, American workers are now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/OPHNFB#0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 480%}}}\uc2  more productive than they were in the 1940s.\par \par \uc2 In addition, there are far more workers today. In the 1940s, less than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/LNS11300060"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 65%}}}\uc2  of Americans between 25 and 54 were in the workforce. Today, with most families requiring two breadwinners to pay the bills, that number is over 83%.\par \par \uc2 Yet despite all of these incredible gains in productivity, over 40% of US employees now work more than 45 hours per week; 12% work more than 60 hours a week; and the average worker now works {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/1720/work-work-place.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 43 hours per week}}}\uc2 . Many are on their computers or answering emails seven days a week.\par \par \uc2 Moving to a 32-hour work week with no loss of pay is not a radical idea. In fact, movement in that direction is already taking place in other developed countries. France, the seventh-largest economy in the world, has a 35-hour work week and is considering reducing it to 32. The work week in Norway and Denmark is about 37 hours.\par \par \uc2 Recently, the United Kingdom conducted a four-day pilot program of 3,000 workers at over 60 companies. Not surprisingly, it showed that happy workers were more productive. The pilot was so successful that 92% of the companies that participated decided to maintain a four-day week, because of the benefits to both employers and employees.\par \par \uc2 Another pilot of nearly 1,000 workers at 33 companies in seven countries {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static1.squarespace.com/static/60b956cbe7bf6f2efd86b04e/t/6387a0e53881be1284cb046e/1669832945858/The+Four+Day+Week-+Assessing+Global+Trials+of+Reduced+Work+Time+with+No+Reduction+in+Pay+%E2%80%93+A+%E2%80%93+30112022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that revenue increased by more than 37% in the companies that participated and 97% of workers were happy with the four-day workweek.\par \par \uc2 Studies have shown that despite working fewer hours, workers are either more, or just as, productive during a four-day work week. One study found that worker productivity increased {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.4dayweek.com/why-pilot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 55%}}}\uc2  after companies implemented a four-day week. A trial of four-day work weeks for public-sector workers in Iceland {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://autonomy.work/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/ICELAND_4DW.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that productivity remained the same or improved across the majority of workplaces. In 2019, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  tested a four-day work week in Japan and reported a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/microsoft-4-day-work-week-boosts-productivity-2019-11?r=US&IR=T"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 40%}}}\uc2  increase in productivity.\par \par \uc2 In addition, 57% of workers in companies that have moved to a four-day work week have indicated that they are less likely to quit their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, at a time when so many of our people are struggling with their mental health, 71% of workers in companies that have moved to a four-day work week report feeling less burnout, 39% reported feeling less stress and 46% reported feeling less fatigued.\par \par \uc2 As much as technology and worker productivity has exploded in recent years, there is no debate that new breakthroughs in artificial intelligence and robotics will only accelerate the transformation of our economy. That transformation should benefit all, not just the few. It should create more time for friends and family, more time for rest and relaxation, more time for all of us to develop our human potential.\par \par \uc2 Eighty-three years after President Franklin Delano Roosevelt signed a 40-hour work week into law, it\u8217\'20\'19s time for us to move to a 32-hour work week at no loss of pay.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230504ej54001bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
