\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Pens down!\u8217\'20\'19: Hollywood writers strike as late-night comedy shows go dark\b0\par\par\uc2 Lois Beckett in Los Angeles \par \uc2 1,116 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2023\par 20:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Members of the Writers Guild of America picket major studios as they demand higher pay and rules for AI\par \par \uc2 Outside Amazon Studios in Los Angeles, the striking writers of Hollywood had a promise for studio executives: \u8220\'20\'1CAI will replace you before it replaces us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Diandra Pendleton-Thompson, 32, was carrying the sign with the AI slogan, and gearing up for what might be a long battle with the Alliance of Motion Picture and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/television"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Television}}}\uc2  Producers.\par \par \uc2 The 11,500 members of the Writers Guild of America went on strike on Tuesday, sending hundreds in Los Angeles and New York to picket lines outside of major studios such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , Paramount and \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2 , instead of their writers\u8217\'20\'19 rooms. As they negotiate a new contract with major studios, writers are arguing that the rise of the online streaming era has left them behind, leading to worse pay and less stable work, and demanding new rules for how studios use artificial intelligence in film and television writing.\par \par \uc2 Some effects of the new strike will be visible to audiences immediately: late-night comedy shows went dark on Tuesday, with favorites like The Daily Show and Saturday Night Live expected to air reruns instead of new material. It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear how long the writers will strike before their union can reach an agreement with studio executives. The last Hollywood writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike, in 2007, lasted 100 days.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFists up, pens down, LA is a union town!\u8221\'20\'1D the writers outside Amazon Studios chanted on Tuesday, with many striking writers wearing blue Writers Guild T-shirts as they marched, demanding better pay and protections in an era of streaming dominance.\par \par \uc2 For Pendleton-Thompson, a Star Trek writer who spent years working as a Hollywood assistant before joining the Writers Guild in 2021, the strike means financial uncertainty, and negotiations over dream film projects suddenly in limbo. But she believes the fight is worth it.\par \par \uc2 She is especially concerned about studios using AI for stories about people of color and people with disabilities: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to get the stories of people who have been disempowered told through the voice of the algorithm rather than people who have experienced it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s beginning of a bigger conversation about how AI is going to be used to continue to funnel money straight to the top, rather than distributing it to the hard-working people who built this industry,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 Still, the necessity of the strike, coming so soon after the economic challenges of the pandemic, is \u8220\'20\'1Csad\u8221\'20\'1D, particularly for newer writers like herself, Pendleton-Thompson said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to see that those footholds that we\u8217\'20\'19ve just gotten seem like they\u8217\'20\'19re slipping away,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Brittani Nichols, a writer for the hit series Abbott Elementary, was picketing outside the \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2  studio on Tuesday, rather than starting work on her show\u8217\'20\'19s third season, which was scheduled to begin this week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a pretty funny coincidence that when the Writers Guild becomes the most diverse it\u8217\'20\'19s ever been, that the studios decide to pull the rug out from under our feet,\u8221\'20\'1D Nichols said, arguing that writers were increasingly treated like gig workers, not artists. \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like we are being turned into content farms.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many of the Writers Guild captains, who are organizing the picket lines, are \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople of color, and people from not the most privileged backgrounds, who are stepping up and saying: \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re not going to take this,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Nichols said. \u8220\'20\'1CWriting was a path to a middle-class life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow that\u8217\'20\'19s being ripped away from us,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not a sustainable career as it stands.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, another writer outside the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  studios carried a sign that read: \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t spell diversity without PAY US!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Erika L Johnson, a writer for Ugly Betty, Queen Sugar and The Good Lord Bird, who started out as a Hollywood assistant during the last writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike, said it had been heartening to see more writers of color in the industry. A networking group she founded for Black women writers now has more than 200 members, she said.\par \par \uc2 But since the last writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike, in 2007, studios have invented new \u8220\'20\'1Cloopholes\u8221\'20\'1D that result in more work for less pay, like the \u8220\'20\'1Cmini rooms\u8221\'20\'1D of writers that finalize scripts even before a show has been greenlit, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe struggle continues,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 Along with the gaps in compensation between network TV and streaming shows, the \u8220\'20\'1Cmini rooms\u8221\'20\'1D have become a major focus on the strike: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat if I told my landlord that this month I\u8217\'20\'19m just going to pay a mini rent?\u8221\'20\'1D comedian Ashley Nicole Black quipped on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some writers said that the strike was forcing a reckoning with the gap between perceptions of Hollywood success and an often rocky financial reality for most workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs we tell the truth about what happens behind the curtains in this business, it\u8217\'20\'19s not all the celebrities, it\u8217\'20\'19s not all the Met Gala,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alex O\u8217\'20\'19Keefe, a speechwriter turned TV writer for the critically acclaimed show The Bear, set in a Chicago sandwich shop.\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Keefe said that he was still making payments on the bow tie he rented to attend an award gala for The Bear, and that his bank account had had a negative balance, even as he was posing for a Getty photographer.\par \par \uc2 The writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike is expected to have ripple effects for workers across Hollywood. But unions for other film industry workers, from the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  to unions representing directors, actors, stagehands and hair and makeup specialists, have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/writers-strike-picket-line-1235477002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued statements supporting}}}\uc2  the writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike. More Hollywood strikes could be coming this summer: contracts for both the \uc2 Directors Guild of America\uc2  and Sag-Aftra, the actors union, expire in June.\par \par \uc2 As cars rolled past the picket line outside \uc2 Amazon\uc2  studios in Culver City, many drivers honked their horns approvingly, and the picketing writers responded with cheers.\par \par \uc2 Studio executives have been expecting the strike for months and making contingency plans for how to operate without writers, including rushing to get scripts in before the strike deadline.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re assuming the worst from a business perspective,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 Warner Bros Discovery\uc2 , said last month. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve got ourselves ready. We\u8217\'20\'19ve had a lot of content that\u8217\'20\'19s been produced.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Overseas series could also fill some of the void. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a large base of upcoming shows and films from around the world,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2  co-chief executive, said on the company\u8217\'20\'19s earnings call in April.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230503ej53001h8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
