\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 Hairdressers, camerapeople, caterers: the \u8216\'20\'18scary\u8217\'20\'19 ripple effect of the Hollywood writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Wilfred Chan \par \uc2 1,304 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2023\par 20:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers across the industry are poised to lose work as productions shut down, but they feel solidarity: \u8216\'20\'18This struggle is our struggle\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 As screenwriters across the country go on strike for the first time in 15 years, it won\u8217\'20\'19t just be writers\u8217\'20\'19 rooms that are empty. Without writers, studios must postpone or shut down productions, bringing shows off the air and affecting virtually every profession in the TV and film industry, from cinematographers to caterers, with effects rippling out to the broader economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis struggle is our struggle,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cory Hunter, a Los Angeles-based camera operator. \u8220\'20\'1CWe all have been squeezed in the change to streaming.\u8221\'20\'1D Without jobs, he\u8217\'20\'19s planning to get through the next few months by helping a family member do real estate work.\par \par \uc2 Darrius Peace, an Alabama-based hairstylist who often flies out to work on movie sets, where he specializes in Afro-texture hair, says he\u8217\'20\'19ll have to replace those jobs with more hours at his salon.\par \par \uc2 Paige Simmons, the owner of Dine With 9, a Los Angeles caterer for production crews, says \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s a little scary right now\u8221\'20\'1D: multiple clients have canceled their orders, and unlike during the height of the pandemic, there\u8217\'20\'19s no federal aid coming for a writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike.\par \par \uc2 While writers are stopping work with the hope of improving their remuneration, the rest of the film industry is simply waiting to hear when they can go back to work. Nevertheless, there is a remarkable amount of solidarity.\par \par \uc2 Peace is cheering on the writers: \u8220\'20\'1CRight now, it may hurt. But in the long run, it\u8217\'20\'19s worth it. Once the writers succeed, everybody else can demand more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Simmons\uc2  supports the strike because many of her serving staff also work in the entertainment industry. \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone understands the importance of solidarity,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 The entertainment business is highly profitable. In recent years, the combined profits of giants like \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Comcast\uc2  and Paramount have reached close to $30bn a year, as their streaming services continue to add subscribers. CEO pay has soared, with the top 12 entertainment bosses receiving about $1bn in total pay in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Entertainment industry workers say that wealth hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been shared with them, and it\u8217\'20\'19s getting harder to stay afloat. After a long drought of opportunities during the pandemic, workers have been hit by recent layoffs and declining job openings. Studios are cutting positions and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/bvmqkv/netflix-anime-dog-and-the-boy-ai-generated-art"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 experimenting}}}\uc2  with AI to replace roles.\par \par \uc2 With talk of the strike looming for months, producers have held back projects in anticipation, \u8220\'20\'1Cso many of us already were kind of in \u8216\'20\'18famine mode\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D, Hunter says.\par \par \uc2 The strike began on Tuesday after the Writers Guild of America and the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  (\uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ), which negotiates on behalf of major studios, failed to reach a new contract agreement. According to the Writers Guild, streaming platforms have upended long-held labor standards by shortening TV seasons and hiring fewer writers for briefer stints \u8211\'20\'13 essentially turning them into gig workers.\par \par \uc2 In the past, entertainment unions have not always agreed on strikes \u8211\'20\'13 sometimes even openly feuding during them \u8211\'20\'13 but this time there\u8217\'20\'19s a remarkable sense of solidarity, at least for now. The International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees (IATSE), the actors\u8217\'20\'19 union Sag-Aftra, and the Hollywood Teamsters union are among guilds that have expressed support for the strike.\par \par \uc2 While these unions aren\u8217\'20\'19t on strike, their members may choose not to cross picket lines \u8211\'20\'13 a decision protected by some of their contracts. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Teamsters\uc2  don\u8217\'20\'19t just talk about solidarity, we are contractually protected to walk the walk,\u8221\'20\'1D the union said in recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ht399.org/app/uploads/2023/05/Teamsters-FAQ-2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 guidance}}}\uc2  sent to its members.\par \par \uc2 One big reason for the unity is a pervasive feeling of worsening inequality, says Jonathan Handel, a Los Angeles-based lawyer and entertainment union expert. As streaming companies \u8220\'20\'1Cspend billions of dollars building out platforms and profit-generation machines\u8221\'20\'1D, writers are demanding \u8220\'20\'1Ca fair share of that and sustainable careers, and that\u8217\'20\'19s a message that resonates with the rest of the workforce as well\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s at stake in the fight is the future of the entertainment business model, says Handel. The last writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike in 2007 was in part a disagreement over the residuals \u8211\'20\'13 a type of royalties \u8211\'20\'13 that writers could earn from new formats like DVDs and online downloads. Streaming is an even bigger shift. \u8220\'20\'1CWe haven\u8217\'20\'19t seen such a dramatic and tumultuous period of technological change in the film entertainment industry since after the second world war,\u8221\'20\'1D he says, when Americans switched from theaters to watching TV at home.\par \par \uc2 Scott Leslie, a Studio City, Los Angeles-based prop man and set dresser at \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2  and a member of IATSE, which represents production crews, says his work hours have dropped during the streaming era, and he fears layoffs will be next. His wife, Julie Ganis, a freelance book editor, worries what will happen to them and their son in college if Leslie loses his job, \u8220\'20\'1Calong with his union paychecks and union benefits, like healthcare\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 With the strike starting, Ganis is trying to renegotiate their son\u8217\'20\'19s financial aid package: \u8220\'20\'1CMy income is not enough to support the family.\u8221\'20\'1D But the couple staunchly backs the writers: \u8220\'20\'1CI really want them to hold the line and get what\u8217\'20\'19s right, even though it\u8217\'20\'19ll cost the town lots of money and wages and ancillary things,\u8221\'20\'1D Leslie says. His wife agrees: \u8220\'20\'1COur enemy is not the writers, it\u8217\'20\'19s the corporate overlords who are putting skyrocketing profits for Wall Street ahead of everything.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hunter, the camera operator and another IATSE member, says he\u8217\'20\'19s been having conversations with other camera operators about how to find side hustles. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re supposed to be in this amazing industry with great careers, yet so many of us are having to look elsewhere to make ends meet. Whether that\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment every 18 months, or side hustles, or whatever, the viability of our careers is at stake,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what really connected with me when the writers say it\u8217\'20\'19s a critical moment for them as the viability of their livelihood hangs in the balance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Entertainment workers are hopeful that by staying united, they will be able to push the studios for better conditions. In less than two months, \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s contract with \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  will need to be renegotiated as well, and many actors see their fates as intertwined with writers\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWriters are on the frontlines, and we have to make sure we support them, because it\u8217\'20\'19s a domino effect,\u8221\'20\'1D says the actor Justin Shenkarow, a Sag-Aftra member. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they\u8217\'20\'19re able to fight and get their proper wages and working conditions, then hopefully that will trickle over to the rest of the unions as well.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shenkarow, who is also the co-founder of ThreePointZero, a small animation studio, says he\u8217\'20\'19s particularly concerned about AI and hopes the unions can win some rules targeting it: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen such a huge uptick in AI in the last several months, and we need to make sure AI doesn\u8217\'20\'19t replace the voiceover industry.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The writer\u8217\'20\'19s strike could even have impacts beyond the United States. Eben Bolter, a UK-based cinematographer who recently worked as a director of photography on \uc2 HBO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s The Last of Us, says \u8220\'20\'1Cwriters in the UK and everywhere are going to look at what\u8217\'20\'19s happening in the US\u8221\'20\'1D, and a win by the Writers Guild \u8220\'20\'1Cwould empower UK writers and everywhere to expect better\u8221\'20\'1D. While he\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cstressed\u8221\'20\'1D about where his next paycheck will come from, \u8220\'20\'1CI still support [the strike], because I think they\u8217\'20\'19re doing the right thing. And what they\u8217\'20\'19re asking for is very reasonable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230503ej53000p4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
