\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 Writers strike: how has it immediately affected the TV industry?\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,465 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2023\par 20:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the WGA strike starts to have an impact, late-night shows go dark and writers\u8217\'20\'19 rooms pause with the industry playing a game of wait and see\par \par \uc2 At 12.01am PT on Tuesday morning, the Writers Guild of America (WGA) called on its 11,500 members to go on strike. By Tuesday afternoon, many were on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/may/03/hollywood-writers-strike-late-night-shows#:~:text=The%2011%2C500%20members%20of%20the,instead%20of%20their%20writers'%20rooms."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 picket lines outside major studios}}}\uc2  in New York and Los Angeles, and the impact of a work stoppage over compensation, streaming-based residuals, minimum staffing guidelines and other concerns {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/may/02/hollywood-writers-strike-industry-effects"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cascaded through Hollywood}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 though, given the timing, the proliferation of streaming, and the ability of studios to bank episodes ahead of time, it\u8217\'20\'19s unclear how many people will notice.\par \par \uc2 At the frontline of impact were late-night variety shows, which rely on writers to craft topical jokes based on up-to-the-minute headlines. On Tuesday, \uc2 CBS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s The Late Show with Stephen Colbert, ABC\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Jimmy Kimmel\uc2  Live!, NBC\u8217\'20\'19s The Tonight Show with Jimmy Fallon and Late Night with Seth Meyers all halted production and will air reruns during a strike that could potentially last several weeks. (The last writers strike 15 years ago stretched for 100 days, from November 2007 until February 2008.) \uc2 HBO\uc2  paused production on the weekly shows Real Time with Bill Maher and Last Week Tonight with John Oliver.\par \par \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s The Daily Show, in the middle of a guest-host run by longtime contributor Dulc\u233\'00\'E9 Sloan as part of a rotating audition to replace Trevor Noah, also switched to reruns. And NBC\u8217\'20\'19s Saturday Night Live, which had three episodes left in its 48th season, announced a production shutdown on Tuesday afternoon, beginning with former cast member Pete Davidson\u8217\'20\'19s hosting debut this Saturday.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not unfamiliar terrain for many of the late-night brethren, who have developed a friendlier camaraderie compared to the more cutthroat ratings wars of the 90s and 2000s, particularly after the mutual upheaval and uncharted terrain of the pandemic. (Several filmed a farewell segment for the Late Late Show\u8217\'20\'19s James Corden, who conveniently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/apr/27/james-corden-late-late-show-ending"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ended his eight-year run}}}\uc2  last week.) In recent weeks, late-night hosts and top producers have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/05/02/business/media/late-shows-writers-strike.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on group calls}}}\uc2  developing a coordinated response in anticipation of a strike, which several hosts have experienced before.\par \par \uc2 In 2007, Colbert\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2  program, The Colbert Report, went dark for two months, then returned without writers to protect non-striking staff. To show respect for striking writers, they rebranded as The Colb ert Rep ort (hard t\u8217\'20\'19s). (The Daily Show with Jon Stewart did the same, returning as A Daily Show.) Kimmel paid his staff out of pocket \u8211\'20\'13 as did \uc2 David Letterman\uc2 , Conan O\u8217\'20\'19Brien and Jay Leno \u8211\'20\'13 and later said he returned to air because he nearly drained {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/100-days-changed-hollywood-writers-strike-10-years-1111860/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his life savings}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meyers, who was on the picket line with SNL writers in 2007, expressed support for writers during his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fuSi2NPTQ5k"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 final monologue}}}\uc2  before the strike on Monday evening. \u8220\'20\'1CNo one is entitled to a job in show business, but for those who have a job in show business, they are entitled to fair compensation. They\u8217\'20\'19re entitled to make a living,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s a very reasonable demand that\u8217\'20\'19s being set out by the Guild, and I support those demands.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Colbert likewise dedicated a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CSE9mT3ljAE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 segment}}}\uc2  of his final show to his writers, with cheeky \u8220\'20\'1Cfuture news jokes now\u8221\'20\'1D ahead of a likely strike. \u8220\'20\'1CWithout these people, this show would be called The Late Show With a Guy Rambling About Lord of the Rings and Boats for an Hour,\u8221\'20\'1D he joked. The host also expressed his support for the writers\u8217\'20\'19 demands, as a member of the Guild himself. \u8220\'20\'1CThe writers\u8217\'20\'19 demands are not unreasonable,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI support collective bargaining. This nation owes so much to unions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asked about the strike on Monday\u8217\'20\'19s Met Gala, Jimmy Fallon, who began hosting The Tonight Show in 2014, said: \u8220\'20\'1CI wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have a show if it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t for my writers, I support them all the way.\u8221\'20\'1D Although, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thedailybeast.com/tonight-show-staffer-calls-out-jimmy-fallon-for-ghosting-striking-writers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one of his own non-Guild staffers}}}\uc2 , that support did not extend to a meeting on Tuesday morning in which Tonight Show staff learned they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be paid after this week. (In contrast, Meyers was reportedly in a Zoom meeting on Tuesday trying to help non-striking staff handle a payment pause by NBC.) Deadline {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2023/05/writers-strike-jimmy-fallon-seth-meyers-nbc-to-extend-staff-pay-1235354983/#recipient_hashed=58e80303eb86ef6e1590496f3313a95182cb7e47e4431739bdb854b95b0768c7&recipient_salt=7e749829dd8fdacaf3affd4f51643a323740a6b6883a45b85c04133fdf95e512"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  that, as of Wednesday morning, both Fallon and Meyers will pay their staff for two extra weeks after NBC stops.\par \par \uc2 The daytime talkshow landscape is more complicated, as some shows, particularly those which rely more on loose interviews, don\u8217\'20\'19t hire Guild writers. \uc2 CBS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s The Talk is the only major daytime program to fully pause production. As it did in 2007, The View will carry on, though without its few WGA scribes. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re going to hear how it will be when it\u8217\'20\'19s not, you know, slicked up,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NickFondacaro/status/1653415914837012481"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  co-host Whoopi Goldberg on Tuesday morning, during the noticeably less polished broadcast; at one point, Goldberg turned directly to executive producer Brian Teta to ask what the next topic of discussion was. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re thrilled you watched us. We hope you weren\u8217\'20\'19t too freaked out about the fact that we have no writers,\u8221\'20\'1D she said at the end of the broadcast. \u8220\'20\'1CBut we did the show anyway because we want to keep everybody employed and we want to do our best and we support our writers because we know what they\u8217\'20\'19re going through.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s syndicated morning show Live With Kelly and Mark as well as ABC\u8217\'20\'19s Tamron Hall are unaffected by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2023/may/02/another-hollywood-writers-strike-is-going-ahead-heres-what-you-need-to-know"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strike}}}\uc2  and aired as usual on Tuesday morning. \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s The Kelly Clarkson Show does employ some Guild writers, but has several episodes banked ahead of time, including the one run on Tuesday. The Drew Barrymore Show, distributed by \uc2 CBS\uc2 , has already wrapped production on its season.\par \par \uc2 The impact on scripted television depends on how long the strike lasts. It has already paused several writers\u8217\'20\'19 rooms, including Yellowjackets, Abbott Elementary and Cobra Kai (scripts for the second season of \uc2 HBO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s House of the Dragon have been turned in). The strike will disrupt promotional efforts for new series such as Davidson\u8217\'20\'19s Bupkis, but it would need to go on for months to significantly alter release dates, though it could shorten seasons. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are a show that writes while we air,\u8221\'20\'1D the Abbott Elementary writer Brittani Nichols {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2023/tv/news/tv-shows-movies-affected-wga-writers-strike-2023-1235601094/#recipient_hashed=58e80303eb86ef6e1590496f3313a95182cb7e47e4431739bdb854b95b0768c7&recipient_salt=7e749829dd8fdacaf3affd4f51643a323740a6b6883a45b85c04133fdf95e512"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  Variety. \u8220\'20\'1CIf this strike goes on for a significant period of time, our show will not come out on time and that could change the amount of episodes which I\u8217\'20\'19m sure people will be very upset about.\u8221\'20\'1D In the meantime, viewers can expect to see renewed investment in unscripted series, as in strikes past, such as Peacock\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2023/05/peacock-developing-pilot-sex-in-the-dark-1235353528/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newly announced}}}\uc2  series Sex in the Dark.\par \par \uc2 On the film side, studios have braced for a possible strike for months, and have numerous movies currently in production (without scribes on set) through at least 30 June, when contracts expire with the actors\u8217\'20\'19 and directors\u8217\'20\'19 unions, as well as many more in post-production. With numerous screenplays already locked and in various stages of pre-production, there will be little tangible effect of the strike on the theatrical calendar; according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2023/04/wga-strike-cinemacon-box-office-impact-theatrical-release-calendar-the-flash-dc-1235334322/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deadline}}}\uc2 , the strike would need to last at least four months for studios to push their release dates.\par \par \uc2 The studio and streaming cushion evinces an issue for the WGA\u8217\'20\'19s efforts this go-round: will audiences notice? The 2007 strike occurred at the beginning of the network television season, in the just-before-streaming era when audience viewing patterns would be disrupted by the pause of popular shows such as 30 Rock, The Office or Heroes. This time, the streaming services, from whom writers are seeking better pay minimums and protections against the gig-ification of their work, have many, many series in the bank \u8211\'20\'13 a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/peak-tv-2022-count-599-scripted-shows-1235298139/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record 599 original English-language scripted}}}\uc2  shows aired in 2022, more than any person can watch. Late-night shows have weathered {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2023/04/28/late-night-tv-corden-ratings-decline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shrinking ratings and ad revenue for years}}}\uc2 , and has contracted significantly since last spring (James Corden, Trevor Noah, Samantha Bee and Desus & Mero have all exited the field; only The Daily Show will find a replacement.)\par \par \uc2 TV fans of all stripes will notice if the strike goes on long enough, but that may be longer than anyone is comfortable going. The impact on several shows, and thus many people\u8217\'20\'19s livelihoods, was immediate on Tuesday. But whether or not that courts the same level of public pressure and attention as in strikes past will be, as with the fraught negotiation, a game of wait and see.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230503ej53001v3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
