\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 Another Hollywood writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike is going ahead. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what you need to know\b0\par\par\uc2 Sian Cain \par \uc2 1,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2023\par 20:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Writers Guild of America have announced a strike will begin tomorrow, after talks broke down with major studios and streamers\par \par \uc2 Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s writers are going out on strike for the first time in more than 15 years.\par \par \uc2 The Writers Guild of America announced on Monday night that its 11,500 members will stop working on Tuesday afternoon, after negotiations between the union and the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  (\uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ), a group representing most major studios and streaming services, broke down.\par \par \uc2 The WGA\u8217\'20\'19s board of directors \u8220\'20\'1Cvoted unanimously to call a strike, effective 12:01 AM, Tuesday, May 2\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WGAWest/status/1653242408195457025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the WGA West tweeted on Monday night.}}}\par \par \uc2 This decision could have a huge impact on the television and possibly films you will be able to watch in the future. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 Though our Negotiating Committee began this process intent on making a fair deal, the studios\u8217\'20\'19 responses have been wholly insufficient given the existential crisis writers are facing.\u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Writers Guild of America West\uc2  (@WGAWest) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WGAWest/status/1653242580195500033?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 2, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Why are writers striking?\par \par \uc2 Residuals are like royalty payments for screenwriters; they earn these from both regular broadcasts and streaming, but they currently earn a lot more from the former \u8211\'20\'13 which, as streaming continues to proliferate and dominate, is causing a lot of disquiet in the industry.\par \par \uc2 It is complicated, but essentially if an episode of a TV show is broadcast on both regular TV and a streaming service, most companies pay less in residuals for the latter. Writers for streamed shows are paid a set amount of money regardless of whether the show is a hit. But broadcast television uses a \u8220\'20\'1Creward-for-success\u8221\'20\'1D model that means if a show is a hit, writers can earn more in residuals.\par \par \uc2 The WGA has argued that because success is not factored into streaming residuals, seasoned writers are not being recognised with the wages they deserve and wages are stagnating.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, studios and streamers have been trying to cut spending on labour. Streaming services have been merging and dropping old shows entirely from their catalogues, meaning writers can\u8217\'20\'19t earn anything from those shows. They are also ordering shorter seasons, which is affecting what writers can earn.\par \par \uc2 Another important trend has been the rise of the \u8220\'20\'1Cmini room\u8221\'20\'1D: a condensed version of a writers\u8217\'20\'19 room that takes place before a show is given the green light. These have essentially replaced the pilot episode, which studios used to use to gauge a show\u8217\'20\'19s potential. Studios like mini rooms, as it gives them a better sense of what a show might be before committing, but writers earn less for their work at that stage of production \u8211\'20\'13 in effect meaning they work longer for less on shows that may never be made.\par \par \uc2 The WGA is also campaigning for a higher salary floor and for regulation on the use of artificial intelligence in scriptwriting, asking the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  for a guarantee that it won\u8217\'20\'19t be \u8220\'20\'1Cused as source material\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really is the perfect storm,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2023/tv/news/writers-strike-final-day-contract-talks-1235599704/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one anonymous insider told Variety}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe writers\u8217\'20\'19 concerns are completely and objectively valid, which has the studios and producers a bit more rattled than they\u8217\'20\'19d like to admit. Someone has to budge, but Wall Street is unrelenting in its expectations for studios.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Every three years, the WGA negotiates a contract under which all of its members are employed with the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2 , which represents more than 350 film and television companies. It includes film studios \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , \uc2 Sony Pictures\uc2 , \uc2 Universal Pictures\uc2 , \uc2 Walt Disney Studios\uc2  and \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 ; US networks ABC, \uc2 CBS\uc2 , Fox and NBC; streaming services \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , Apple TV+ and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime; and some other cable and independent companies.\par \par \uc2 The current contract was set to end on 1 May; in mid-April, a decisive 97.85% of WGA members voted in favour of a strike if their terms were not met.\par \par \uc2 The negotiations began on 20 March; now they have ended with no deal, causing the WGA\u8217\'20\'19s first strike since the infamous writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2007/11/19/striking-out"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 late 2007 and early 2008}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here we go again! {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/wgastrong?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #wgastrong}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/9hCG61CHj0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/9hCG61CHj0}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 Wanda Sykes (@iamwandasykes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/iamwandasykes/status/1653250465222791174?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 2, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 What will the strike involve?\par \par \uc2 The WGA-issued rules for members taking part in this strike involve: no writing, revising, pitching or negotiating with the more than 350 television and film companies in the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The WGA is strict about strikes: its members \u8211\'20\'13 who make up a huge proportion of the industry \u8211\'20\'13 have been told they must picket at assigned locations unless they have a \u8220\'20\'1Cvalid medical excuse,\u8221\'20\'1D personal issue or emergency.\par \par \uc2 When writers strike, it affects everyone working in film and TV production, including electricians, caterers and background actors, who all have to find other work.\par \par \uc2 How might it play out?\par \par \uc2 The WGA\u8217\'20\'19s last strike in 2007-2008 lasted 100 days, with writers fighting over better residuals from DVD sales, union protection for animation and reality program writers, and compensation for what was then called \u8220\'20\'1Cnew media\u8221\'20\'1D: scripts written for films and shows broadcast online.\par \par \uc2 the children don\u8217\'20\'19t remember how bad TV got during the 2007 writers strike\u8212\'20\'14 ashley "big shoes" ray (@theashleyray) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/theashleyray/status/1653167329482788865?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 1, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 It had an immediate and now-infamous impact on US television and film across the board. Within a week, companies were firing most assistants and junior staffers working on shut-down programs. Late night hosts Jay Leno, Conan O\u8217\'20\'19Brien, Jon Stewart and Stephen Colbert soon returned to air but without any writers; Leno and O\u8217\'20\'19Brien paid their non-writing out of their own pockets.\par \par \uc2 Popular shows in production at the time, including the Simpsons, the Office, 30 Rock, Mad Men, Gossip Girl, Breaking Bad and Lost, were affected by the strike; many ended up with a much shorter than normal season, which had a huge impact on storytelling. Some shows arguably benefited from the break, like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2017/jul/10/when-good-tv-goes-bad-friday-night-lights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Friday Night Lights}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other shows postponed production entirely; it has been estimated that the California economy lost an estimated $2.1bn over the four-month strike.\par \par \uc2 How will this affect what I watch?\par \par \uc2 Any strike will have an immediate impact on the US\u8217\'20\'19s late-night talkshows, which are mostly staffed by WGA members who have to write up-to-the minute commentary and jokes; expect to see a few of these shows become very free-wheeling or start airing re-runs.\par \par \uc2 Next to be disrupted will probably be soap operas, which are traditionally written not long before they are filmed.\par \par \uc2 The latest seasons of big comedies and dramas currently airing will remain uninterrupted as their episodes are all likely written and filmed already. But if this strike continues for more than a couple weeks, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nam02.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.hollywoodreporter.com%2Fbusiness%2Fbusiness-news%2Fwriters-guild-impact-on-streamers-legacy-networks-as-strike-looms-1235399135%2F&data=05%7C01%7Ckatherine.kilkenny%40thr.com%7C8a613e1fec0a494a7a9e08db4ab9d12f%7Ce950f25546e44144a778a6ff4f557492%7C0%7C0%7C638185934183685981%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJWIjoiMC4wLjAwMDAiLCJQIjoiV2luMzIiLCJBTiI6Ik1haWwiLCJXVCI6Mn0%3D%7C3000%7C%7C%7C&sdata=KTyrtui1D%2FG53NmqftNKM81ACdUw5W%2BV9LpzXFJoXig%3D&reserved=0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WGA has warned}}}\uc2  that it will impact network television season, which encompasses 45% of episodes made by companies like \uc2 Disney\uc2 , Paramount and Comcast NBCUniversal, as writers usually begin to work around now on the big series that usually premiere in the US fall.\par \par \uc2 The flow of films to cinemas will not stop because studios have a pipeline of films already written and shot years ago. It would take a very long strike to interrupt film schedules \u8211\'20\'13 but given both sides show no sign of backing down, it could happen.\par \par \uc2 What happens if actors and directors strike as well?\par \par \uc2 The actors\u8217\'20\'19 guild \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  and the Directors Guilde of America contracts expire on 30 June \u8211\'20\'13 if they also go on strike, production on scripted shows would end completely.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230502ej52000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
