\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US investigation uncovers two 10-year-olds working at Kentucky McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 505 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2023\par 15:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Investigation also found three franchises in four states employed a total of 305 children working more than legally permitted hours\par \par \uc2 A US department of labor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20230502-0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigation }}}\uc2  uncovered child labor violations at three \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s locations in Kentucky, which included finding two 10-year-olds working unpaid, sometimes until 2am.\par \par \uc2 The three \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s franchisees cited in the investigation own a total of 62 locations across Kentucky, Indiana, Maryland and Ohio, and were found to employ 305 children working more than legally permitted hours and performing job tasks prohibited by law for their age.\par \par \uc2 Civil penalties totaling $212,754 were issued against the franchisees for the violations.\par \par \uc2 The franchisees include Bauer Food, based in Louisville; Archways Richwood, based in Walton, Kentucky; and Bell Restaurant Group, also based in Louisville. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://corporate.mcdonalds.com/corpmcd/franchising-overview.html#:~:text=Welcome%20to%20McDonald's%20Franchising&text=Approximately%2093%25%20Of%20McDonald's%20restaurants,by%20independent%20local%20business%20owners."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 About 93% }}}\uc2  of \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s locations worldwide are operated by franchisees.\par \par \uc2 The investigation found the franchisees employed 14- and 15-year-olds who were working outside of and over the number of hours minors are permitted to work, and exceeding the daily and weekly limits \u8211\'20\'13 including cases where the workers were on the job during school hours.\par \par \uc2 The news comes as states across the US have moved to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/may/01/us-surge-efforts-reduce-child-labor-regulations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 relax child labor laws.}}}\par \par \uc2 Karen Garnett-Civils of the labor department said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are seeing an increase in federal child labor violations, including allowing minors to operate equipment or handle types of work that endangers them or employs them for more hours or later in the day than federal law allows.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn employer who hires young workers must know the rules. An employer, parent or young worker with questions can contact us for help understanding their obligations and rights under the law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The child labor violations are the latest among several recent cases involving \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s franchises.\par \par \uc2 In February, the labor department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20230222-2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fined}}}\uc2  a franchise operator of seven \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s restaurants in the Pennsylvania towns of Erie and Warren for illegally allowing 14- and 15-year-olds to work at times and over hours not permitted by federal child labor laws, and cases of assigning nine workers under the age of 16 to operate deep fryers.\par \par \uc2 The violations affected 154 minor employees at the restaurants and led to a fine of $92,107.\par \par \uc2 Another Pennsylvania-based \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s franchisee, the owner of 13 locations in the Pittsburgh area, was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20221205-0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fined}}}\uc2  in December for child labor violations covering 101 minor workers. The violations include allowing the children to work later than 9pm, more than eight hours on school days, more than 18 hours during school weeks, and more than three hours a day and after 7pm on school days. A civil penalty of $57,332 was assessed for those violations.\par \par \uc2 In June last year, a \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s franchisee in Tennessee was fined $3,258 for illegally assigning a 15-year-old worker to use a deep fryer, considered a hazardous job task not permitted for minors. The child suffered a burn while doing so.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230503ej53001pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
