\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Dumb and dangerous\u8217\'20\'19: US sees surge in efforts to weaken child labor regulations\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2023\par 13:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ten states have rolled back child labor laws recently, with a federal bill to allow teens to work in one of the most dangerous industries\par \par \uc2 This March a bill was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.risch.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/2023/3/risch-king-golden-thompson-lead-bipartisan-bicameral-future-logging-careers-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reintroduced}}}\uc2  in the US House and Senate that would allow 16- and 17-year-olds to work in certain mechanized operations in the logging industry under parental supervision. Timber industry groups have strongly backed the legislation. For Wendy Bostwick, the news was a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 Bostwick\u8217\'20\'19s son Cole, who had just turned 18, died in a logging accident in 2014 on a job site in Washington where his father, Tim Bostwick, was also working.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CObviously that was a tragic situation, but somebody who does want to get into logging can and should be supervised by their parents,\u8221\'20\'1D Senator \uc2 Jim Risch\uc2  of Idaho, chief sponsor of the Future Logging Careers Act, said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.chronline.com/stories/after-sons-logging-death-winlock-couple-opposes-child-labor-law-changes,32716"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in an interview}}}\uc2  with the local Washington paper the Chronicle. \u8220\'20\'1CIf a child is going to go into logging, what better way than to start with your family and having your family teach it to you?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBullshit!\u8221\'20\'1D Bostwick told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CIt should open our eyes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the most dangerous jobs in the world and people want to put their children out there? Kids that age are not emotionally ready for something like this. They don\u8217\'20\'19t have the mental faculties to drink alcohol, but they can go out there and make life-and-death decisions? I don\u8217\'20\'19t think so. It\u8217\'20\'19s dumb and dangerous,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 One of the most dangerous jobs in the world and people want to put their children out there?\par \par \uc2 Wendy Bostwick\par \par \uc2 The logging bill is just one of several efforts across the US to roll back child labor protections at a time when many employers are still struggling to fill jobs.\par \par \uc2 Backed by big business and lobby groups, politicians nationwide are pushing attempts to expand work hours for minors, expand the industries minors are permitted to work in, reduce enforcement and legislate sub-minimum wages for minors. These rollbacks at the federal and state levels are being proposed even as child labor violations have soared in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The logging industry is one of the most dangerous fields in which to work in the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://advisorsmith.com/data/most-dangerous-jobs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 consistently leading}}}\uc2  with the highest workplace fatality rate in the nation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf this thing goes through, there are going to be a lot more families out there like ours. It\u8217\'20\'19s been almost nine years and we still live it every day. We have to be careful of what music we listen to or what movies we watch, because it reminds us of Cole and the accident. We will never be right again. My whole family still has PTSD issues. My husband and I have been in and out of therapy since it happened,\u8221\'20\'1D added Bostwick.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLosing an adult in the family is nothing compared to losing a child. Why, why, why would anyone want a child out there? There are plenty of adults who need those jobs too.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Against this legislative drive, surveys show an already alarming surge in child labor violations. The number of children employed in violation of child labor laws has increased by 37% in the last year and by 283% since 2015, from 1,012 reported cases of children working in violation of child labor laws to 3,876 in 2022, according to a March 2023 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/child-labor-laws-under-attack/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  (EPI).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think what we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing in terms of the state push right now should be viewed as the latest multi-industry push to really wipe out regulation of child labor, not in one fell swoop, but that\u8217\'20\'19s always sort of the end goal of the various pushes coming all at the same time,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jennifer Sherer, senior state policy coordinator for the Economic Analysis and Research Network (Earn) Worker Power Project, and author of the EPI report.\par \par \uc2 The 2023 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/reports/death-job-toll-neglect-2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Death on the Job report }}}\uc2  by the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  labor federation noted that 350 workers in the US under the age of 25 died on the job in 2021, including 24 workers younger than 18 years old.\par \par \uc2 Ten states have proposed or passed legislation to roll back child labor protections in the past two years, including eight states so far in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Arkansas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://arkansasadvocate.com/2023/03/08/arkansas-governor-signs-wide-ranging-education-bill-into-law/%20Text%20-%20S.671%20-%20118th%20Congress%20(2023-2024):%20Future%20Logging%20Careers%20Act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed}}}\uc2  legislation in 2023 to eliminate age verification and parental or guardian permission requirements.\par \par \uc2 In Iowa, a bill was passed in 2022 to lower the minimum age of childcare staff and reduce staff-to-child ratios. The state\u8217\'20\'19s Republican majority senate also recently passed a broader bill that enables minors to work in hazardous occupations and extends permitted work hours.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is part of a nationwide redefinition of childhood in the United States. It\u8217\'20\'19s pushing Iowa kids decades into the past and it\u8217\'20\'19s really concerning, because I think it\u8217\'20\'19s going to take advantage of some of the most economically vulnerable kids in Iowa and around the nation,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Democratic state senator Liz Bennett.\par \par \uc2 New Jersey and New Hampshire also passed legislation in 2022 to roll back child labor protections. Missouri recently included an amendment to a house bill ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legiscan.com/MO/bill/HB188/2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HB188)}}}\uc2  that would expand work hours for minors. Legislation to relax child labor laws has also been introduced in Minnesota, Missouri, Nebraska, Ohio and South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the legislation cited in the report, Nina Mast, an economic analyst at Earn and co-author of the EPI report, noted bills have been introduced in Maine and Virginia to create a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legiscan.com/ME/bill/LD1332/2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sub-minimum wage}}}\uc2  for workers aged 14 to 17 years old. A bill in Georgia was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.legis.ga.gov/api/legislation/document/20232024/215318"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proposed}}}\uc2  but failed this year to eliminate work youth permits.\par \par \uc2 This is a nationwide redefinition of childhood in the United States. It\u8217\'20\'19s pushing kids decades into the past\par \par \uc2 Democratic Iowa state senator Liz Bennett\par \par \uc2 Emails {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2023/04/23/child-labor-lobbying-fga/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 obtained}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  revealed much of the legislation aimed at rolling back child labor protection was drafted by the Foundation for Government Accountability, a Florida-based conservative thinktank that touts parental freedoms in advocating for the bills.\par \par \uc2 Business lobby groups are also pushing for change. \u8220\'20\'1CCould extra teen working hours help restaurants with the labor shortage?\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2  asked last year in a blogpost calling for Congress to pass the Republican-backed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dustyjohnson.house.gov/sites/evo-subsites/dustyjohnson.house.gov/files/evo-media-document/TEENS%20Act.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Teenagers Earning Everyday Necessary Skills (Teens) Act}}}\uc2 , which would lift restrictions on when and how much time teenagers can work.\par \par \uc2 The efforts, backed by business industry groups and conservative thinktanks, often contradict federal child labor law standards. In its report, the EPI argues the goal is to challenge federal standards at the local level in order to weaken or eliminate federal wage and hour standards.\par \par \uc2 The drive to increase child labor is being accompanied by a push to undermine oversight, said Sherer. She noted that the legislation in Iowa to roll back child labor protections also includes language to relax enforcement of any child labor violations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe most immediate problem and consequences, I think, created by the state rollbacks is the message that it\u8217\'20\'19s sending, that on top of the already weak enforcement, it\u8217\'20\'19s the state officials saying to the business community and to workers that they\u8217\'20\'19re not going to be enforcing wage and hour or child labor laws,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sherer.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have proposed several federal bills to counter the trend and the Department of Labor and Department of Health and Human Services have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hhs.gov/about/news/2023/03/24/us-departments-labor-health-human-services-announce-additional-steps-tackle-child-labor-violations-strengthening-coordination.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proposed}}}\uc2  policies to strengthen enforcement of child labor violations and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/bipartisan-bill-aims-increase-penalties-us-child-labor-violations-2023-03-29/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increase}}}\uc2  monetary penalties for violations. Opponents of the bills argue that enforcement is already inadequate and enforcement agencies haven\u8217\'20\'19t been adequately staffed or funded to respond to the rise in child labor violations.\par \par \uc2 If these bills are not stopped, said Sherer, the long-term consequences for many children will be severe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s really extensive research in the public health and education world on why we have developed, over time, restrictions on work hours,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTeens and high school students that get close to or exceed that 20-hour-a-week mark for paid labor during the school year end up in a higher-risk category, are less likely to complete high school, less likely to be in a position to pursue post-secondary education or training, and that puts young people on a path to fewer job opportunities and lower earnings for a lifetime. It\u8217\'20\'19s actually very consequential,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sherer. \u8220\'20\'1CEven those pushes to extend work hours are a really slippery slope, and dangerous in terms of long-term economic outcomes for children and their families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230501ej5100107\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
