\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 The workers who say their migrant status has been \u8216\'20\'18weaponized\u8217\'20\'19 against them\b0\par\par\uc2 Yusra Farzan, The Fuller Project \par \uc2 1,847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2023\par 17:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Undocumented workers play a crucial role in California\u8217\'20\'19s labor force, but are forced to take exploitative jobs\par \par \uc2 When Irma Mejia moved into a garage with her three children, she was hoping it would be temporary. Then, on the very first payday after pandemic lockdowns went into effect, she says her new manager told her she was only going to get paid half of what she was owed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[I thought] if you couldn\u8217\'20\'19t pay me, you should have asked me,\u8221\'20\'1D Mejia recalls. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause I get to decide if I\u8217\'20\'19m working here or not, right? If you\u8217\'20\'19re only gonna pay me these hours, then I\u8217\'20\'19m only working those hours.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mejia, 45, was stunned. She lived in a garage, but still had a $750 rent check due. She felt helpless. Undocumented, she knew she would have no legal protections in a labor dispute, and believes that played a role in the decision of her manager at a laundromat in Boyle Heights, a neighborhood east of Los Angeles, to not pay her full wages.\par \par \uc2 Mejia says she quit after four months of trying to get paid what she was due. She believes she is still owed almost half of her total wages, but isn\u8217\'20\'19t sure about the exact amount as she says she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t keeping track during the chaos of the early days of the pandemic. She says she has lost hope of recovering that money.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy immigration status has been weaponized,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 More than 1 million workers in California are undocumented, making up {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://clc.ucmerced.edu/sites/clc.ucmerced.edu/files/page/documents/essential_fairness.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 6% of California\u8217\'20\'19s workforce}}}\uc2  and contributing $3.7bn towards the state\u8217\'20\'19s tax revenue, according to a University of California, Merced, report released in March. They also play a crucial part in the labor force, filling one in 16 jobs , especially in manufacturing, food service, construction and agriculture. Yet these workers are not eligible for unemployment benefits, making it more likely that they\u8217\'20\'19ll end up working in exploitative or high-risk environments.\par \par \uc2 The UC Merced report found that undocumented migrants in California were eligible for $1,700 in state and federal assistance in the first year of the pandemic, compared with $35,000 for residents who were also US citizens. Immigrants made up {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calmatters.org/california-divide/2022/04/california-undocumented-immigrants/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 58% of pandemic-related deaths}}}\uc2  in California\u8217\'20\'19s deadliest industries \u8211\'20\'13 agriculture, landscaping and food processing \u8211\'20\'13 between March and December 2020.\par \par \uc2 The data is not broken down for undocumented migrants, but activists who work with them say they have been especially vulnerable \u8211\'20\'13 particularly women, whose top 10 occupations in the US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americanimmigrationcouncil.org/research/impact-immigrant-women-americas-labor-force"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all involve in-person work}}}\uc2 , such as cleaning, childcare and hospitality work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of the inequities that we see generally out in the world are ramped up for that population,\u8221\'20\'1D says Sasha Harnden, an attorney at Inner City Law Center in Los Angeles. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are dealing with mountains of rent debt, having to go back to work, despite potentially having to take care of family members, and have to deal with the loss of a lot of lives, including primary breadwinners [during Covid].\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Providing undocumented people with a pathway to citizenship would be one solution to this problem. But ongoing efforts in the Senate to achieve this before the Republicans took the House stumbled in mid-December over disputes about also increasing funding for Customs and Border Protection at the same time.\par \par \uc2 In the House, 28 Democrats led to move legislation forward on this issue, claiming that providing access to citizenship to undocumented migrants would raise US GDP by $1.7tn over 10 years. The House has passed a bill providing a path to citizenship for undocumented people who came to the US as children, known as Dreamers, but supporters have little hope that the Senate will consider it.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Honest labor\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Before the laundromat, Mejia worked in different convention centers in Long Beach, Anaheim, and Los Angeles as janitorial staff and also looked after elderly people in a private home. \u8220\'20\'1CHonest labor,\u8221\'20\'1D she calls it.\par \par \uc2 When the pandemic hit, she worried about exposing her children and mother by going into work, but her finances left her with no option. Unable to afford childcare, Mejia worked the night shift from 10pm to 9am at a 24-hour laundromat in East Los Angeles. Her oldest surviving children are in college now, and she wants the younger ones to follow suit. To her, this means not just working to provide for them, but also being present with them during the day. Having to call a garage their home has made this dream feel more distant.\par \par \uc2 When asked about Mejia\u8217\'20\'19s overdue wages, her former employer, Fresco Laundromat, in a written statement said: \u8220\'20\'1CAfter an internal investigation into the allegations that an employee of our company was dismissed without full compensation for work rendered, we have determined that this event happened under the direct supervision and management of an outside contractor hired to build a night processing team in early 2020.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They also went on to state that the outside contractor was dismissed from the role in June 2020 for multiple reasons, such as poor performance and lack of communication. The owner, Adam Xavier, added: \u8220\'20\'1CThe employee never spoke directly to me regarding missing pay. In fact, the reason for her dismissal was not brought to me, as those decisions were handled by the contractor due to a language barrier.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Xavier says they will reimburse Mejia if she brings her payslips, but Mejia says the contractor did not give her any to begin with. The Fuller Project and the Guardian have requested the name and contact information of the contractor from Fresco Laundromat several times but have not received a response. Mejia, who did not realize the person who hired her was an outside contractor and not an employee of the laundromat, referred to the person as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe manager\u8221\'20\'1D and told The Fuller Project that she cannot recall his name.\par \par \uc2 Fighting over funding\par \par \uc2 Twice as many noncitizen workers (38%) live below a \u8220\'20\'1Cliving wage\u8221\'20\'1D as citizen workers (18%), according to the UC Merced study. This has generational implications.\par \par \uc2 Alejandra \u8220\'20\'1CHoney\u8221\'20\'1D Arias, a single mother to three daughters, says she was fired from a clothing store in the summer of 2020. She believes her immigration status made it easier for her former employer to fire her. Being undocumented also meant that she was not eligible for unemployment funds.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI lost my job and I got evicted from where I was living,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CThey literally told me to get out. They didn\u8217\'20\'19t care. The landlord did not care. And I got evicted with my three children.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For more than a year, Arias shuttled between temporary housing and shelters, trying to make ends meet with the $1,600 she gets from CalFresh food subsidies, as well as $1,600 in supplemental security income cash assistance that she gets for her youngest daughter, who is autistic.\par \par \uc2 The disparities highlighted by the pandemic prompted some lawmakers to try to take action. California assemblymember Eduardo Garcia, a Democrat from Coachella, introduced legislation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202120220AB2847"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AB 2847}}}\uc2  in April 2022 to create the Excluded Workers Pilot Program. It aimed to make unemployed, undocumented migrants eligible for $300 a week for 20 weeks \u8211\'20\'13 a total of $6,000 in assistance.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAccess to unemployment benefits can make all the difference in a family affording rent and food to feed their children,\u8221\'20\'1D Garcia said. \u8220\'20\'1COur immigrant communities are Californians who contribute millions to our unemployment program and economy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the California governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , vetoed the bill, arguing it needed a dedicated funding source.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith our state facing lower-than-expected revenues over the first few months of this fiscal year, it is also important to remain disciplined when it comes to spending,\u8221\'20\'1D Newsom said when announcing his veto.\par \par \uc2 Advocates of the bill say the program would have cost $597m, but Newsom countered that it would cost \u8220\'20\'1Cwell over $20bn in one-time spending commitments and more than $10bn in ongoing commitments.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Struggling to remain resilient\par \par \uc2 State support can make a bigger difference for undocumented people than most, as their precarious immigration status means problems can easily compound. Arias says she was injured in a hit-and-run accident while crossing the street, and ended up in the hospital, seeking emergency treatment. Friends and family came together to raise almost $20,000 to pay the hospital bills for the uninsured Arias, but she says she could not afford to pay for follow-up treatment or physical therapy.\par \par \uc2 For now, Arias focuses on the day-to-day, helping her two older children with online schooling. Without a car and reliant on temporary housing, she felt her children\u8217\'20\'19s education was being disrupted by having to change schools frequently, and enrolled them in a kindergarten through 12th grade program that offers a virtual public school.\par \par \uc2 Her youngest daughter attends a special education school nearby in Boyle Heights. Like Mejia, Arias values education for her children above all else. She says she dropped out of high school at age 15 to help provide for her family and is determined to see that her children don\u8217\'20\'19t share the same fate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI needed to help out my mom because my mom was in a really toxic relationship with my father. He was really abusive,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to be home so I would just go outside to try to bring some money in so there wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be that much tension at home.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She recently started a job working as a canvasser for a community organization, and is hopeful this will help her attain the stable and secure housing she craves, a place \u8220\'20\'1Cwhere I could feel relaxed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt shelters, you see a lot of things that you shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t,\u8221\'20\'1D Arias says. \u8220\'20\'1CI do want to protect my children.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mejia echoes this sentiment. \u8220\'20\'1CA home,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, \u8220\'20\'1Cis where you play, where you eat and where you have conversations.\u8221\'20\'1D Without that, she says she feels no control over life.\par \par \uc2 She reaches up to touch her red, heart-shaped pendant. It contains the ashes of her son, who died in 2016, a month shy of his 20th birthday. He was walking down a street with friends when he was caught in an argument between rival gangs and was gunned down.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am going to keep fighting,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 The Golden State is expected to overtake Germany as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.ca.gov/2022/10/24/icymi-california-poised-to-become-worlds-4th-biggest-economy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the world\u8217\'20\'19s fourth-largest economy}}}\uc2  this year, but not all of this wealth is being shared equally. In this series, the Guardian and the Fuller Project look at the lives of women, especially women of color, who help drive the economy of the US\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2021/08/2020-united-states-population-more-racially-ethnically-diverse-than-2010.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 second-most racially diverse state}}}\uc2  but don\u8217\'20\'19t get their fair share of the pie.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  is publishing this article in partnership with the Fuller Project, a nonprofit newsroom dedicated to the coverage of women\u8217\'20\'19s issues around the world. Sign up for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fullerproject.org/sign-up-to-our-newsletter/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Fuller Project\u8217\'20\'19s newsletter}}}\uc2 , and follow the organization on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FullerProject?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Eauthor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Twitter}}}\uc2  or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/company/fuller-project/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 LinkedIn}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230423ej4n000um\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pic