\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US adds 236,000 jobs in March as labor market weakens\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 869 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2023\par 15:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 March saw fewer jobs added to economy compared with February as effects of Fed\u8217\'20\'19s interest rate increases are felt\par \par \uc2 The US added 236,000 jobs in March, a sign of gradual weakening in the labor market as the effects of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s interest rate increases start to be seen in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Friday\u8217\'20\'19s closely watched jobs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/empsit.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  provides data that will heavily influence the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s decision to either halt or continue interest rate hikes at its next board meeting in early May. The non-farm payrolls data published by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 (BLS) suggested a slowdown in the jobs market in the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy last month, after an upwardly revised 326,000 jobs were added in February and 504,000 in January.\par \par \uc2 The March number was also 3,000 lower than the 239,000 forecast by economists.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate dropped 0.1% to 3.5%.\par \par \uc2 Fewer jobs were added to the leisure and hospitality and healthcare sectors than in previous months, though the industries are still trending up in job availability. Government and professional and business services continued to grow at a similar paces to previous months.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe 236,000 gain in non-farm payrolls in March adds to the evidence that the economy\u8217\'20\'19s strong start to the year was partly weather related, with momentum fading again,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Andrew Hunter\uc2 , deputy chief US economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the sharp fall in job openings and upward trend in jobless claims also pointing to cooling labor demand, and the drag from the recent banking turmoil still to feed through, we expect jobs growth to slow more sharply soon.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the government\u8217\'20\'19s job survey other reports gave hints that the labor market, while still growing, is cooling from highs seen over the last two years. \uc2 BLS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Job Openings and Labor Turnover {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/jlt/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Survey}}}\uc2  last week showed that employers are starting to slow the pace of hiring. There were less than 10m active job openings by the end of February for the first time in nearly two years.\par \par \uc2 Payroll company ADP released its national employment {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://adpemploymentreport.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  on Wednesday, showing that private employers added 145,000 jobs in March \u8211\'20\'13 down from the 261,000 jobs in the private sector added in February.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.challengergray.com/blog/job-cuts-rise-15-in-march-2023-up-319-from-same-month-last-year-highest-q1-since-2020/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released}}}\uc2  Thursday from outplacement consultant firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2  showed an uptick in job cuts in March. Last month, US-based employers cut 89,703 jobs \u8211\'20\'13 a 15% increase compared with the 77,770 cuts that were seen in February. The losses were predominantly seen in the tech sector, which saw mass layoffs after companies over-hired during the pandemic, followed by the job cuts in the financial sector.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of Friday\u8217\'20\'19s report, stock markets \u8211\'20\'13 which are closed on Friday in observance of Good Friday \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/markets/us/futures-muted-focus-shifts-jobs-data-amid-recession-fears-2023-04-06/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wavered}}}\uc2  on Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s as investors showed ambivalence over the incoming report and what it could mean for interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The Fed will have to weigh the latest job report against incoming inflation news as it decides whether or not to hike interest rates again. Inflation reached a 40-year high last year and while it is declining, it remains stubbornly high.\par \par \uc2 The Fed chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , has consistently said getting prices under control is the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s top priority. Despite the banking crisis caused by the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , the Fed went ahead and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/mar/22/interest-rate-rise-us-federal-reserve-banking-turmoil"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased}}}\uc2  interest rates by a quarter point last month, bringing rates to 4.75% to 5% \u8211\'20\'13 its ninth consecutive raise and the highest since 2007.\par \par \uc2 When discussing the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s decision to increase rates, Powell cited economic data from January, including the jobs report, that showed the economy was not slowing down as much as the Fed would like.\par \par \uc2 Powell acknowledged the banking crisis\u8217\'20\'19s effect on the broader economy, but remained adamant on the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s goal to bring inflation down to 2%. Inflation in February {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/mar/14/us-inflation-slows-banking-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slowed}}}\uc2  to 6%, but Powell said the rate is still far from their goal. March\u8217\'20\'19s inflation figures will be released on 12 April.\par \par \uc2 Some economists have criticised Powell for taking such a stringent approach to lowering inflation, saying that being too hawkish could push the economy toward a recession.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe effects of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s unrelenting rate hikes are apparent: job growth is slowing and unemployment insurance claims are up, now breaking 200,000 a week,\u8221\'20\'1D said Chris Becker, senior economist at Groundwork Collaborative. \u8220\'20\'1CBut the labor market is the wrong target for the Fed because workers and their wages have not driven inflation \u8211\'20\'13 supply disruptions and corporate profits have. Additional rate hikes will only edge us closer to a painful, avoidable recession.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Still, Powell has continued to reiterate that lowering inflation is the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s priority, and that recent coolings have not been enough.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInflation remains too high, and the labor market continues to be very tight,\u8221\'20\'1D Powell said at a press conference 22 March, after the latest quarter-point increase. \u8220\'20\'1CMy colleagues and I understand the hardship that high inflation is causing, and we remain strongly committed to bringing inflation back down to our 2% goal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230407ej47001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
