\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18An inherent indignity\u8217\'20\'19: the fight to get workers with disability a living wage\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,535 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 March 2023\par 14:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Advocates are highlighting a system of state tax credits across the US that allow employers to pay employees as low as $3 an hour\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are people who really can\u8217\'20\'19t do anything,\u8221\'20\'1D Shawn Tarwater, a state representative in Kansas told a state house committee last month as he argued to protect a scheme that allows companies to pay people with disabilities as little as $3 an hour. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you do away with programs like that, they will rot at home. There is no place for them to go.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The comments have sparked a storm of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kansasreflector.com/2023/02/16/kansas-organizations-denounce-rep-tarwaters-claim-people-with-disabilities-cant-do-anything/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protests}}}\uc2  from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.drckansas.org/about/news/pressrelease_february162023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disability advocates}}}\uc2  and highlighted a system of state tax credits across the US that allows employers to pay people with disabilities significantly less than the federal minimum wage. In some cases, disability advocates say, people are being paid as little as three cents an hour.\par \par \uc2 The row over Tarwater\u8217\'20\'19s comments comes as disability rights groups have been pushing for the end of subminimum wage waivers for employers who employ people with disabilities in several states and at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 Since the federal minimum wage was first enacted in 1938, exemptions have existed for people with disabilities. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/39-14c-subminimum-wage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 14(c) waiver}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  allows employers to pay people with disabilities less than the federal or state minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 A US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usccr.gov/files/2020/2020-09-17-Subminimum-Wages-Report.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 commission on civil rights report}}}\uc2  found the average wage of a person with a disability working in a job covered by the lower-than-minimum-wage waiver made $3.34 an hour in 2017 and 2018. On average they worked 16 hours a week, meaning they made just $53.44 each week, or $213.76 a month.\par \par \uc2 Joe Cheray, the chair of the Kansas Democratic Disability Caucus and member of the Topeka ADA advisory council, said she has been trying to meet with Tarwater and address his comments and the lack of any disability policies included in the Kansas Republican party platform.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t feel like it\u8217\'20\'19s fair for businesses to get a tax credit for hiring people with disabilities and not pay them an actual wage,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like that\u8217\'20\'19s double dipping off the backs of people with disabilities. I have a son who is disabled, he has cerebral palsy and I take care of him full time. I would not want him to go out to a job and get paid $2 an hour. With subminimum wage they can pay whatever they feel like.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cheray pointed to other problems facing people with disabilities, such as lack of compliance with the Americans With Disabilities Act, lack of accessibility to spaces and other worrying legislative measures, such as in Oklahoma, where the Republican-dominated state government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/politics/government/2023/03/16/jim-olsen-oklahoma-spare-the-rod-corporal-punishment-disabled-students/70016228007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted against}}}\uc2  a bill that would have banned physical discipline against students with disabilities.\par \par \uc2 A Republican opponent to the bill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox23.com/news/local/bill-to-ban-corporal-punishment-for-special-needs-students-fails-in-ok-house/article_25f36aba-c2a1-11ed-9065-b7980076d3cb.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recited}}}\uc2  a Bible passage on the state house floor in support of the use of corporal punishment on children.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re always in the dark ages when it comes to just about everything,\u8221\'20\'1D added Cheray.\par \par \uc2 Disability advocates have made progress in recent years. Thirteen {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apse.org/state-legislation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states}}}\uc2  have passed laws banning the subminimum wage exemptions for people with disabilities. Virginia recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lis.virginia.gov/cgi-bin/legp604.exe?ses=231&typ=bil&val=hb1924"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed a law}}}\uc2  banning the subminimum wage and a grant has been provided by the federal government to support the transition. The bill is currently awaiting the governor\u8217\'20\'19s signature. Laws to ban the subminimum wage in Minnesota, Connecticut, New York, West Virginia and Kentucky were also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.governing.com/work/minnesota-bill-would-abolish-disabled-workers-subminimum-wage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 introduced}}}\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 The disability community wants this provision gone. We want to be paid fair wages. We want to be valued for our work\par \par \uc2 Matthew Shapiro\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs a person who has a disability, I would never accept a job where I was making $3 an hour,\u8221\'20\'1D said Matthew Shapiro, who lobbies on behalf of the Virginia chapter of the Association of People Supporting Employment First (Apse) which strongly supported the Virginia bill to end the subminimum wage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn many instances, the individuals who are being supported under 14(c) certificates may or may not know they can go across the street and make $10 an hour or whatever,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI just don\u8217\'20\'19t find that to be appropriate. The majority of the disability community wants this provision gone. We want to be paid fair wages. We want to be valued for our work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The non-profit Melwood, a former 14(c) certificate holder that voluntarily phased out using the certificate, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://melwood.org/newsroom/hb1924vatoeliminate14c/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 led}}}\uc2  efforts to pass the bill in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are places in this country under this law where there are people being paid three cents an hour,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jewelyn Cosgrove, vice-president of government and public relations at Melwood. \u8220\'20\'1CUnderstanding that this law enabled such a stark difference from the actual minimum wage has helped people to understand more of the reality of what it\u8217\'20\'19s like to be paid under 14(c).\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Larysa Kautz, CEO and president of Melwood, added the minimum wage is not based on productivity; it\u8217\'20\'19s based on what a person\u8217\'20\'19s hour is determined to be worth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople with disabilities are people, they\u8217\'20\'19re whole people, they\u8217\'20\'19re complete people and they should not be paid by an employer less than the minimum wage \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s the bottom line,\u8221\'20\'1D Kautz said.\par \par \uc2 In several states, from Kansas to Missouri, efforts to get rid of the subminimum wage for people with disabilities have faced staunch resistance. In 2021, Missouri {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.route-fifty.com/management/2022/11/missouri-allows-some-disabled-workers-earn-less-1-hour-state-says-its-fine-if-never-changes/379779/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed a law}}}\uc2  to issue their own waivers to employers to pay subminimum wages to people with disabilities if the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  stops issuing them, the first state to do so.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re letting this population make less than minimum wage and live with the inherent insult of that \u8230\'20\'26 of being treated as a fractional worker\par \par \uc2 R Larkin Taylor-Parker\par \par \uc2 Sheltered workshops operating with subminimum wage waivers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.route-fifty.com/management/2022/11/missouri-allows-some-disabled-workers-earn-less-1-hour-state-says-its-fine-if-never-changes/379779/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 range}}}\uc2  from light manufacturing lines to recycling facilities. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/workers-with-disabilities/section-14c/certificate-holders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 About 1,000 employers}}}\uc2  around the US currently hold these waivers, with estimates of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tcf.org/content/commentary/the-movement-to-invest-in-disabled-workers-is-gaining-ground/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 40,000 to 100,000 workers}}}\uc2  being paid subminimum wages by these employers.\par \par \uc2 A report published by the \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  in January 2023 found the number of employers with these waivers have declined from 2010 to 2019, but the majority of workers at these employers make less than $3.50 an hour. Between 2012 to 2021, the labor department\u8217\'20\'19s wage and hour division {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.casey.senate.gov/news/releases/casey-daines-scott-mcmorris-rodgers-announce-bipartisan-bill-to-phase-out-subminimum-wage-for-people-with-disabilities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 identified}}}\uc2  over $15m in unpaid back wages to more than 73,500 employees working under these waivers.\par \par \uc2 For R Larkin Taylor-Parker, legal director of the Autistic Self-Advocacy Network, the efforts to get rid of the subminimum wage for people with disabilities are an anti-poverty effort and about dignity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is an inherent indignity to being placed in an environment that is by definition segregated, and that pays less than any other worker in the economy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Taylor-Parker. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re letting this population make less than minimum wage and live with the inherent insult of that, and also live with the inherent insult of being treated as a fractional worker, in a job that may not be that suitable for the individual instead of being asked to do what most of us do when we look for a job and find something that really plays to our strengths and takes advantage of what we do well, instead of being so focused on our weaknesses that we\u8217\'20\'19re not really that productive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Taylor-Parker said that the opposition to transitioning these sheltered workshops into competitive employment that pays at least minimum wage is predicated on fear, but that is why transition grants and periods are included in legislative efforts to end the subminimum wage for people with disabilities, to build better, more dignified systems and sustainable models that have been built and are thriving elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.casey.senate.gov/news/releases/casey-daines-scott-mcmorris-rodgers-announce-bipartisan-bill-to-phase-out-subminimum-wage-for-people-with-disabilities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bipartisan federal bill}}}\uc2  to phase out the subminimum wage for people with disabilities, the Transformation to Competitive Integrated Employment Act, was reintroduced to Congress in February 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you look at any of the protected classes from our civil rights laws, there\u8217\'20\'19s only one of those groups that\u8217\'20\'19s exempt from the federal minimum wage, and that is people with disabilities,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Julie Christensen, director of policy and advocacy for Apse. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s based on a law that existed for a different population in a different period of time with a different labor market, with a different level of expectation, and I think most importantly, with a different sense of what people with disabilities can do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dr Christensen explained the bill provides a phase-out period for sheltered workshops to transition to paying at least minimum wage, includes funding for transition resources and data collection on the affected workers and the efforts to integrate them into the workforce.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have a lot of empathy for how scary this transition can look like,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I think we do a disservice in the messaging and the stories that are being perpetuated that are not the reality of where successful transition has happened and has led to amazing outcomes for people even with the most significant disabilities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230328ej3s0015s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
