\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18My name is cleared\u8217\'20\'19: the US workers fighting back against union busting\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2023\par 14:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Organizers say an increased unionizing drive has been met with threats and retaliation \u8211\'20\'13 and now the Senate wants answers\par \par \uc2 Last year, public support for labor unions hit a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/398303/approval-labor-unions-highest-point-1965.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high unseen since 1965}}}\uc2  amid high-profile union campaigns at major corporations including \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , Chipotle, REI and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s.\par \par \uc2 This renewed interest has been accompanied by aggressive opposition from employers: threats, intimidation and what workers allege are retaliatory firings. The backlash seems to be working, but workers and their supporters are fighting back.\par \par \uc2 The tension between popularity and pushback has prompted Senator Bernie Sanders and the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s health, education, labor and pensions (Help) committee to hold recent hearings on union busting, including what is expected to be a heated hearing with the recently departed \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  chairman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/mar/07/howard-schultz-testify-senate-starbucks-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Howard Schultz next Wednesday}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Help committee hearing comes as the number of petitions for union elections at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores has dropped from 71 a month last spring to about 10 a month recently. And, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://click.nl.npr.org/?qs=4533e4c79638a416a0bfc35136b795cc7d95dcd85848511e6daa3f67d5b39e0bde44a28a36f0b8671525114b9564ac9177cc60989e2bc307"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics}}}\uc2 , last year the national union membership rate fell by 0.2 percentage points, to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://click.nl.npr.org/?qs=4533e4c79638a416d8a58cc6a58d320df080a0950ce1137954a8966bdd5d347501c330ddae1db2bb72f052ccffd2fbb818565584881403dd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 10.1%}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the lowest on record and down from nearly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://click.nl.npr.org/?qs=4533e4c79638a416bd4aa1a5844fac72d85d9b2f647da6ffbef121278d3bc3408a44066b4cd033659a27a550dbdcc1f73d1b04c0201687a9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in three workers}}}\uc2  during the heyday of unions back in the 1950s.\par \par \uc2 Labor experts say one of the reasons that union popularity is not translating into union membership is corporate America\u8217\'20\'19s willingness to fire union organizers with few deterrents for doing so. One out of every five union election campaigns involves a claim that a worker was fired in retaliation for union organizing, according to a 2019 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/unlawful-employer-opposition-to-union-election-campaigns/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  (EPI).\par \par \uc2 Jesse Mason, a \uc2 Verizon\uc2  retail worker in Seattle, Washington, was supporting a union organizing drive at another local store and about to start trying to organize a union at his store when he was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cwa-union.org/news/releases/verizon-wireless-illegally-fired-retail-employee-for-organizing-union-seattle-store"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fired}}}\uc2  in April 2022.\par \par \uc2 He went on to file an unfair labor practice charge with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) alleging his firing was retaliatory for union activity.\par \par \uc2 In March 2023, Mason returned to work after winning reinstatement with back pay and reaching a settlement with \uc2 Verizon\uc2 , which did not admit to any wrongdoing in the settlement and still disputes that Mason\u8217\'20\'19s firing was related to union activity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs soon as I heard about the settlement, I was jumping up and down calling everyone I know. The whole reason I was insistent on getting my job back was I wanted to show everyone you have the right to organize,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mason. \u8220\'20\'1CMy store still isn\u8217\'20\'19t union-represented, but day one I\u8217\'20\'19m going to start with it, everyone knows I\u8217\'20\'19m going to do that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Once an unfair practice charge is filed with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , field staff gather evidence to determine the merit of the charge. If the regional director finds there is sufficient evidence, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  can file a complaint against the employer, unless both parties involved reach a settlement, and the case is brought to an administrative law judge.\par \par \uc2 Based on recent \uc2 NLRB\uc2  annual performance {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/sites/default/files/attachments/pages/node-130/nlrb-fy2022-par-508.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2 , 84% of charges with merit were resolved within a year. But the penalties for incurring unfair practice violations are slight.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe way that our current labor law is, employers do not receive any monetary penalties for breaking the law and so that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t deter them from breaking the law,\u8221\'20\'1D said Margaret Poydock, a policy analyst at the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CBy being willing to fire workers, it kind of sends a chilling effect among organizers who are trying to organize a union, so employers are able to suppress workers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to form unions with no repercussions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Poydock noted the data on worker firings tied to union elections is probably an undercount because it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t account for workers who file unfair labor practice charges before or in the absence of a union election being filed.\par \par \uc2 She cited the need to pass legislation such as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/11/us-workers-unionization-nlrb-level-playing-field"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pro act}}}\uc2 , reintroduced in Congress this year, which would establish financial penalties of at least $50,000 for labor law violations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEmployers are continuing to commit these violations because there\u8217\'20\'19s no financial disincentive, so they\u8217\'20\'19re considered a cost of doing business,\u8221\'20\'1D said Poydock. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are in a time where we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing a lot of workers who are joining unions, despite all the challenges, so that\u8217\'20\'19s great, but we do need to restore labor law in order to make it so workers have the right to freely organize and collectively bargain.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , more than 280 stores have won union elections since late 2021, but amid organizing campaigns more than 200 workers have been fired, with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  issuing complaints related to 60 firings and ordering the reinstatement of 22 workers so far. Many of the fired \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers have had to resort to \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  campaigns to help cope with their lost income while waiting for the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  to process their complaints.\par \par \uc2 Austin Locke, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  worker for three years in New York City, was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/mar/03/starbucks-unions-fired-workers-new-york-city-labor-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fired}}}\uc2  in July 2022 after leading a unionization effort at his store. He won reinstatement with back pay through New York City\u8217\'20\'19s Just Cause protections and returned to work in March 2023, though \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  continued to deny any wrongdoing in his firing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a long process. It was almost like I was in purgatory, because I was in a subliminal state, in between two different states. It was weird, and now that it\u8217\'20\'19s all over, I\u8217\'20\'19m happy and my name is cleared,\u8221\'20\'1D said Locke.\par \par \uc2 Aleah Bacetti worked at a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in Bel Air, Maryland, and successfully organized a union at her store in early 2022, but was then fired in July 2022 for what \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.restaurantdive.com/news/5-store-level-changes-driving-the-starbucks-union/636052/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 described}}}\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Cuse of harassing or abusive language\u8221\'20\'1D. Bacetti argued she experienced a lot of racism from customers and management while working at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and has since started working as a union organizer for Starbucks Workers United.\par \par \uc2 Employers are continuing to commit these violations because there\u8217\'20\'19s no financial disincentive, so they\u8217\'20\'19re considered a cost of doing business\par \par \uc2 Margaret Poydock of the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\par \par \uc2 Because she was fired on a policy technicality, she hasn\u8217\'20\'19t filed a charge with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , but is still considering filing a charge with the Equal Employment Opportunity Center.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  needs to be held accountable for it. But from all of my friends that have also been fired, what I\u8217\'20\'19ve witnessed and seen, it is a very long process,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1C[ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/mar/20/howard-schultz-starbucks-ceo-steps-down-labor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Former Starbucks CEO}}}\uc2  ] \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  doesn\u8217\'20\'19t actually care about any of his partners. It\u8217\'20\'19s a facade, because if he did, he would be listening to them instead of spending thousands of dollars to union-bust, to fire people and appeal all these motions in court instead of facing the facts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://one.starbucks.com/2023/03/08/contesting-a-complaint-involving-our-heritage-market-stores/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disputed}}}\uc2  all \uc2 NLRB\uc2  complaints and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/news-story/nlrb-region-3-buffalo-wins-administrative-law-judge-decision-requiring"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court rulings}}}\uc2  against the company and denied all allegations of labor law violations, of which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static1.squarespace.com/static/61195afce6d74d317a220f9f/t/640b36791a82e42b4191e603/1678456442091/SBWU+Shareholder+Letter.docx.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 1,300}}}\uc2  have been included in \uc2 NLRB\uc2 -issued complaints. It has pointed to two rulings in its favor, over a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://one.starbucks.com/2023/02/24/nlrb-recommends-new-election-in-overland-park-citing-substantial-misconduct/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 union election}}}\uc2  in Kansas and a case in which a judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/3517552-starbucks-hails-judges-ruling-in-union-case/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled}}}\uc2  against reinstating a fired worker in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 For workers who are fired during union campaigns, it can take several months to well over a year for an unfair labor practice charge to be reviewed and processed, complaints to be issued, and a settlement or court decision reached for the worker to be reinstated with back pay.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the quelling effects on unionization efforts that firings induce are immediate and can contribute to or result in workers\u8217\'20\'19 losing union elections or having to pull union election petitions due to lost support with workers fearful of losing their jobs and standing up against their management.\par \par \uc2 Vince Quiles, who worked at a \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, began an independent union-organizing campaign at his retail store in November 2022. He garnered enough support to file for a union election with more than 100 signatures, only to come up short with just 51 votes in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://whyy.org/articles/philadelphia-home-depot-unionization-vote/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 election }}}\uc2  after \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  brought in outside managers to the store to deter workers from voting in favor of the union.\par \par \uc2 A few months after the election, Quiles, who had worked at \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/home-depot-managers-philadelphia-union_n_63336cece4b0e376dbf0830c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than five years}}}\uc2 , was fired from his job, and he is now in the process of filing an unfair labor practice charge alleging it was retaliatory.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s definitely a targeted firing for insubordination,\u8221\'20\'1D said Quiles. \u8220\'20\'1CI definitely think \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  tried to calculate when they did it \u8211\'20\'13 they felt far enough removed past the union election, now they can set up a situation to fire me, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not just myself, its other people being targeted, people who were close to me and people who were vocally critical of \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  denied Quiles\u8217\'20\'19s firing was retaliatory and that others are being targeted by management. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re committed to protecting all of our associates\u8217\'20\'19 legal rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said a spokesperson in an email.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230322ej3m0015t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
