\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Scabby the Rat is an American labor icon. Why are his manufacturers disowning him?\b0\par\par\uc2 Tarpley Hitt \par \uc2 2,104 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2023\par 10:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The frightening character who appears amid union disputes can be traced back to a single factory \u8211\'20\'13 which wasn\u8217\'20\'19t unionized\par \par \uc2 In New York \u8211\'20\'13 a city poised to hire its first \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.entrepreneur.com/business-news/nyc-is-still-looking-to-hire-a-rat-czar/443829"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rat czar}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D after rat sightings {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnewyork.com/investigations/i-team-rat-sightings-doubled-in-nyc-over-past-12-months-according-to-health-data/4070243/#:~:text=2022%20NYC%20Rodent%20Activity%20by%20Zip%20Code&text=Prior%20to%20the%20pandemic%2C%20in,they%20found%20five%20years%20before."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doubled}}}\uc2  in the past year \u8211\'20\'13 street-side rodents are fairly commonplace. But the rat stationed on a Union Square curb is something of a different beast. This one is roughly 10ft tall, with incisors the size of iPads. Its eyes are bloodshot, its claws extended, and its belly marked with what look like open, oozing sores. Depending whom you ask, its name is \u8220\'20\'1CScabby\u8221\'20\'1D, or just \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Rat\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Since January, give or take, the Rat has been strapped into the bed of a \uc2 Ford\uc2  F-150, staring down Tammany Hall. The historic building is poised to become a giant pet store, \uc2 Petco\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s New York flagship, a three-floor bestial mall equipped with its own animal hospital.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Petco\uc2  has a reputation for hiring non-union contractors and the city\u8217\'20\'19s District Council of Carpenters is protesting the move by inflating a huge Scabby out front. \u8220\'20\'1CShame on \uc2 Petco\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D a nearby LED truck sign reads, \u8220\'20\'1Cfor destroying the area standard wages, health benefits, and pensions for construction workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Most days, the Rat stands there alone. But few need more context; Scabby is, as the name suggests, a familiar signifier of organized labor. He appears beside workers nationwide \u8211\'20\'13 at an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/WhereIsScabby/videos/154951549950623"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon warehouse}}}\uc2  in Illinois over the summer of 2021, at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/pjbwu/status/1550515059553697792"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Philadelphia Starbucks}}}\uc2  the following July, and at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/photo/?fbid=650770147052847&set=a.537087221754474"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rockford gas station}}}\uc2  just a few months back, where he was \u8220\'20\'1Cmortally wounded\u8221\'20\'1D after a disgruntled employee took it upon himself to \u8220\'20\'1Cshank defenseless Scabby in his side\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s impossible to say how many Scabbies are on the street at any given time, but there are 60 unions in the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the nation\u8217\'20\'19s largest federation of unions, which represents over 87% of unionized workers in the country \u8211\'20\'13 and many, perhaps most, have their own collections of inflatable Scabbies. A representative from the \uc2 Laborers\u8217\'20\'19 International Union of North America\uc2  (Liuna) estimated it had \u8220\'20\'1C40 or 50 rats\u8221\'20\'1D in the eastern region alone. An organizer with the International Union of Bricklayers and Allied Craftsworkers (BAC) guessed it had even more: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19d be surprised if the number was less than 60.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The rat elicits polarizing opinions (they tend to fall neatly along worker-management lines). But to both sides, Scabby is, as one organizer told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/08/06/1024315097/how-a-beloved-giant-rat-won-free-speech-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NPR}}}\uc2  in 2021, an \u8220\'20\'1Ciconic symbol of a labor dispute\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 If Scabby has name recognition, it is a relatively new feature in the history of American labor. The presence of building-sized rodent inflatables at worker actions was an outgrowth of the post-Reagan era, just a few years after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://libraries.uta.edu/news-events/blog/1981-patco-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1981 Patco air-traffic controllers strike}}}\uc2  sent union membership, and the wages they fought for, into the gutter. The exact origin of the labor dispute icon is, appropriately, disputed; two Chicago unions claim they independently came up with the idea, and their members still loyally, if somewhat wryly, guard their proprietary stake.\par \par \uc2 On one side, there\u8217\'20\'19s the \uc2 International Union of Operating Engineers (IUOE) \uc2 Local 150, a sheet metal union, whose president, Jim Sweeney, claims he thought it up in 1989 as a new tactic to shame non-union hiring. First, Sweeney made rat costumes to wear at strikes, playing with the idea of \u8220\'20\'1Crat contractors\u8221\'20\'1D. He found that, in the Chicago summer, the mascot suits acquired a distinctly rat-like smell, so he pivoted instead to a version no one would need to wear: a small inflatable rat roof attachment. Workers would strap it to a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  painted to look like an exterminator. In November 1989, Local 150 held a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://local150.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/12/scabby1.jpg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Name the Rat}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D contest for the balloon and its accompanying Rat Mobile. The next January, it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://local150.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/12/scabby2.jpg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  the winner: the car would be \u8220\'20\'1CScab Tracker\u8221\'20\'1D. The rat was dubbed \u8220\'20\'1CScabby\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But BAC members dismiss Sweeney\u8217\'20\'19s story. The actual Scabby genesis, BAC claims, came in 1990 or 1991. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no question that the first real Scabby \u8211\'20\'13 you know, a giant, ugly looking rat with a nasty belly and all that \u8211\'20\'13 was designed by Ken Lambert, who was working for our Chicago District Council at the time,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jake McIntyre, the assistant to the secretary-treasurer of BAC. His colleague Lou Padilla agrees. The original model, Padilla said, was still sitting in the Chicago office storage.\par \par \uc2 Anthony \u8220\'20\'1CDino\u8221\'20\'1D Esemplare, an organizer with Liuna, has special insight to the Rat\u8217\'20\'19s biggest break: a cameo in a 2002 episode of The Sopranos, courtesy, he said, of the unionized crew, who had access to a Scabby through the theatrical \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe only reason I know that was because my mother was a cast member on The Sopranos,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CMy mother was Frances Esemplare, who played {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sopranos.fandom.com/wiki/Nucci_Gualtieri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nucci}}}\uc2 , Paulie Walnuts\u8217\'20\'19s mom.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Every Scabby comes from one factory\par \par \uc2 At least one aspect of Scabby\u8217\'20\'19s backstory is agreed upon \u8211\'20\'13 nearly all of the rat balloons across the country have come from a sole distributor: a Plainfield, Illinois, company called Big Sky Balloons. Big Sky was founded in 1980 as a hot air balloon tour business by Peggy and Mike O\u8217\'20\'19Connor, a couple whose interest in activism manifested only as obsessive balloon advocacy. They\u8217\'20\'19d had a balloon-themed wedding, where guests could float around on company vessels; they traveled in their own personal hot air balloon, dubbed August One after their wedding date; and they set a record in 1984, when they became the first married couple to make the 6.5-hour trip across Lake Michigan by balloon. (In 1987, one article notes, Mike floated the idea of birthing their son, Tylor, in a balloon; Peggy shot it down).\par \par \uc2 Big Sky is partially responsible for the standalone inflatables one sees across America\u8217\'20\'19s curbs. In the early 90s, frustrated by ballooning\u8217\'20\'19s weather sensitivity, the O\u8217\'20\'19Connors developed \u8220\'20\'1Can electronic motor and fan system\u8221\'20\'1D that kept the inflatables\u8217\'20\'19 form even on Chicago\u8217\'20\'19s windiest days.\par \par \uc2 The O\u8217\'20\'19Connors, who did not comment for this piece, have backed up BAC\u8217\'20\'19s story in the past: it was Ken Lambert who commissioned the first official Scabby, after seeing Big Sky\u8217\'20\'19s billboard off I-294 and calling them immediately. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.chicagotribune.com/business/ct-biz-scabby-giant-rat-balloon-union-protest-ban-20190807-fbqnn7rorne75oc2w72wel3ise-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interviews}}}\uc2 , Peggy recalled that Mike had come up with a design and Lambert had given feedback; he wanted it \u8220\'20\'1Cmeaner looking\u8221\'20\'1D, with big claws and \u8220\'20\'1Cfestering nipples\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Between the electric motor and burgeoning union business, Big Sky\u8217\'20\'19s sales doubled between 1990 and 1991. It started making inflatable friends for Scabby: a cockroach, a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inflatableimages.com/gallery/union-inflatables/union-inflatables-union-inflatables"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Corporate Fat Cat}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inflatableimages.com/gallery/union-inflatables/union-inflatables-union-inflatables"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Greedy Pig}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. The originals changed, too. \u8220\'20\'1CThey range in size from 6ft to 14ft,\u8221\'20\'1D Padilla said. \u8220\'20\'1CSome are yellow. The early ones were just black with white and red bellies.\u8221\'20\'1D In the later years, the fingers and whiskers became more menacing. \u8220\'20\'1CThey would make [the belly] more scabrous,\u8221\'20\'1D he added, \u8220\'20\'1Cwith sores on it and stuff.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Big Sky itself was, incredibly, not a union shop. It made most of its other balloons for major city businesses \u8211\'20\'13 the \uc2 Chicago Bulls\uc2 , Cubs, and \uc2 White Sox\uc2 ; the Navy Pier, the Chicago Auto Show, and most car dealerships in the area, employers who could easily have found themselves with a Scabby parked out front. By the late 2010s, Mike was also, according to Padilla, \u8220\'20\'1Ca big Trumper\u8221\'20\'1D who refused to do \u8220\'20\'1Cany political stuff\u8221\'20\'1D. None of that kept Big Sky from selling, at various points, a half-dozen Scabbies per month, at up to $10,000 a pop.\par \par \uc2 But these days, America\u8217\'20\'19s Scabby supply may be running dry. When I called Big Sky last week, a woman identifying herself as Michelle said that, in the past month, the business had come under new ownership. \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t do any of the unions or the rat stuff any more,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have nothing to do with the inflatable rats.\u8221\'20\'1D The identities of Big Sky\u8217\'20\'19s new owners or their plans for the business remain a mystery. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not at liberty to say,\u8221\'20\'1D Michelle repeated, claiming \u8220\'20\'1Ca lot of stuff is non-disclosure\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear whether the sale will actually change the rat market. The website still advertises a selection of \u8220\'20\'1CUnion Inflatables\u8221\'20\'1D, linking out to an Ohio-based contractor called Inflatable Images. Michelle wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t say how long Big Sky had used its services, and the firm didn\u8217\'20\'19t respond for comment. But one Chicago Tribune article suggests the O\u8217\'20\'19Connors might have been outsourcing Scabbies as early as 1997. \u8220\'20\'1CI had the feeling that they never made the rats,\u8221\'20\'1D Padilla admitted. Their warehouse was filled with hot air balloons, but nothing for rodent production, he said. And they bought in an outside seamstress when the rats needed repairs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think they used someone else,\u8221\'20\'1D Padilla said, but \u8220\'20\'1Cthey didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to let us know. They probably thought we would go around them and order directly from the company. But my intention was just to find out who made them in case they went out of business, which is where we\u8217\'20\'19re at now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Threat of extermination\par \par \uc2 Scabbies have survived worse than a supply chain crunch. The inflatables make for an easy and frequent target of anti-union vandalism \u8211\'20\'13 attracting over the years countless stabbings, multiple hit-and-runs, at least one \u8220\'20\'1Csmurfing\u8221\'20\'1D incident (getting doused with blue paint), and, per Esemplare, an arrest by \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , who held a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gothamcenter.org/blog/people-love-the-rat-how-scabby-labors-mascot-took-new-york#_ftn14"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 super-sized}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D 30ft version \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2001/09/04/nyregion/public-lives-a-break-from-the-rat-race-for-a-union-organizer.html?searchResultPosition=3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for interrogation in 1 Police Plaza}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for over eight hours.\par \par \uc2 The rats are also routinely challenged in court. The New York labor attorney Tamir Rosenblum told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2020/12/18/947918263/the-history-of-scabby-the-rat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NPR}}}\uc2  he had represented Scabby \u8220\'20\'1Cat least 50 times\u8221\'20\'1D, and every few years, federal judges deliberate over whether the Rat constitutes protected speech. There was the time the Illinois town of Orland Park confiscated a Scabby stationed outside the Crystal Tree Golf and Country Club in 2000, prompting a \u8220\'20\'1CFree the Rat\u8221\'20\'1D rally attended by about 1,000 Scabby fans. Scabby {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.casemine.com/judgement/us/59147ce2add7b049344362d1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won}}}\uc2  a federal first amendment lawsuit against the village, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://casetext.com/case/tucker-v-city-of-fairfield-ohio"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he did again}}}\uc2  in 2003, when a union sued after an Ohio zoning official threatened strikers with arrest for violating the city\u8217\'20\'19s prohibition on structures in the public right-of-way.\par \par \uc2 Legally speaking, the Rat has mostly come out on top \u8211\'20\'13 outlasting a George W Bush-era \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  lawyer who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20080923111156/https://www.nlrb.gov/shared_files/Board%20Decisions/346/346105.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tried to limit}}}\uc2  the character\u8217\'20\'19s legal scope in 2006, a New Jersey supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://eu.njherald.com/story/news/2009/02/05/court-inflatable-rat-has-rights/4070476007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 case}}}\uc2  in 2009, and another {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.natlawreview.com/article/obama-board-and-giant-rat-nlrb-holds-union-use-inflatable-monster-rat-does-not-constitute--0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NLRB challenge}}}\uc2  in 2011, when the board decided 3-1 in favor of the rat\u8217\'20\'19s rights (the fourth dissented, lambasting the coercive tactics of the \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.natlawreview.com/article/obama-board-and-giant-rat-nlrb-holds-union-use-inflatable-monster-rat-does-not-constitute--0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rat collosi}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D.)\par \par \uc2 Scabby had less luck in 2018, when a union claimed a sign ordinance in Grand Chute, Wisconsin \u8211\'20\'13 which seemed to bar union inflatables, but not Christmas-related ones \u8211\'20\'13 discriminated based on content. The town\u8217\'20\'19s code enforcement officer seemed to support the union\u8217\'20\'19s case in court, testifying that Scabby might qualify for the holiday exception \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberglaw.com/public/desktop/document/Constr__Gen_Laborers_Union_No_330_v_Town_of_Grand_Chute_No_181739?doc_id=XV3TSKB0000N"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 if he donned a Santa hat}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. But the seventh circuit disagreed, upholding a lower court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling for Grand Chute.\par \par \uc2 Just recently the Rat survived its greatest threat of extermination, after Trump appointed Peter Robb, the labor lawyer who led Reagan\u8217\'20\'19s crusade against Patco, and a known Scabby hater, to the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 . In 2020, Robb brought a complaint against Local 150, one of the Chicago unions that claimed to have invented it, arguing its inflatables amounted to \u8220\'20\'1Csecondary boycotts\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 or prohibited demonstrations intended to scare away an employer\u8217\'20\'19s business associates. The case was dismissed in 2021, when Joe Biden fired Robb; his replacement declared the proceeding a \u8220\'20\'1Cwaste\u8221\'20\'1D of \uc2 NLRB\uc2  resources.\par \par \uc2 Even if Big Sky is out of the rat game, Esemplare doubts unions will struggle to find them: \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s so many out there that if somebody got jammed up, you can always lend them.\u8221\'20\'1D Both Liuna and BAC said they had a stash of 50 or 60, and the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have taken to loaning their store to smaller shops. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Rat was a statement,\u8221\'20\'1D Esemplare explained. \u8220\'20\'1CIt wasn\u8217\'20\'19t about Big Sky Balloons \u8211\'20\'13 that was just a vendor who was lucky enough to make hundreds of rats for the labor movement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Rat \u8211\'20\'13 when people see that in the streets \u8211\'20\'13 they know what that rat means,\u8221\'20\'1D Esemplare said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat there\u8217\'20\'19s people out there trying to better somebody else\u8217\'20\'19s life. It could have been anything. It could have been a squirrel. But we chose a rat.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230316ej3g002pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
