\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 Arkansas leads charge to weaken child labor protections\b0\par\par\uc2 Edwin Rios \par \uc2 631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2023\par 18:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Law weakens state\u8217\'20\'19s oversight of child labor and represents latest attempt in mostly Republican-led states to ease regulations\par \par \uc2 The governor of Arkansas, \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2 , on Tuesday signed a bill that significantly rolls back protections against child labor, eliminating state requirements to verify that children are at least 16 before they receive a job.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Sanders, who before becoming governor was White House press secretary under Donald Trump, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/03/08/huckabee-sanders-arkansas-child-labor/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  state law had been \u8220\'20\'1Cburdensome and obsolete\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The new law weakens official oversight of child labor practices across the state.\par \par \uc2 The bill\u8217\'20\'19s sponsor, Rebecca Burkes, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nwahomepage.com/news/governor-sanders-signed-into-law-new-child-labor-laws/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  KNWA the previous regulation was \u8220\'20\'1Cone small burden on businesses, and also steps in front of parents\u8217\'20\'19 decision-making process about whether their child under 16 years of age can get a job\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Passage of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nwahomepage.com/news/governor-sanders-signed-into-law-new-child-labor-laws/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Youth Hiring Act of 2023}}}\uc2  represents the latest attempt in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/v7b4d9/rkansas-republicans-relaxing-child-labor-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mostly Republican-led states}}}\uc2  to loosen child labor laws, despite federal officials seeking to crack down on offenses.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, lawmakers are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2023/03/03/child-labor-hours-could-change-under-ohio-senate-bill/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 considering}}}\uc2  a bill that would let 14- and 15-year-old children work year-round until 9pm each day. The bill has bipartisan support and includes a resolution that would ask the federal government to change its child labor laws.\par \par \uc2 In Iowa, legislative proposals would allow children at least 15 years old to sell alcohol and children at least 14 years old to work specific jobs in meatpacking plants. The Iowa bill would also protect companies from liability if a child got sick or injured or died while at work.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in Minnesota, which is led by a Democratic governor, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2023/02/11/child-labor-iowa/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a bill}}}\uc2  that would permit children aged 16 and 17 to work construction jobs.\par \par \uc2 Reports by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/25/us/unaccompanied-migrant-child-workers-exploitation.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2  have exposed the hiring of migrant children to work dangerous jobs at factories and elsewhere, flouting federal law.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/25/us/unaccompanied-migrant-child-workers-exploitation.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Describing}}}\uc2  a \u8220\'20\'1Cwidespread exploitation of migrant children\u8221\'20\'1D, the Times noted that a recent surge in unaccompanied minors across the southern border has driven many into brutal work across the US, forcing some to balance school and work, whether processing milk for Ben & Jerry\u8217\'20\'19s ice cream or making socks for clothing stores.\par \par \uc2 A proposed class action lawsuit against Ben & Jerry\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.classaction.org/blog/class-action-alleges-ben-and-jerrys-supply-chain-uses-migrant-child-labor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accuses}}}\uc2  the company of defying its own claims that its ice cream is \u8220\'20\'1Cethically sourced\u8221\'20\'1D, pointing to the use of migrant children in at least one manufacturing supplier.\par \par \uc2 In response, the company has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nypost.com/2023/02/28/ben-jerrys-suppliers-using-child-labor-despite-progressive-values/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  it is \u8220\'20\'1Copposed to child labor of any kind whatsoever\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Chas an established track record standing for justice and equity for all\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Ben & Jerry\u8217\'20\'19s did not immediately respond to a Guardian request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Federal regulators have clamped down on companies that have used children as labor.\par \par \uc2 Recently, labor department investigators {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20230217-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that Packer Sanitation Services, \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the largest food safety sanitation services in the United States\u8221\'20\'1D, hired more than 100 children between the ages of 13 and 17 to work in \u8220\'20\'1Cin hazardous occupations and had them working overnight shifts at 13 meat processing facilities in eight states\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Duties included working with caustic materials that caused chemical burns.\par \par \uc2 In December, the company agreed to \u8220\'20\'1Ccomply with [federal] child labor provisions in all of its operations nationwide, and to take significant steps to ensure future compliance with the law, including employing an outside compliance specialist\u8221\'20\'1D, according to a labor department press release.\par \par \uc2 In February, the company paid $1.5m in civil penalties.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese children should never have been employed in meat packing plants and this can only happen when employers do not take responsibility to prevent child labor violations from occurring in the first place,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20230217-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  Jessica Looman, principal deputy administrator for the labor department wage and hour division.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230308ej38002pb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
