\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  fired a union organizer. New York City got him rehired\b0\par\par\uc2 Wilfred Chan \par \uc2 1,292 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2023\par 15:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Austin Locke was sacked days after he helped unionize workers. A monumental new labor law means he\u8217\'20\'19s back on the job\par \par \uc2 Austin Locke was halfway through his shift at the New York City Starbucks where he\u8217\'20\'19d worked for three years when his supervisor ordered him into the back room. The store manager and the district manager were there too, and they had a piece of paper for him: he was fired.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAre you sure want to do this?\u8221\'20\'1D Locke asked them repeatedly. He knew that what they were about to do was illegal.\par \par \uc2 It didn\u8217\'20\'19t seem like a coincidence that just five days earlier, Locke had helped organize a successful vote to unionize {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DitmarsSBWU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the coffee shop}}}\uc2 , joining hundreds of other shops in a growing national movement called Starbucks Workers United. And \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has been known for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/26/amazon-trader-joes-starbucks-anti-union-measures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 retaliating}}}\uc2  against those involved.\par \par \uc2 But the Seattle-based coffee company said Locke was fired for forgetting to sign a log after getting his temperature checked for Covid \u8211\'20\'13 something that many other workers regularly forgot as well. The company also accused the 28-year-old of making a false report about a supervisor physically pushing to keep him out of a meeting \u8211\'20\'13 an incident caught on security footage that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  refused to look at.\par \par \uc2 Locke was fired last July and if it had happened in another American city, there\u8217\'20\'19s a chance that would have been the end of it. Most employment in the US operates according to a nightmarish legal doctrine called \u8220\'20\'1Cat-will\u8221\'20\'1D, meaning companies can fire you over almost anything. While it\u8217\'20\'19s illegal to fire someone for organizing a union, or over characteristics such as race, gender or disability, a boss can legally justify axing you for just about any other reason \u8211\'20\'13 maybe they don\u8217\'20\'19t like your haircut or your music taste, or they think you laugh too loud. And if you think your firing may have broken the law, the burden is on you to provide the evidence \u8211\'20\'13 often something you don\u8217\'20\'19t have access to.\par \par \uc2 If that sounds barbaric, that\u8217\'20\'19s because it is. The United States is one of just a few places where at-will is the norm; virtually every other country requires employers to provide some form of cause to terminate someone after a probation period. What few Americans know is that at-will employment is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nelp.org/blog/reversing-labor-laws-rooted-in-slavery/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rooted in slavery}}}\uc2  : after emancipation, railroad bosses who had profited from forced labor argued that if workers now had the \u8220\'20\'1Cright to quit\u8221\'20\'1D, then bosses should have the \u8220\'20\'1Cright to fire\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a way to keep workers fearful and in line. Unfortunately, judges agreed, and a century and a half later, the doctrine remains the default form of employment here. That doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean it\u8217\'20\'19s popular: a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.filesforprogress.org/memos/worker-power.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2020 survey}}}\uc2  found that 68% of Americans disagreed that an employer should be able to fire you for any reason.\par \par \uc2 Irene Tung, a policy analyst at the National Employment Law Project (NELP), says her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nelp.org/publication/fired-with-no-reason-no-warning-no-severance-the-case-for-replacing-at-will-employment-with-a-just-cause-standard/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research}}}\uc2  has found \u8220\'20\'1Cthe at-will system creates pressure for US workers to accept poor working conditions to avoid being fired, and keeps the power balance in favor of the employer. It drives down the quality of life for all workers, even those who aren\u8217\'20\'19t fired.\u8221\'20\'1D At the height of the pandemic, a serious movement emerged to question it. \u8220\'20\'1CSo many workers were experiencing retaliation for speaking up with concerns about Covid in their workplaces, and then really finding out that the protections for speaking out in the workplace are very, very weak.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2021, the NELP and labor organizations like the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  (32 BJ SEIU) helped pass a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/workers/workersrights/fastfood-retail-workers.page"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 law}}}\uc2  in New York City overturning at-will employment for fast-food workers \u8211\'20\'13 what Tung calls a \u8220\'20\'1Cmonumental shift\u8221\'20\'1D. The provision is called \u8220\'20\'1Cjust cause\u8221\'20\'1D and requires any boss firing a fast-food worker in New York City to provide a valid reason \u8211\'20\'13 such as poor performance or misconduct \u8211\'20\'13 only after giving the worker multiple warnings and enough time to correct the issue, a system called \u8220\'20\'1Cprogressive discipline\u8221\'20\'1D. Any fast-food worker found to have been fired without just cause must be reinstated to their position, with back pay.\par \par \uc2 Locke just became the first worker in New York City to experience that. The day after his firing, he filed a complaint with the city against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for sacking him without just cause. The form \u8220\'20\'1Cwas very simple to fill out\u8221\'20\'1D, Locke says \u8211\'20\'13 and he almost immediately heard back from New York\u8217\'20\'19s department of consumer and worker protection saying it would take his case. Over the next few months, he worked with the agency\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers, who were able to subpoena \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for the security footage and prove that Locke\u8217\'20\'19s supervisor had indeed pushed him.\par \par \uc2 Last week, after nearly seven months of waiting, Locke finally got the good news: \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had agreed to settle. He would get his job back along with back pay and damages, equalling about $17,000 before tax. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  also agreed to pay $3,500 in civil penalties, and to abide by the just cause law. But the coffee giant did not admit any wrongdoing in the settlement. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile we continue to disagree with the city\u8217\'20\'19s interpretation of what constitutes egregious misconduct under the Just Cause law, we have agreed to reinstate Mr Locke and reduce his corrective action to a written warning for violation of health and safety protocols,\u8221\'20\'1D said a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesperson, Andrew Trull, in an emailed statement.\par \par \uc2 Locke had also filed a complaint with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , alleging that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  illegally retaliated against him for his involvement with the union \u8211\'20\'13 he dropped that suit as part of his settlement. But on Wednesday, a federal judge ruled that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had committed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2023/03/01/starbucks-union-ruling-buffalo/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cegregious\u8221\'20\'1D violations}}}\uc2  of federal labor law in firing other \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers at locations in upstate New York. After that ruling, Senator Bernie Sanders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/news-sanders-announces-help-committee-vote-on-subpoena-for-starbucks-ceo-howard-schultz/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scheduled}}}\uc2  a vote to subpoena the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , to \u8220\'20\'1Chold \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Mr Schultz accountable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In New York City, Locke\u8217\'20\'19s victory has invigorated other \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  employees, including 19-year-old Riley Fell and 26-year-old Crys Mathieu. Both are working with 32 BJ SEIU to try to unionize their shops in lower Manhattan, and last week each filed a complaint against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for violating another part of the just cause law that gives workers the right to consistent hours. If \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  retaliates against them, they\u8217\'20\'19re hoping they\u8217\'20\'19ll be protected like Locke was. \u8220\'20\'1CDCWP gave him the full back pay, which is not something we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen before. It\u8217\'20\'19s awesome to know that that\u8217\'20\'19s possible,\u8221\'20\'1D Fell says.\par \par \uc2 Labor advocates\u8217\'20\'19 next goal is to pass an even broader bill called the secure jobs act, which would extend just cause protections to nearly all private sector workers in the city (and ban employers from using invasive electronic surveillance to discipline workers). Tung, who is involved in the effort, hopes to see laws like these spread across the United States. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s long overdue that this country joins the rest of the world,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Locke donned his green apron and strode back into the store. He\u8217\'20\'19s happy to see his co-workers again, and with a union behind him, he\u8217\'20\'19s feeling \u8220\'20\'1Crelaxed and secure\u8221\'20\'1D. But he\u8217\'20\'19s still working for the same managers. \u8220\'20\'1CI can tell it\u8217\'20\'19s awkward for them,\u8221\'20\'1D he says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s been so many times when they\u8217\'20\'19ve been having problems with my numbers in the system,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I\u8217\'20\'19ve had to hold back my tongue from saying: \u8216\'20\'18Oh, I guess that wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have happened if you hadn\u8217\'20\'19t fired me.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230303ej33000us\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
