\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  condemned for \u8216\'20\'18intimidation\u8217\'20\'19 of US union organizers\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 652 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2023\par 19:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bernie Sanders moves to summon chief executive \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  to Senate committee to explain repeated anti-union violations\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is under fire over the company\u8217\'20\'19s response to unionization efforts as senator Bernie Sanders threatens to call its chief executive before his committee on alleged labor violations and staff petition for it to end \u8220\'20\'1Cintimidation\u8221\'20\'1D of organizers.\par \par \uc2 Sanders, chairman of the Senate health, education, labor and pensions (Help) committee, announced on Wednesday that the committee will be voting on whether to issue a subpoena to compel the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  chief, \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , to testify about Starbuck\u8217\'20\'19s federal labor law violations, and to authorize a committee investigation into labor-law violations committed by major corporations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor nearly a year, I and many of my colleagues in the Senate have repeatedly asked Mr Schultz to respect the constitutional right of workers at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to form a union and to stop violating federal labor laws,\u8221\'20\'1D Sanders said in a press release confirming the 8 March vote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMr Schultz has failed to respond to those requests. He has denied meeting and document requests, skirted congressional oversight attempts, and refused to answer any of the serious questions we have asked. Unfortunately, Mr Schultz has given us no choice but to subpoena him.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The move came after 44 employees at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  headquarters in Seattle and 22 additional anonymous employees signed on to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SBWorkersUnited/status/1630945049243537409/photo/1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 petition}}}\uc2  calling on the company to reverse a return-to-office mandate and \u8220\'20\'1Cto commit to a policy of neutrality and respect federal labor laws by agreeing to follow fair election principles, and allow store partners, whether pro- or anti-union, to decide for themselves, free from fear, coercion, and intimidation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 According to Starbucks Workers United, more than 200 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers have been fired in retaliation for organizing. The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  has alleged that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has fired over 60 union leaders across the country. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has aggressively opposed unionization efforts from the first stores to unionize in late 2021 in Buffalo, New York, to over 350 stores around the US that have held union elections. More than 280 stores have won union elections, though a first union contract has not been reached at any store so far.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, administrative law judge Michael A Rosas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/bFNSB8EwVu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued}}}\uc2  a sweeping decision in Buffalo, ordering the reinstatement of seven fired \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers with back pay, and issuing a bargaining order for three \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores. The order requires 27 workers to be reimbursed for lost wages, for Schultz and the senior vice-president of operations, Denise Nelson, to read a notice or make a video for employees in Buffalo informing them of their rights, and for the company to post a national physical and electronic notice.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s what we, the workers, have been saying for more than a year now: that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , at every chance they get, bust the union and get us to be intimidated by it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Austin Locke, an employee for nearly six years in New York who was fired and recently won reinstatement after the city {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/sep/02/new-york-city-sues-starbucks-firing-union-organizing-barista"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  sued }}}\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  under \u8220\'20\'1Cjust-cause\u8221\'20\'1D protections. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ve just been stonewalling us the whole time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe news of this win is single-handedly the most exciting thing that\u8217\'20\'19s happened in this campaign thus far,\u8221\'20\'1D said Michael Sanabria, a barista from the Transit Commons location in Buffalo, New York, in a press release on the decision .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHaving to reinstate all of these workers, reopen the first \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  location closed in the name of union-busting, and most importantly, post notices in every single store across the country for the duration of the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  organizing campaign is such a massive win for us, and for the labor movement as a whole.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter waiting through months of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 stalling tactics, this will reinvigorate and re-energize the momentum of this movement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Guardian has contacted \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230302ej32002xo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
