\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporters criticise union for backing trans coverage protest\b0\par\par\uc2 Martin Pengelly \par \uc2 514 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2023\par 16:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 High-profile reporters say in letter \u8216\'20\'18We are journalists, not activists\u8217\'20\'19 after contributors protest coverage of trans issues\par \par \uc2 A dispute at the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  over its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/17/new-york-times-contributors-open-letter-protest-anti-trans-coverage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 coverage of transgender issues}}}\uc2  deepened with news of a letter signed by high-profile reporters, criticising the Times\u8217\'20\'19 union president for her own letter on the issue, in which she said staff who protested the paper\u8217\'20\'19s trans coverage were concerned about \u8220\'20\'1Ca hostile working environment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFactual, accurate journalism that is written, edited and published in accordance with Times standards does not create a hostile workplace,\u8221\'20\'1D read the new letter, signed by the chief White House correspondent, Peter Baker, political correspondent Lisa Lerer and other senior figures and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2023/02/new-york-times-journalists-letter-guild"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  by Vanity Fair.\par \par \uc2 It added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are journalists, not activists. That line should be clear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last week, nearly a thousand contributors and thousands of supporters signed a letter protesting against the newspaper\u8217\'20\'19s coverage of trans issues.\par \par \uc2 Though \u8220\'20\'1Cplenty of reporters at the Times cover trans issues fairly\u8221\'20\'1D, the letter said, the paper had published more than \u8220\'20\'1C15,000 words of front?-?page coverage debating the propriety of medical care for trans children\u8221\'20\'1D in the past year.\par \par \uc2 The letter also said \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporting had been cited in support of anti-trans legislation in Republican-led states.\par \par \uc2 In response, a paper spokesperson rejected \u8220\'20\'1Cany claim that our coverage is biased\u8221\'20\'1D and highlighted \u8220\'20\'1Cthe breadth of our coverage on transgender issues across both news and opinion\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In an internal memo to staff, \uc2 Joe Kahn\uc2 , the executive editor, said the paper had \u8220\'20\'1Ca clear policy prohibiting Times journalists from attacking one another\u8217\'20\'19s journalism publicly or signaling their support for such attacks\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In her letter, reported by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.semafor.com/article/02/19/2023/the-row-over-the-new-york-times-transgender-coverage-embroils-its-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Semafor}}}\uc2 , Susan DeCarava, president of the New York Times News Guild chapter, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.semafor.com/article/02/19/2023/the-row-over-the-new-york-times-transgender-coverage-embroils-its-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  Times employees were \u8220\'20\'1Cprotected in collectively raising concerns that conditions of their employment constitute a hostile working environment. This was the concern explicitly raised in the letter at issue.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday the new staff letter said DeCarava had betrayed \u8220\'20\'1Ca fundamental misunderstanding of our responsibilities as journalists\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It added: \u8220\'20\'1CRegretfully, our own union leadership now seems determined to undermine the ethical and professional protections that we depend on to guard the independence and integrity of our journalism.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery day, partisan actors seek to influence, attack, or discredit our work. We accept that. But what we don\u8217\'20\'19t accept is what the Guild appears to be endorsing: a workplace in which any opinion or disagreement about Times coverage can be recast as a matter of \u8216\'20\'18workplace conditions\u8217\'20\'19 \u8230\'20\'26 We are journalists, not activists. That line should be clear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2023/02/new-york-times-journalists-letter-guild"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to Vanity Fair}}}\uc2 , the Guild said it was \u8220\'20\'1Ccommitted to representing every member equally and fairly, regardless of reporting assignment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe take no position on the subject matter of editorial coverage and fight hard for every member\u8217\'20\'19s right to work in a healthy and safe environment, free of harassment and discrimination. These are principles that are at the core of trade unionism and are central to our mission.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230222ej2m002bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
