\par\par \uc2 Global development\par \b \uc2 As US hospital profits, health workers struggle with chronic understaffing\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 957 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2023\par 12:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The largest hospital company in the country has money to address staff shortages but chooses not to, workers say\par \par \uc2 Union contracts covering 22,000 healthcare workers at HCA, the largest hospital corporation in the US, are expiring in the coming months at 30 hospitals in five states as healthcare workers push the company to address chronic understaffing they say burns out workers and jeopardizes patient safety.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hcavsamerica.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/SEIU_StaffingPaper_R8.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  published in January 2023 by \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  (\uc2 SEIU\uc2 ) found staffing ratios at HCA Hospitals in 2020 were significantly lower than state averages in 19 of 20 states where HCA operates facilities and 30% lower than national averages.\par \par \uc2 Workers contacted by the Guardian said the corporation had the money to fix staffing issues. HCA spent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hcavsamerica.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/SEIU_StaffingPaper_R8.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $8bn}}}\uc2  on stock buybacks and reported a profit of about $7bn in 2021 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/finance/hca-revenue-net-income-up-in-q4-6-details.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $5.6 bn}}}\uc2  in 2022, while many HCA workers are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/business/corporations/ceos-public-u-s-firms-earn-320-times-much-workers-n1263195"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid less than $15 an hour}}}\uc2 . From 2011 to 2021, HCA paid out $4.9bn in dividends to shareholders and spent $26.9bn on stock buybacks.\par \par \uc2 In Las Vegas, Nevada, Jody Domineck, a pediatric nurse for 18 years, said among 4,700 members across the three hospitals in town, the primary issues facing healthcare workers are the lack of safe staffing and resources required to take care of patients.\par \par \uc2 I know it wasn't enough... our patients deserve more\par \par \uc2 Jody Domineck, pediatric nurse\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorking short-staffed means that one kid is having trouble breathing and one kid is in excruciating pain, and I have to decide where to go because there\u8217\'20\'19s only one of me, which means somebody waits,\u8221\'20\'1D Domineck said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt means that I\u8217\'20\'19ve had to walk away from a mom that\u8217\'20\'19s worried about her kid because I have to go check on the other patients because there\u8217\'20\'19s not enough people to do both. It means that at the end of the day, I sit in my car and I cry because I did everything and I know it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t enough for all of them, that our patients deserve more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said the understaffing has driven high turnover and it puts workers\u8217\'20\'19 nursing licenses at risk because of the heightened risk of missing something or making a mistake because each nurse is responsible for too many patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a huge burden and at some point, people can\u8217\'20\'19t tolerate that anymore, they can\u8217\'20\'19t survive under that, so we\u8217\'20\'19ve lost a lot of bedside nurses,\u8221\'20\'1D added Domineck. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen that HCA made billions in profits last year. We would really like to see them invest that to improve patient care, improve staffing ratios and improve the working conditions for the staff.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hcavsamerica.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/SEIU_StaffingPaper_R8.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey }}}\uc2  conducted by \uc2 SEIU\uc2  in January 2022 of 1,500 nurses in HCA hospitals, 89% agreed short staffing is compromising patient care at their hospitals. Among HCA workers surveyed in Florida, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hcavsamerica.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/SEIU_StaffingPaper_R8.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 47%}}}\uc2  reported wanting to leave their job due to burnout.\par \par \uc2 Penny Caesar, a unit secretary at HCA Florida Westside Hospital in Plantation, Florida, since 2008, described chronic understaffing at her hospital, low and stagnant wages and high turnover that have deteriorated working conditions and patient care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe patients complain. They\u8217\'20\'19re waiting 30 minutes to go to the bathroom -now they need their own bed changed. That they\u8217\'20\'19ve been waiting over 30 minutes for pain meds. The weekends are the worst. They\u8217\'20\'19re really understaffed on the weekends,\u8221\'20\'1D said Caesar. \u8220\'20\'1CI love my job and all my nurses love their job, but we\u8217\'20\'19ve been burned out. We really get burnt out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She argued patient-to-nurse ratios are high to the point where workers can\u8217\'20\'19t take breaks because there is no coverage, that many nurses have left through the Covid-19 pandemic because they can make more money working as travel nurses, and that the hospital hasn\u8217\'20\'19t made efforts to resolve staffing issues and if workers complain, they get disciplined with write-ups.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re so understaffed because the nurses are so overworked. They want to keep calling us heroes and the families of patients ask: \u8216\'20\'18Why are they calling you heroes and they\u8217\'20\'19re not giving us the proper staff?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Caesar. \u8220\'20\'1CThey see it, we don\u8217\'20\'19t have to tell them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bella Panchal, a speech pathologist at HCA-owned Riverside Community Hospital in Riverside, California, since 2018, also claimed her hospital suffers from chronic understaffing and the constant burnout and turnover it induces, especially given the traumatic conditions many nurses worked under during the Covid-19 pandemic. Her hospital lost three workers to Covid-19, and many nurses had to take on the burden of holding patients\u8217\'20\'19 hands as they passed away in hospitals, without family members being able to sit by their side due to Covid-19 lockdown protocols.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was horrifying for the nurses,\u8221\'20\'1D Panchal said. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of the nurses have PTSD.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Due to the chronic understaffing, Panchal claimed the workplace culture at the hospital is one where workers often have to skip breaks and work past their scheduled shift unpaid.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe almost never take a break. If I were to take a break, I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have finished my work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Panchal. \u8220\'20\'1CUnfortunately, with HCA, they want you to work every other weekend, so there\u8217\'20\'19s very little work and life balance and you\u8217\'20\'19re running ragged when you are working, so it\u8217\'20\'19s very stressful.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She argued HCA needs to invest in improving workplace cultures and conditions and hiring workers as staff rather than on a per diem basis.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey have the money, they can do it,\u8221\'20\'1D added Panchal.\par \par \uc2 HCA did not respond to multiple requests for comment on this story.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230222ej2m001me\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
