\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 FTSE 100 surges over 8,000 points before rising US producer prices dampen mood; \uc2 British Gas\uc2  owner \uc2 Centrica\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s profits triple \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 6,971 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 February 2023\par 16:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 UK share index has hit record levels this morning, before surprise jump in US producer price inflation hits stocksFull story: \uc2 British Gas\uc2  owner \uc2 Centrica\uc2  reports record profits of \u163\'00\'A33.3bnFTSE 100 hits 8,000 points for first time as recession fears ease\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 hits new closing high\par \par \uc2 And finally\u8230\'20\'26. the UK\u8217\'20\'19s blue-chip FTSE 100 index has ended the day at a new alltime closing high.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s despite disappointment over the higher-than-expected US producer price inflation data.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 has closed at 8012 points tonight, up 14 points or 0.18%, having hit an intraday high of 8047 points this morning.\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  was the top riser, surging 5.7% after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/british-gas-owner-centrica-reports-record-profits-of-33bn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 beating profit forecasts }}}\uc2  and announcing a new \u163\'00\'A3300m share buyback programme today.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Thousands of workers at Britain\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  voted in favour of further strikes on Thursday, warning of more walkouts until a long-running dispute over pay is resolved.\par \par \uc2 More than 95% of those who participated in the ballot voted for strikes, said the \uc2 Communication Workers Union (CWU) \uc2 , which represents more than 110,000 postal workers at the centuries-old postal company.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up\u8230\'20\'26 here are today\u8217\'20\'19s main stories:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/british-gas-owner-centrica-reports-record-profits-of-33bn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Centrica boss refuses to say if he will waive bonus after \u8216\'20\'18obscene\u8217\'20\'19 \u163\'00\'A33.3bn profit }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/feb/16/uk-solicitors-warned-not-to-act-as-hired-guns-to-silence-critics-of-super-rich"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Solicitors in UK warned not to act as \u8216\'20\'18hired guns\u8217\'20\'19 to silence critics of super-rich }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/o2-virgin-mobile-costumers-bills-increase"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 O2 and Virgin Mobile customers to pay 17.3% more for calls, texts and data }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/standard-chartered-profits-top-bankers-payout"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Standard Chartered hikes top bankers\u8217\'20\'19 payouts to $1.6bn }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/aldi-to-hire-6000-people-across-uk-this-year"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Aldi to hire 6,000 people across UK this year }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/rockefeller-foundation-boss-favourite-to-succeed-david-malpass-at-world-bank"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rockefeller Foundation boss favourite to succeed David Malpass at World Bank }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/boohoo-boss-could-be-in-line-for-50m-bonus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Boohoo boss could be in line for \u163\'00\'A350m bonus }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The head of \uc2 the Rockefeller Foundation\uc2  , \uc2 Rajiv Shah\uc2  , has emerged as the favourite to succeed \uc2 David Malpass\uc2  as head of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/worldbank"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 World Bank }}}\uc2  amid calls for the White House to lose its stranglehold on choosing who should run the global development body.\par \par \uc2 Shah, a doctor, health expert and former head of the \uc2 US Agency for International Development\uc2  , is one of the names hotly tipped to be the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s administration choice as a replacement for Malpass following his announcement that he would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/15/david-malpass-world-bank-president-steps-down"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaving his post  }}}\uc2  by June.\par \par \uc2 But the likelihood that Washington will use a \u8220\'20\'1Cgentleman\u8217\'20\'19s agreement\u8221\'20\'1D under which the US picks the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  president while Europe chooses the managing director of its sister organisation \u8211\'20\'13 the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 brought immediate calls for the process to be opened up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the dismal Malpass gone, the US are already manoeuvring to appoint the new head of the Bank\u8221\'20\'1D, said Oxfam\u8217\'20\'19s head of inequality policy, \uc2 Max Lawson\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Lawson added:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis neo-colonial 80-year-old stitch up has to end if the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  is to retain any credibility with the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should back a fully transparent open recruitment process to show the world that his administration is different.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/rockefeller-foundation-boss-favourite-to-succeed-david-malpass-at-world-bank"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rockefeller Foundation boss favourite to succeed David Malpass at World Bank }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 New Look cutting more than 500 warehouse jobs in move to axe night shift\par \par \uc2 More than 500 warehouse jobs are being cut at fashion chain New Look under plans to axe the night shift at its site in Staffordshire, PA Media reports.\par \par \uc2 The move to switch to a day shift only will impact 503 of its 1,200 workers at the group\u8217\'20\'19s warehouse in Lymedale Business Park site, Newcastle-under-Lyme.\par \par \uc2 But New Look is hoping to be able to re-employ some of those employees affected as it hires an extra 300 staff on the expanded day shift.\par \par \uc2 A New Look spokesman said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith this shift, it has become increasingly clear that the processes at the distribution centre no longer suit our operational needs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTherefore, we are proposing a necessary change to working hours in the distribution centre, including the removal of the night shift. Regrettably, we expect this will result in a number of redundancies at the site.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are focusing on supporting our affected colleagues at this time and we expect to be able to offer a considerable number of these individuals new roles on the day shift.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The nasty surprise from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/feb/16/centrica-profits-british-gas-ftse-100-8000-points-inflation-growth-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-63ee32918f08826dd5d95db2#block-63ee32918f08826dd5d95db2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 today\u8217\'20\'19s US producer price inflation report }}}\uc2  means the FTSE 100 may not hit a new closing high tonight.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s currently down 6 points today at 7991, having soared to 8047 points for the first time this morning.\par \par \uc2 Rob Morgan\uc2  , chief investment analyst at \uc2 Charles Stanley\uc2  , points out that the FTSE 100 has been a laggard in recent years:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COver the past two decades, the UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 index has been a poor performer compared with most other markets. The index, representing the 100 largest stock market-listed UK companies, is a mere 25% higher than at the turn of the millennium.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAdmittedly, this represents an unflattering starting point. The index was puffed up by unsustainable valuations of \u8216\'20\'18TMT\u8217\'20\'19 stocks during the dotcom bubble in the late 1990s. Yet it has been a clear laggard against the US or emerging markets where investors have comfortably trebled their money \u8211\'20\'13 in capital growth terms alone.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere the FTSE has stood out, and consequently generated respectable returns, is dividends \u8211\'20\'13 the profits declared by companies and paid to shareholders. Reinvesting these for growth has boosted returns substantially, and the old fashioned values of seeking out sustainable and growing pay outs from shares are a large part of why the index has hit the landmark of 8000 points.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s record profits have made the front of the Evening Standard, who report that the surge in earnings has \u8216\'20\'18sparked fury\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe owner of \uc2 British Gas\uc2  has sparked fury by revealing it made record profits of more than \u163\'00\'A33.3 billion last year {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/frontpage?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #frontpage }}}\uc2  ??? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/PA9oAAaEU5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/PA9oAAaEU5 }}}\uc2  ??? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/0xgIFjHGW9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/0xgIFjHGW9 }}}\uc2  ???\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Evening Standard (@EveningStandard) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/EveningStandard/status/1626230231991476224?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 They quote Liberal Democrat Leader Ed Davey saying:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a betrayal for \uc2 British Gas\uc2  customers across the country who are struggling to keep their heating on. Once again the government\u8217\'20\'19s failure to implement a proper windfall tax is allowing oil and gas businesses to make billions off the back of hardworking families.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Worryingly, a gauge of factory activity in the Philadelphia region has moved further into negative territory.\par \par \uc2 The Philadelphia Fedreal Reserve\u8217\'20\'19s index of manufacturing activity has fallen to -24.3 this month, down from -8.9 in January,.\par \par \uc2 This is the sixth consecutive negative reading, and the lowest reading since May 2020.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe Philly Fed Manufacturing Index has moved down to -24, its lowest level since May 2020.\par \par \uc2 In the past (data since 1968), every time this indicator was at or below current levels the US economy was either in or approaching a recession. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/xXEhFwqDFh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/xXEhFwqDFh }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Charlie Bilello (@charliebilello) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/charliebilello/status/1626225734468636674?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  could cut interest rates next week, to support the economy after the devastating earthquake that struck last week.\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  has prediced that the \uc2 Central Bank of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  will lower its headline rate by a full percentage point, to 8%.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street bank has also estimated that direct costs from the destruction of physical structures in Turkey from the devastating earthquake on Feb. 6 could amount to 2.5% of growth domestic product or $25 billion.\par \par \uc2 enltrJP Morgan now expects a 100 bps cut in Turkey to 8%. Erdoganomics must go. Elections coming up aswell. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/WEY4dXvs6O"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/WEY4dXvs6O }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sir Of Finance (@SirOfFinance) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SirOfFinance/status/1626188641151205377?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 European Union\uc2  countries are said to be \u8220\'20\'1Con good track\u8221\'20\'1D to adopt new sanctions against Moscow in time for the first anniversary of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine on 24th February.\par \par \uc2 Diplomats in Brussels have told Reuters that talks are underway between \uc2 EU\uc2  members over proposed new sanctions estimated to be worth some 11 billion euros ($11.8 bln) in trade flows.\par \par \uc2 One EU diplomat familiar with the discussions said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe package should be concluded well in time for February 24th, it\u8217\'20\'19s on good track, there are no major sticking points.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Switzerland, meanwhile, has ruled out confiscating private Russian assets to pay for the reconstruction of Ukraine, saying it would undermine the Swiss constitution.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss Justice Ministry said on Wednesday that:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe confiscation of frozen private assets is inconsistent with the Federal Constitution and the prevailing legal order and violates Switzerland\u8217\'20\'19s international commitments.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Switzerland has frozen financial assets worth around 7.5 billion Swiss francs (\u163\'00\'A36.7bn) under sanctions against Russians.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bond prices are also weakening, pushing up the yield (or interest rate) on \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  bills.\par \par \uc2 enltrSo much for the markets focusing more on the labor market than inflation. Inflation data, as witnessed by this week\u8217\'20\'19s CPI and PPI reports, are still very much driving yields higher. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Aj65RgvgOm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Aj65RgvgOm }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kathy Jones (@KathyJones) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KathyJones/status/1626219658780221447?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 UK government\uc2  bond yields are also higher, another sign that the markets are disappointed that inflation pressures are higher than hoped.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Stock markets are taking a knock from the strong US PPI inflation report just released.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 has sunk back from its earlier record highs, and is now back below the 8,000-point mark which it cleared for the first time yesterday.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US producer prices inflation higher than expected\par \par \uc2 US goods and services producers hiked their prices by more than expected last month, dampening hopes that inflation is easing.\par \par \uc2 The Producer Price Index for final demand rose by 0.7% in January, the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  has reported.\par \par \uc2 On an anual basis, producer prices jumped by 6% in the year to January. Economists had expected it would fall to 5.4%, from 6.5% in the year to December.\par \par \uc2 Goods prices rose by 1.2% in Janary alone, which is the largest monthly increase since last June.\par \par \uc2 This was driven by higher energy costs, with prices for gasoline increasing by 6,2% in the month.\par \par \uc2 enltrUS PPI YoY% w/ Contributions -- In short, it's not decelerating as fast as people hoped: \{ECAN\} {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/nj9J4Q5xNj"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/nj9J4Q5xNj }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Michael McDonough\uc2  (@M_McDonough) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/M_McDonough/status/1626214183888109568?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\uc2  (@M_McDonough)\par \par \uc2 This is a sign that US inflation may be stickier than hoped, which may push the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates still higher, and hold them their for longer than expected. Especially with the US jobs market still strong last week.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is rallying in response, pushing the pound down below $1.20.\par \par \uc2 enltrnot as much disinflation in US then thought perhaps and labour market still strong {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/USD?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #USD }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Michael Hewson ???? (@mhewson_CMC) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mhewson_CMC/status/1626213210520002561?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Key event\par \par \uc2 Just in: the number of Americans filing new unemployment claims has dropped, suggesting the jobs market is still strong.\par \par \uc2 There were 194,00o fresh \u8216\'20\'18initial claims\u8217\'20\'19 for jobless support last week, down from 195,000 the previous week, and lower than expected \u8211\'20\'13 suggesting firms are holding onto workers.\par \par \uc2 enltrInitial jobless claims (194k) came in 6k lower than 200k est and down 1k from last week. Continuing claims were up 16k to 1,696k at (1,695k est). {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/jobs?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #jobs }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/unemployment?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #unemployment }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/fed?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #fed }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/recession?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #recession }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Cqh9Q3vUSc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Cqh9Q3vUSc }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Macro84 (@macro84) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/macro84/status/1626214429275881472?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 These are low figures in historic terms, which may indicate that the increase in US interest rates has not yet cooled the labour market.\par \par \uc2 enltr???? *US JOBLESS CLAIMS 194,000 IN FEB. 11 WEEK; EST. 200K - BBG\par \par \uc2 *US CONTINUING CLAIMS ROSE TO 1,696K LAST WEEK; EST. 1,695K\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Christophe Barraud???? (@C_Barraud) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/C_Barraud/status/1626213196217581569?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Millions of consumers with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/o2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 O2 }}}\uc2  and Virgin Mobile contracts are to be hit with an inflation-busting 17.3% increase in their bills for making calls, sending texts and using data, adding to pressure on households amid the cost of living crisis.\par \par \uc2 The price hike is the latest in a series of big increases imposed on consumers for vital utilities, and will add to pressure on the government to step in with tougher protections.\par \par \uc2 The telecoms operators \u8211\'20\'13 merged under the same umbrella company since 2021 \u8211\'20\'13 confirmed the cost of their airtime contracts, or what customers pay for calls, texts and data, would go up by the annual rate of retail prices index (RPI) inflation announced this week, plus 3.9 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Official figures on Wednesday showed RPI stood at 13.4% in January, landing consumers with a 17.3% increase in their airtime bills in total \u8211\'20\'13 almost triple the rate of growth for average workers\u8217\'20\'19 pay. Consumers will see the increase from April.\par \par \uc2 More {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/o2-virgin-mobile-costumers-bills-increase?CMP=Share_iOSApp_Other"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here }}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/o2-virgin-mobile-costumers-bills-increase"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 O2 and Virgin Mobile customers to pay 17.3% more for calls, texts and data }}}\par \par \uc2 enltrNew: \uc2 O2\uc2  and Virgin Mobile customers to pay inflation-busting 17.3% more for calls, texts and data\par \par \uc2 It comes as \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  has pledged to halve inflation, and is insisting public sector workers take real-terms cuts.\par \par \uc2 Govt under pressure to act on telcos\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/GWHVz9TqLR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/GWHVz9TqLR }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Richard Partington (@RJPartington) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RJPartington/status/1626200295117053952?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 France\u8217\'20\'19s stock market is also celebrating a record high today.\par \par \uc2 France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC 40 index broke over its previous lifetime high this session, hitting 7,387.29 points.\par \par \uc2 enltrFrance's CAC 40 index briefly hits record high as European stocks continue to sizzle\par \par \uc2 The CAC 40 is up by around 1% so far today {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Snoi9Lr4If"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Snoi9Lr4If }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 AnirudhSethi-Chart Surgeon (@CHARTISKING) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CHARTISKING/status/1626185028354048001?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 Hopes that Europe\u8217\'20\'19s economy will avoid a recession have boosted stocks in Paris, as have Beijing\u8217\'20\'19s push to reopen China\u8217\'20\'19s economy\par \par \uc2 Reuters explains:\par \par \uc2 French stocks have risen nearly 14% so far this year, following a battering in 2022, boosted by hopes that the euro zone will narrowly avoid a recession and surging shares of luxury goods groups that rely heavily on Chinese shoppers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFrance has particularly leveraged the faster-than-expected Chinese reopening,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andrea Cicione, head of research at TS Lombard.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fact that there is significant chunk of consumer discretionary in there, particularly luxury goods as opposed to Germany for example, where consumer discretionary is mostly autos, is definitely a positive for France.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 The boss of \uc2 Centrica\uc2  is refusing to say if he will waive his bonus for the past year, after the \uc2 British Gas\uc2  owner tripled its profits this morning.\par \par \uc2 Chris O\u8217\'20\'19Shea\uc2  , chief executive of \uc2 Centrica\uc2  , could be in line for a personal financial windfall after the company beat forecasts with profits of \u163\'00\'A33.3bn last year.\par \par \uc2 However, he said on Thursday that it is \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo early to have a conversation\u8221\'20\'1D about his potential bonus payout despite pressure from campaigners to reject it.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe boss of \uc2 British Gas\uc2  owner \uc2 Centrica\uc2  , \uc2 Chris O'Shea\uc2  , have refused to rule out taking an annual bonus that could see him net \u163\'00\'A31.6million.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Graham Hiscott (@Grahamhiscott) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Grahamhiscott/status/1626132679748997121?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 Mr O\u8217\'20\'19Shea told reporters:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COn the bonus, we\u8217\'20\'19ve been a bit more efficient this year, and we\u8217\'20\'19re a bit earlier in the process.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLast year we reported a little bit later. It\u8217\'20\'19s a bit early for us to say \u8211\'20\'13 the annual report will be published in March and it will have everything that you need.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrThree valiant attempts from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NathalieThomas3?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @NathalieThomas3 }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MrAlexLawson?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @MrAlexLawson }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emilygosden?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @emilygosden }}}\uc2  to press Chris O\u8217\'20\'19Shea Centrica boss on whether he\u8217\'20\'19ll take a bonus this year.\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Shea bats away every time saying \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s too early and have to wait for annual report\u8221\'20\'1D. Hmmmm\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ashley Armstrong (@AArmstrong_says) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AArmstrong_says/status/1626137015543554048?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 Given the shocking revelations this month that agents working for \uc2 British Gas\uc2  had ignored customers\u8217\'20\'19 vulnerabilities and broken into homes to fit pre-payment gas meters, there will surely be an outcry if O\u8217\'20\'19Shea pockets a bonus for 2022.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/01/british-gas-halts-use-of-warrants-to-force-people-on-to-prepayment-meters-allegations-vulnerable-customers-break-in"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 British Gas halts use of warrants to force people on to prepayment meters }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 Mike Ashley\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s sports empire could drop out of the FTSE 100 when the index is next reshuffled, in March.\par \par \uc2 Frasers Group\uc2  only rejoined the blue-chip index in September ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/sep/09/mike-ashley-retail-empire-ftse-100-return-frasers-group"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 when the takeover of defence firm Meggitt created a vacancy }}}\uc2  ). It is currently the lowest-valued company on the FTSE 100, at around \u163\'00\'A33.7bn, so could be dislodged when index provider FTSE Russell recalculates which companies should be in the index.\par \par \uc2 Investec\uc2  , the most valuable member of the FTSE 250 index of medium-sized firms, has a market capitalisation of \u163\'00\'A35.17bn, enough to propel it into the blue-chip FTSE 100.\par \par \uc2 FTSE Russell will conduct the review after the market closes on 28th February, with the reshuffle taking place on 20th March.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.proactiveinvestors.co.uk/companies/news/1006268/mike-ashley-s-frasers-on-brink-of-ftse-100-relegation-in-quarterly-reshuffle-1006268.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Proactive Investors reported yesterday }}}\uc2  that \uc2 Frasers\uc2  is on the brink of a drop back to the FTSE 250 index, saying:\par \par \uc2 In order to avoid companies yo-yo-ing between the two indices, a company must be able to climb into the top 90 by market cap to be promoted into the FTSE 100. Likewise, to be demoted, it must fall below the 110th position in terms of market cap.\par \par \uc2 Based on current market caps, \uc2 Frasers\uc2  is on the brink of the drop, with its \u163\'00\'A33.6bn market cap putting it in 109th position.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 Ed Miliband\uc2  MP, Labour\u8217\'20\'19s Shadow Climate Change and Net Zero Secretary, has issued a statement on \uc2 Centrica\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s soaring profits:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt cannot be right that, as oil and gas giants rake in the windfalls of war, the \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s Conservatives refuse to implement a proper windfall tax that would make them pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a matter of weeks, the Government plans to allow the energy price cap to rise to \u163\'00\'A33,000. Labour would use a proper windfall tax to stop prices going up in April.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen it comes to oil and gas interests, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  is too weak to stand up for the British people. Only Labour is on your side - with a plan to tackle the cost of living crisis now, and a long term plan to cut bills for good and make Britain a clean energy superpower.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 Russian central bank\uc2  not planning to ease capital controls soon - governor\par \par \uc2 Over in Moscow, Russia\u8217\'20\'19s top central banker has dashed hopes that capital controls could be lifted soon.\par \par \uc2 Capital controls were introduced almost a year ago, including curbs on foreign currency withdrawals. They were brought in after the full-scale invasion of Ukraine sent the rouble plunging, helping the currency to recover.\par \par \uc2 Reuters has the details:\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  does not yet see an opportunity to significantly ease capital controls, Governor \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2  told reporters on Thursday, stating that it could extend restrictions on foreign currency withdrawals in March.\par \par \uc2 Nabiullina said the controls that have been left in place are important for financial stability. Russia introduced capital controls to stabilise the FX market shortly after Moscow sent troops into Ukraine a year ago and the rouble tumbled to a record low.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK consumer spending weakened last week, the latest realtime economic data shows.\par \par \uc2 But, takings at \uc2 Pret A Manger\uc2  rose, as workers returned to the office following rail strikes the previous week.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  has reported that aggregate UK spending on debit and credit cards is estimated to have dropped by 3 percentage points last week. Younger shoppers cut back the most; debit card transactions by those aged 18-34 fell by 12 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 But, the number of in-store transactions at all \uc2 Pret A Manger\uc2  locations increased in the week to 9 February 2023, apart from in \u8220\'20\'1CLondon Suburban\u8221\'20\'1D where they were broadly unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Pret takings are a proxy for consumer spending and high street visits.\par \par \uc2 The ONS says:\par \par \uc2 This latest recovery in transactions may be partially because of the rail strikes in the previous week. Manchester stores saw the largest increase of 12 percentage points compared with the previous week, followed by Regional Stations where they increased by 9 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 enltrIn the week ending 9 Feb 2023, transactions at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Pret?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Pret }}}\uc2  stores increased in all locations except "London Suburban".\par \par \uc2 The largest increases were in Manchester stores and regional stations.\par \par \uc2 This may be partially because of the rail strikes the previous week. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4IiT2O5Mfo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/4IiT2O5Mfo }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1626152937184526336?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS)\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  has beaten City forecasts with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/feb/16/centrica-profits-british-gas-ftse-100-8000-points-inflation-growth-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-63edd5848f08826dd5d95a2b#block-63edd5848f08826dd5d95a2b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  its 2022 earnings this morning }}}\uc2 , reports Keith Bowman, investment analyst at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ii.co.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interactive investor }}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Bowman explains:\par \par \uc2 Higher energy prices following the war in Ukraine helped push upstream adjusted operating profit to \u163\'00\'A31.79 billion from last year\u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A3663 million. Profit on the same basis for its \uc2 British Gas\uc2  supply business fell 39% to \u163\'00\'A372 million, while profit at its Irish supply business rose 11% to \u163\'00\'A331 million. Hindered by the loss of customer numbers as consumers look to reduce outgoings, its boiler and other services business saw an adjusted loss of \u163\'00\'A39 million.\par \par \uc2 A final dividend of 3p per share follows the reinstatement of shareholder payouts at the half year results in July, and leaves the shares sat on an historic yield of around 4%. \uc2 Centrica\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s existing \u163\'00\'A3250 million share buyback scheme is being extended by a further \u163\'00\'A3300 million.\par \par \uc2 In all, and as with business in general, elevated inflation is raising costs, Bowman adds:\par \par \uc2 The weather and its unseasonal swings also cause uncertainty over customer energy demand, while a lot is already in the price after outperforming the FTSE 100 by 20%-plus over the last year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 enltrBritish Gas owner \uc2 Centrica\uc2  posts record profits of \u163\'00\'A33.3bn - beating the \u163\'00\'A32.7bn notched up in 2012.\par \par \uc2 - Plans to pay out \u163\'00\'A3200m to investors and extra \u163\'00\'A3300m in share buybacks.\par \par \uc2 - Says it was "extremely disappointed" by debt agents on prepayment meters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/GlGAbMskFG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/GlGAbMskFG }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Alex Lawson (@MrAlexLawson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MrAlexLawson/status/1626119063536648192?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Ed Miliband\uc2  , Shadow Climate and Net Zero Secretary, on \uc2 Centrica\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s profit surge:\par \par \uc2 enltrIt cannot be right that, as oil and gas giants rake in the windfalls of war, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  's Conservatives refuse to implement a proper windfall tax that would make them pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 Labour would use a real windfall tax to stop the energy price cap going up in April. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/RyJrki0EYs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/RyJrki0EYs }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Ed Miliband\uc2  (@Ed_Miliband) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Ed_Miliband/status/1626119312292429826?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\uc2  (@Ed_Miliband)\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 Shares in Standard Chartered are now up 3.5%, after the bank upgraded its forecasts and posted a 28% rise in profits for last year.\par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  said the reopening of China after the pandemic is giving grounds for optimism, after it made a statutory pre-tax profit of $4.3bn (\u163\'00\'A33.56bn) for last year.\par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  , which is focused on the Asia-Pacific region, told shareholders that the pace of economic recovery in many of its \u8220\'20\'1Cfootprint markets\u8221\'20\'1D was encouraging.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe recent opening-up of China and the generally receding impacts of COVID-19 should help,\u8221\'20\'1D it added.\par \par \uc2 It also saw its net interest margin \u8211\'20\'13 the difference between what a bank charges for loans and pays for savings \u8211\'20\'13 increase by 0.2 percentage points as it benefited from a higher interest rate environment.\par \par \uc2 CEO \uc2 Bill Winters\uc2  says the bank is upgrading its expectations, and now targeting \u8220\'20\'1Ca return on tangible equity approaching 10% in 2023, to exceed 11% in 2024, and to continue to grow thereafter\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 The scandal-hit owner of British {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/gas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gas }}}\uc2  has reported record profits of \u163\'00\'A33.3bn boosted by soaring wholesale gas prices after Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine and as many households in Britain struggle with the cost of living, our energy correspondent Alex Lawson reports .\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s bumper profits are likely to anger campaigners calling for tougher windfall taxes, lower bills and better treatment of vulnerable customers against the backdrop of the prepayment meter scandal.\par \par \uc2 The company\u8217\'20\'19s profits for 2022 more than tripled compared with the \u163\'00\'A3948m in 2021, aided by soaring profits in its North Sea oil and gas division. They also surpass the company\u8217\'20\'19s previous profit high of \u163\'00\'A32.7bn, recorded in 2012.\par \par \uc2 British Gas\uc2  faced widespread criticism earlier this month when it emerged that debt agents working for Britain\u8217\'20\'19s largest energy supplier {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/01/british-gas-halts-use-of-warrants-to-force-people-on-to-prepayment-meters-allegations-vulnerable-customers-break-in"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had ignored customers\u8217\'20\'19 vulnerabilities }}}\uc2  and forced them on to prepayment meters to recover debts.\par \par \uc2 The company suspended the use of court warrants to install prepayment meters and the government and Great Britain\u8217\'20\'19s energy regulator, \uc2 Ofgem\uc2  , later ordered all energy suppliers to pause the tactic.\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s North Sea profits are subject to a windfall tax on North Sea oil and gas operators while it also has a 20% stake in Britain\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear power stations, which are subject to the electricity generator levy implemented by the chancellor, \uc2 Jeremy Hunt\uc2  , to capture windfall gains.\par \par \uc2 However, Labour has called for the oil and gas windfall tax to be expanded to capture a greater proportion of profits.\par \par \uc2 More {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/british-gas-owner-centrica-reports-record-profits-of-33bn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here }}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/16/british-gas-owner-centrica-reports-record-profits-of-33bn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 British Gas owner Centrica reports record profits of \u163\'00\'A33.3bn }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 Windfall tax calls will grow louder following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/feb/16/centrica-profits-british-gas-ftse-100-8000-points-inflation-growth-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-63edd5848f08826dd5d95a2b#block-63edd5848f08826dd5d95a2b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Centrica tripling its profits to \u163\'00\'A33.3bn last year }}}\uc2 , even though the company paid nearly \u163\'00\'A31bn, predicts Tom Gilbey, equity research analyst at investment management firm \uc2 Quilter Cheviot\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Gilbey say:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Centrica\uc2  produced some strong results this morning as its trading division took advantage of energy trends that came to dominate in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Its adjusted profit grew over 250% to over \u163\'00\'A33bn , and it more than doubled its free cash flow, putting this to use by paying a dividend \u8211\'20\'13 which they only reinstated a year ago - and getting approval to buy back a potential 10% of their shares. It is this that may draw controversy given it is also benefiting from elevated energy prices and it has come under fire in recent weeks for its consumer practices.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe business still expects conditions to remain tough for customers and stressed it will try and support them, pointing to the fact it recruited 700 additional people over 2022 to help.\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, \uc2 Centrica\uc2  paid around \u163\'00\'A31bn in tax, perhaps putting the larger energy producing giants to shame. However, with these profits quite so extraordinary for \uc2 Centrica\uc2  , calls are only going to grow for stricter windfall taxes, particularly in the run up to the general election next year. While the results are strong, it is a reminder that \uc2 Centrica\uc2  faces a number of headwinds, some of its own making but some out of their control.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 hits new record over 8,000 points as \uc2 Centrica\uc2  shares jump\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Centrica\uc2  have jumped by 6% at the start of trading in London.\par \par \uc2 Investors are cheering its new \u163\'00\'A3300m share buyback plan announced this morning, on top of the tripling of its profits last year.\par \par \uc2 This has helped drive the FTSE 100 index to a fresh alltime high. It\u8217\'20\'19s gained around 48 points or 0.6% to 8046 points, beating yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s record high.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 share index Photograph: Refinitiv\par \par \uc2 Asia-Pacific focused bank Standard Chartered are also rallying, up 1.7% after reporting a jump in profits and a new $1bn share buyback programme.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/feb/16/centrica-profits-british-gas-ftse-100-8000-points-inflation-growth-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-63edccbc8f0892f0cefe5e9c#block-63edccbc8f0892f0cefe5e9c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As flagged in the introduction }}}\uc2 , the FTSE 100\u8217\'20\'19s surge to record highs reflects hopes that the world economy may avoid recession, rather than optimism specifically about the UK economy.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Ryan, head of market strategy at global financial services firm Ebury, explains:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith more than 80% of FTSE 100 revenues earned abroad, we see the move as more of a consequence of growing optimism surrounding the easing in inflation rates and risk of recessions globally, rather than necessarily an improvement in sentiment towards the domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe harsh reality is that with UK inflation still stubbornly high, a recession remains very much on the cards, and that does not present a particularly encouraging environment for British stocks. Indeed, the FTSE 250 index, which has a greater weighting towards British firms, is still trading around 17% off its 2021 highs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe recent drop in the value of sterling, which has fallen by more than 2% on the dollar so far this month, can also partly explain the boom, as this boosts the value of earnings from overseas.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.58am GMT\par \par \uc2 Aldi\uc2  to hire 6,000 UK staff\par \par \uc2 The British arm of \uc2 Aldi\uc2  , the German discount supermarket chain, has announced plans to hire more than 6,000 workers in the UK, as part of its expansion plans.\par \par \uc2 Aldi\uc2  said new stores were planned in Norwich and Newcastle in England, and that it was also recruiting for 450 jobs across its 11 regional distribution centres.\par \par \uc2 Giles Hurley\uc2  , chief executive officer of Aldi UK, said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDemand for \uc2 Aldi\uc2  has never been higher as more and more people realise they can make significant savings on every shop without compromising on quality. It\u8217\'20\'19s more important than ever that we are making it even easier for more people to shop with us \u8211\'20\'13 including by opening dozens of new stores.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur success is dependent on the amazing work that colleagues do, day in and day out, and we\u8217\'20\'19re looking forward to welcoming thousands more colleagues to Team \uc2 Aldi\uc2  throughout 2023.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Aldi\uc2  currently has more than 990 stores and employs around 40,000 people.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.grocerygazette.co.uk/2022/12/06/lidl-market-share/#:~:text=Lidl%20has%20achieved%20a%20new,to%20new%20data%20by%20Kantar."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Market research firm Kantar has reported }}}\uc2  that \uc2 Aldi\uc2  , and rival discount chain \uc2 Lidl\uc2  , have both seen sales growth, boosting their market share, as households have tried to control their shopping bills.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  has announced a new \u163\'00\'A3300m share buyback this morning.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s on top of its existing \u163\'00\'A3250m share buyback programme.\par \par \uc2 Share buybacks are a way of returning cash to shareholders (and to pump up a company\u8217\'20\'19s stock price). Critics say they simply funnel money from customers, who may be strugging in the cost of living crisis, to wealthier investors.\par \par \uc2 Dr George Dibb, head of the Centre for Economic Justice at \uc2 IPPR\uc2  , says the UK should tax such buybacks.\par \par \uc2 \u8221\'20\'1DThese scandalous profits are undeserved and come directly from the pocket of bill-payers. We all know that wholesale energy prices have been sky-high for the past year, but that\u8217\'20\'19s no reason that gas suppliers should be making higher profits on the back of higher bills.\par \par \uc2 These profits, which are then being transferred directly to shareholders via buybacks and dividends, are a direct transfer away from bill-payers during a cost of living crisis. It is time to introduce a tax on share buybacks and use those revenues to support public services.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the US, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  pushed through a 1% tax on corporate stock buybacks. Last week, the US president called for it to be quadrupled, in his State of the Union speech.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 Scope, the disability equality charity, are calling for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scope.org.uk/campaigns/cost-of-living-crisis/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a social energy tariff }}}\uc2  to help disabled people through the cost of living crisis.\par \par \uc2 Tom Marsland, policy manager at Scope, said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s obscene that energy companies continue to make massive profits as disabled people face devastating situations because they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford enough energy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLife costs a lot more when you\u8217\'20\'19re disabled. We\u8217\'20\'19re being inundated with heart-breaking calls from disabled people who haven\u8217\'20\'19t eaten for days, who can\u8217\'20\'19t afford energy to charge wheelchairs and stairlifts, but are still racking up huge energy debts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen, many have been forced onto prepayment meters as a result, putting lives and health in danger.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEnergy companies need to start putting disabled customers first.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need a social energy tariff - a discounted rate - for disabled people, to put an end to sky-high energy bills.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Scope and \uc2 Age UK\uc2  have drawn up an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tracking.scope.org.uk/tracking/click?d=DHrEqlUm0TbtsvDKPQFJ1n9RGxpPiqdajl0mattSYSWrCeoPX9JZu4Sx8t-Qly-qYEXOI8QJ8csvIh06ScapZp6nltJI3JE2iqtgV8nk1uNdfuRNk5x6WfZqGk4NW_orJSIS-G9dYoftQq2Iptj5YVNtWsiPfkWL2wRbhGLROA2czY97dFKawKSmyBkwqW3A6Hv-8O4es1KtvOos892Zh9nwVgalOVXcS2CPBBg9az0KiQyAdLcZ0jTC6xLSO6Ft9XEA35knY8AML5IEnS89pE3mphnVaKfk4cySD4BAvU1Z0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 open letter }}}\uc2  asking the government to introduce a social tariff, which can be signed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tracking.scope.org.uk/tracking/click?d=DHrEqlUm0TbtsvDKPQFJ1n9RGxpPiqdajl0mattSYSWrCeoPX9JZu4Sx8t-Qly-qYEXOI8QJ8csvIh06ScapZp6nltJI3JE2iqtgV8nk1uNdfuRNk5x6WfZqGk4NW_orJSIS-G9dYoftQq2Iptj5YVNtWsiPfkWL2wRbhGLROA2czY97dFKawKSmyBkwqW3A6Hv-8O4es1KtvOos892Zh9nwVgalOVXcS2CPBBg9az0KiQyAdLcZ0jTC6xLSO6Ft9XEA35knY8AML5IEnS89pE3mphnVaKfk4cySD4BAvU1Z0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 Friends of the Earth are also calling for a tougher levy on energy company profits.\par \par \uc2 Sana Yusuf, climate campaigner at \uc2 Friends of the Earth\uc2  , says \uc2 Centrica\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s earnings will fuel \u8216\'20\'18further outrage\u8217\'20\'19, given the cost of living crisis:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnother set of bumper profits from one of the companies fuelling the energy and climate crises will no doubt spark further outrage as millions of people struggle to pay their bills and face a drop in government support from April.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe new Energy Security and Net Zero Secretary [ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/department-for-energy-security-and-net-zero"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Grant Shapps }}}\uc2  ] needs to step up and back growing calls for a tougher windfall tax on the excessive profits of fossil fuel companies like \uc2 Centrica\uc2  to help fund the investment in insulation and homegrown renewables needed to bring down bills and cut emissions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 Unite\uc2  : Obscene Centrica profits confirm need for tougher energy windfall tax\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Cobscene\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/feb/16/centrica-profits-british-gas-ftse-100-8000-points-inflation-growth-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-63edd5848f08826dd5d95a2b#block-63edd5848f08826dd5d95a2b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  profits reported by Centrica this morning }}}\uc2  show that the government should bring in a meaningful windfall tax on the energy industry, says the \uc2 Unite union\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Unite\uc2  general secretary Sharon Graham says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 British Gas\uc2  owner \uc2 Centrica\uc2  has been coining it in from our massive energy bills while sending bailiffs to prey on vulnerable consumers the length and breadth of the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese energy companies are showing us everything that is wrong with the UK\u8217\'20\'19s broken economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  should get a grip \u8211\'20\'13 pull the plug on rampaging energy profiteering, impose a meaningful, tough windfall tax and give the \uc2 NHS\uc2  a pay rise with the proceeds.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  says today it paid nearly \u163\'00\'A31bn in tax related to its 2022 profits.\par \par \uc2 The current windfall tax, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/may/26/sunak-u-turns-on-energy-profits-levy-in-15bn-cost-of-living-package"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 or \u8216\'20\'18energy profits levy\u8217\'20\'19 }}}\uc2 , was introduced last May and then {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/nils-pratley-on-finance/2022/nov/17/jeremy-hunt-windfall-tax-on-energy-leaves-several-billion-of-the-table"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased in November }}}\uc2 . But critics say it is flawed, because it allows energy producers to offset increased development in the North See off their tax bill.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/nils-pratley-on-finance/2022/nov/17/jeremy-hunt-windfall-tax-on-energy-leaves-several-billion-of-the-table"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hunt\u8217\'20\'19s windfall tax on energy leaves several billion of the table | Nils Pratley }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 British Gas\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s adjusted operating profit decreased by 39% to \u163\'00\'A372m in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  says this \u8220\'20\'1Clargely reflects voluntary donations made to support customers and the repayment of furlough funds received by the Group in 2020\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In August, \uc2 British Gas\uc2  announced it would donate 10% of its profits to help its poorer customers.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2022/aug/25/british-gas-to-donate-10-of-profits-to-struggling-customers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 British Gas to donate 10% of profits to struggling customers }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  profits triple to over \u163\'00\'A33bn\par \par \uc2 The owner of \uc2 British Gas\uc2  has reported a surge in annual profits this morning, becoming the latest energy giant to benefit from the jump in energy prices since the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  made total operating profits of \u163\'00\'A33.3bn, a record, and more than three times as much as the \u163\'00\'A3948m adjusted profits it made in 2021.\par \par \uc2 The jump in earnings comes just weeks after \uc2 British Gas\uc2  suspended the forced installation of prepayment meters due to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/02/watchdog-urges-uk-energy-suppliers-to-use-only-accredited-bailiffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 concerns over its treatment of vulnerable customers }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Centrica\uc2  says it benefitted from \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong gas production and electricity generation against a backdrop of higher commodity prices\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The company operates oil and gas production in the North Sea, plus holds interests in Britain\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear power plants and operates an energy trading business. These divisions, rather thatn \uc2 British Gas\uc2  , provided the bulk of \uc2 Centrica\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s profits.\par \par \uc2 Such high profits, when the energy crisis is fuelling the cost of living crisis, will intensify calls for tougher windfall taxes on the industry, with \uc2 BP\uc2  and \uc2 Shell\uc2  have also reported record profits for 2022.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/feb/09/profits-energy-fossil-fuel-resurgence-climate-crisis-shell-exxon-bp-chevron-totalenergies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Monster profits\u8217\'20\'19 for energy giants reveal a self-destructive fossil fuel resurgence }}}\par \par \uc2 Chris O\u8217\'20\'19Shea\uc2  , \uc2 Centrica\uc2  chief executive, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur performance in 2022 demonstrates the benefits of our balanced portfolio and our strong balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 The energy crisis and cost of living pressures have created a challenging environment for customers and communities, but we have been able to provide much needed stability and support.\par \par \uc2 We invested \u163\'00\'A375m in supporting our energy customers in 2022, which was greater than the \u163\'00\'A38 post-tax profit per customer earned by \uc2 British Gas Energy\uc2  . Whilst customers may see some relief given recent easing of prices, it remains clear that some will continue to need help and we will do what we can to support them in the year ahead.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrCentrica has published its 2022 preliminary results today.\par \par \uc2 Read more ? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/gKwRkhFXk2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/gKwRkhFXk2 }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/pURFVxnaVM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/pURFVxnaVM }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Centrica plc\uc2  (@centricaplc) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/centricaplc/status/1626115693992181761?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\uc2  (@centricaplc)\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: FTSE 100 to power over 8,000 points\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s blue-chip share index is set to hit new record highs today, after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/feb/15/ftse-100-hits-8000-points-recession-fears-ease"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hitting the 8,000 point mark for the first time on Wednesday }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 hit 8,003.65 points yesterday, before closing just below the 8k milestone at 7997.83 (a new closing high), as hopes grow that inflation may have peaked.\par \par \uc2 Shares are set to keep rising today, as global stock markets continue to be cheered by optimism that central banks may ease their interest rate increases soon.\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Thursday?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Thursday }}}\uc2  mks: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/FTSE100?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #FTSE100 }}}\uc2  now almost in bull mkt with another record high. CAC40 0.5% away from a record. Europe expected up on strong earnings from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/STAN?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #STAN }}}\uc2  now almost in bull mkt with another record high. CAC40 0.5% away from a record. Europe expected up on strong earnings from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/CBK?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #CBK }}}\uc2  now almost in bull mkt with another record high. CAC40 0.5% away from a record. Europe expected up on strong earnings from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/NESN?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #NESN }}}\uc2  now almost in bull mkt with another record high. CAC40 0.5% away from a record. Europe expected up on strong earnings from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/AIR?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #AIR }}}\uc2 . Meanwhile {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/CSCO?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #CSCO }}}\uc2  up 10% after good earnings and outlook. Record trade deficit in Japan. Gold 5wk lows {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/jpODQeSya0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/jpODQeSya0 }}}\uc2  up 10% after good earnings and outlook. Record trade deficit in Japan. Gold 5wk lows\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jeremy Naylor (@JeremyNaylor_IG) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JeremyNaylor_IG/status/1626112266599817216?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 enltrIndices Update: As of 05:00, these are your best and worst performers based on the London trading schedule:\par \par \uc2 Germany 40: 0.38%\par \par \uc2 France 40: 0.36%\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100: 0.27%\par \par \uc2 US 500: 0.16%\par \par \uc2 Wall Street: 0.04%\par \par \uc2 View the performance of all markets via {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/2NUaqnUPED"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/2NUaqnUPED }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/gEz7VFLaXf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/gEz7VFLaXf }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 DailyFX Team Live (@DailyFXTeam) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DailyFXTeam/status/1626086471932280834?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 16, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 Jason Hollands, managing director of online investment service {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.google.com/aclk?sa=l&ai=DChcSEwi75b_Wupn9AhWH3-0KHfaeDLgYABAAGgJkZw&sig=AOD64_0QZqzQESiVB9Zeztq-XLifgPGLfQ&q&adurl&ved=2ahUKEwi6_bjWupn9AhUKesAKHSSUBGsQ0Qx6BAgHEAE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bestinvest }}}\uc2  , says the UK market is increasingly seen as a bargain by international investors.\par \par \uc2 A number of large investment banks are taking a positive view on the opportunity, Hollands says, while some private investors have been \u8220\'20\'1Cheavy sellers of UK equity funds for several months\u8221\'20\'1D, probably ground down by relentless gloomy news on the domestic economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 However, the FTSE 100 is not a barometer of the UK domestic economy, given its dominance by multinationals such as banks, mining companies and oil giants.\par \par \uc2 Hollands explains:\par \par \uc2 It is a highly international index, which makes around 79% of its revenues overseas. This includes around 13% of revenues earned in China, and so these companies should also be a beneficiary of the expected rebound in the Chinese economy this year following its ditching of draconian COVID restrictions in December.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn recent years, many investors have dismissed UK blue chip shares as \u8216\'20\'18boring\u8217\'20\'19, lacking exposure to exciting sectors like technology and social media. But in a more trying economic environment, solid companies churning out reliable dividends are well worth considering. Boring is the new sexy. With an abundance of exposure to energy, commodities, consumer staples and healthcare companies, the FTSE 100 looks well placed for the current environment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since the start of January, the FTSE 100 index has gained 7% \u8211\'20\'13 which wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be a bad return for a full year.\par \par \uc2 Other European markets have made an even more sparkling start, with Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX and France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC having gained around 12%.\par \par \uc2 enltrHow much higher can the FTSE 100 go? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JeremyNaylor_IG?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @JeremyNaylor_IG }}}\uc2  takes a look at the details and where we could be heading {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/c7uCy5UbS8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/c7uCy5UbS8 }}}\uc2  takes a look at the details and where we could be heading\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 IG (@IGcom) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/IGcom/status/1625966428548464641?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 15, 2023 }}}\par \par \uc2 We get new US producer prices data and jobless claims figures later today, which may move the markets \u8211\'20\'13 if they change investors\u8217\'20\'19 views of the path of inflation and interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am GMT: ECB Economic Bulletin\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am GMT: UK business insights and economic activity data\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm GMT: US PPI index of producer prices\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm GMT: US weekly jobless figures\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 5pm GMT: \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  gives a fireside chat at the Warwick Think Tank on the UK economy\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230216ej2g0010b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
