\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in Coventry to go on strike for seven more days\b0\par\par\uc2 Jasper Jolly \par \uc2 497 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 February 2023\par 15:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 310 staff to take part in stoppages after becoming first UK employees of online retailer to strike\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers at a warehouse in Coventry have announced seven more days of strike action as they continue their push for better pay.\par \par \uc2 More than 310 staff at a giant fulfilment centre in the West Midlands city will strike on 28 February, 2 March and from 13 to 17 March, according to the \uc2 GMB union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The workers became the first ever employees of the online retailer in the UK to take strike action on 25 January. They are asking for higher pay and have also complained of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jan/22/first-industrial-action-at-amazon-uk-hopes-to-strike-at-firms-union-hostility"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overbearing management practices and long hours}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The strike threat came as workers across the UK economy face a steep fall in real-terms pay because of high inflation. However, it also follows an announcement last month by \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, \uc2 Andrew Jassy\uc2 , that the company is to cut {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jan/05/amazon-to-axe-18000-jobs-citing-economic-uncertainty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 18,000 jobs globally}}}\uc2  in response to slowing economic growth after the pandemic tech boom.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jan/22/first-industrial-action-at-amazon-uk-hopes-to-strike-at-firms-union-hostility"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not recognised the union}}}\uc2 , continuing a pattern of hostility towards organised labour across the world. The company has consistently argued that its employees should not unionise.\par \par \uc2 The striking Coventry workers, a minority of the 1,400 staff working at the warehouse, are seeking \u163\'00\'A315 an hour, a 43% increase from the \u163\'00\'A310.50 rate they are paid. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  had offered a 5% rise, 50p more an hour. The national living wage, the legal minimum, is \u163\'00\'A310.42 an hour for workers over 23.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has previously said it gave staff a \u163\'00\'A3500 cost of living payment at Christmas.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Gearing, a GMB senior organiser, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis unprecedented week-long strike shows the anger among \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in Coventry. They work for one of the richest companies in the world, yet they have to work round the clock to keep themselves afloat.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s sickening that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in Coventry will earn just 8p above the [minimum wage] in April 2023. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  bosses can stop this industrial action by doing the right thing and negotiating a proper pay rise with workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has also faced a push for increased unionisation in the US. In November a US judge ruled that the company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/nov/28/amazon-staten-island-new-york-retaliation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cease and desist from retaliating against workers}}}\uc2  for organising in the workplace at a warehouse in Staten Island, New Jersey. Workers at the warehouse were the first \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees to win a union vote in the US, although the compnay has defeated other attempts to unionise in the US.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesperson said the workers going on strike represented only a small proportion of its Coventry workforce.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re proud to offer competitive pay which starts at a minimum of between \u163\'00\'A310.50 and \u163\'00\'A311.45 per hour, depending on location. This represents a 29% increase in the minimum hourly wage paid to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees since 2018,\u8221\'20\'1D the spokesperson added.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230213ej2d0025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
