\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 US experts warn AI likely to kill off jobs \u8211\'20\'13 and widen wealth inequality\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,405 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 February 2023\par 07:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Economists wary of firm predictions but say advances could create new raft of billionaires while other workers are laid off\par \par \uc2 ChatGPT is just the latest technology to fuel worries that it will wipe out the jobs of millions of workers, whether advertising copywriters, Wall Street traders, salespeople, writers of basic computer code or journalists.\par \par \uc2 But while many workforce experts say the fears that ChatGPT and other artificial intelligence (AI) technologies will cause unemployment to skyrocket are overblown, they point to another fear about AI: that it will widen the US\u8217\'20\'19s already huge income and wealth inequality by creating a new wave of billionaire tech barons at the same time that it pushes many workers out of better paid jobs.\par \par \uc2 Like many revolutionary technologies before it, AI is likely to eliminate jobs. But, as has been the case in the past, experts argue, AI will likely offset much of that by spurring the creation of new jobs in addition to enhancing many existing jobs. The big question is: what sort of jobs?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAI will wipe out a lot of current jobs, as has happened with all past technologies,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lawrence Katz, a labor economist at Harvard. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I have no reason to think that AI and robots won\u8217\'20\'19t continue changing the mix of jobs. The question is: will the change in the mix of jobs exacerbate existing inequalities? Will AI raise productivity so much that even as it displaces a lot of jobs, it creates new ones and raises living standards?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anu Madgavkar\uc2 , who leads labor market research at the \uc2 McKinsey Global Institute\uc2 , estimates that one in four workers in the US are going to see more AI and technology adopted in their jobs. She said 50-60% of companies say they are pursuing AI-related projects. \u8220\'20\'1CSo one way or the other people are going to have to learn to work with AI,\u8221\'20\'1D Madgavkar said.\par \par \uc2 One way or the other people are going to have to learn to work with AI\par \par \uc2 Anu Madgavkar\par \par \uc2 While past rounds of automation affected factory jobs most, Madgavkar said that AI will hit white-collar jobs most. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s increasingly going into office-based work and customer service and sales,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are the job categories that will have the highest rate of automation adoption and the biggest displacement. These workers will have to work with it or move into different skills.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In other words, many office workers could face downward mobility.\par \par \uc2 Workforce experts are asking many questions about AI: will it displace many call-center workers or merely make those workers more productive by quickly delivering needed information to them as they speak to customers? Will AI displace radiologists because of its ability to read cancer scans or will it help radiologists by enabling them to focus on more complicated, nuanced issues in interpreting scans? Will AI be able to replace some journalists by writing stories about baseball games or Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s daily ups and downs?\par \par \uc2 Some workforce experts say AI and other new technologies will hurt middle-level, white-collar jobs more than lower-paying, physically intensive jobs. McKinsey\u8217\'20\'19s Madgavkar said it will be hard for AI or robots to do the jobs of janitors. In food service, she said, new technologies might be able to take customer orders, but \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19re not going to see many little robots that bring the food to a particular table\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At law firms, AI might eliminate some attorneys\u8217\'20\'19 jobs by being able to prepare first drafts of business contracts. But AI might also enable paralegals to oversee preparation of draft contracts, and that increased responsibility could mean higher pay for paralegals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you make workers more productive, workers are then supposed to make more money,\u8221\'20\'1D said William Spriggs, an economics professor at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  and chief economist at the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the nation\u8217\'20\'19s main labor federation. \u8220\'20\'1CCompanies don\u8217\'20\'19t want to have a discussion about sharing the benefits of these technologies. They\u8217\'20\'19d rather have a discussion to scare the bejesus out of you about these new technologies. They want you to concede that you\u8217\'20\'19re just grateful to have a job and that you\u8217\'20\'19ll pay us peanuts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Spriggs noted that when a wave of automation swept through the auto industry from the 1950s through 1970s, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe \uc2 UAW\uc2  said to \uc2 Ford\uc2  and GM, we\u8217\'20\'19re a lot more productive and you\u8217\'20\'19re a lot more profitable. As a result, the workers got a lot more money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 David Autor\uc2 , an economics professor at MIT, is wary of making predictions about ChatGPT and AI. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s just enormous uncertainty,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The concern is: will AI reduce the value of a lot of skill sets and make labor more commodified?\par \par \uc2 David Autor\uc2 ,\par \par \uc2 But he\u8217\'20\'19s not concerned with the US running out of jobs. \u8220\'20\'1CIf anything, we don\u8217\'20\'19t have enough people for jobs right now,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m concerned about the change of composition in jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D He voiced concern that AI, by eliminating some middle-class jobs and de-skilling some jobs, will move many workers into lower-paying jobs like food service. \u8220\'20\'1CThe concern is: will AI reduce the value of a lot of skill sets and make labor more commodified?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 New technologies like AI often produce jobs that no one could predict \u8211\'20\'13 before the invention of computers, who would have foreseen the job of computer programmer? Workforce experts say AI will create more jobs for engineers and certain types of managers, and that any AI-caused decrease in jobs could be offset by increases in the number of healthcare jobs as the overall population ages. AI might call for a beefed-up system of retraining to, for instance, prepare a laid-off salesperson for a hospital job.\par \par \uc2 Juliet Schor, an economist at Boston College, said it would be far better if employers, instead of laying off people because of AI, would trim employees\u8217\'20\'19 work time, perhaps to three or four days a week, instead of five. \u8220\'20\'1CWork-time reduction is really the far better way to respond to labor-displacing technological change,\u8221\'20\'1D Schor said. She voiced fears that AI could produce a large pool of jobless Americans, and even with some system of universal basic income, \u8220\'20\'1Cthat would create inequality between the people who have work and the people who don\u8217\'20\'19t. That\u8217\'20\'19s a big problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Julie Shah, an MIT professor who leads the Interactive Robotics Group at MIT\u8217\'20\'19s Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence Laboratory, said she works with employers to get them to use AI and robots to \u8220\'20\'1Caugment and enhance workers, rather than replace them\u8221\'20\'1D. She said some employers want to use robots to have a lights-out factory without any human workers, while other companies want robots to work alongside humans to make them more efficient \u8212\'20\'14 and to have human workers on hand to propose future ideas for innovation.\par \par \uc2 Shah pointed to a study of large French corporations that introduced robots; those firms increased overall employment even as their domestic competitors reduced their workforce. She also cited a study of Canadian companies that began using robots and ultimately reduced the number of middle managers, while increasing the number of production workers. In the US, she noted, some companies adopted robots and offered higher wages, while having fewer jobs overall.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese technologies are not leading to one future, but to many possible futures,\u8221\'20\'1D Shah said.\par \par \uc2 Harvard\u8217\'20\'19s Katz is also worried about AI\u8217\'20\'19s effects on income inequality. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s likely to continue to reduce labor\u8217\'20\'19s share of income as many tasks get automated,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Katz said a big issue is who will share in the gains if AI yields major productivity growth, and how those gains will be shared. \u8220\'20\'1CHow much will need to come through redistribution policies?\u8221\'20\'1D he asked. \u8220\'20\'1CIf it\u8217\'20\'19s really good and massively increases productivity, even if workers get a smaller share of the pie, they could end up with higher incomes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But these gains are unlikely to trickle down to workers in the US given current circumstances. \u8220\'20\'1CHaving a stronger say for workers and their representatives in this process is an important element to adjusting to these changes. That\u8217\'20\'19s happened in countries that have stronger unions and works councils. That\u8217\'20\'19s an area where we in the US have fallen behind,\u8221\'20\'1D Katz said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230208ej28000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
