\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Hypocritical\u8217\'20\'19: environmental groups blocking union efforts, US workers say\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,726 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2023\par 19:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A wave of unionization has swept the non-profit sector \u8211\'20\'13 but workers say they are experiencing aggressive opposition and retaliation\par \par \uc2 Workers at some of the top environmental organizations in the US are calling out their managers as \u8220\'20\'1Cincredibly hypocritical\u8221\'20\'1D as they argue the progressive non-profits are fighting workers\u8217\'20\'19 efforts to unionize.\par \par \uc2 A wave of unionization efforts has swept the non-profit sector as part of a renewed national enthusiasm for unionization. Shortly into the Covid-19 pandemic, workers at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/100763358015595/posts/we-are-proud-to-share-that-350org-staff-in-the-us-are-now-unionized-we-are-joini/228997198525543/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 350.org}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cwa-union.org/news/releases/sunrise-movement-staff-form-union-communications-workers-of-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sunrise Movement}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cwa-union.org/news/releases/more-120-national-audubon-society-workers-across-11-regions-vote-form-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the National Audubon Society}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opeiu-local2.org/news/defenders-united-wins-with-93-yes-for-union-votes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Defenders of Wildlife}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/GPUSAWorkers/status/1321943211020062723"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Greenpeace}}}\uc2  USA, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.publicinterestunion.org/press-releases/card-check-win-release"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Public Interest Network}}}\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://biologicaldiversity.org/w/news/press-releases/center-for-biological-diversity-recognizes-employee-union-after-card-check-2021-08-06/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Center for Biological Diversity}}}\uc2  unionized.\par \par \uc2 But workers at many of these organizations say they have experienced aggressive opposition and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gofundme.com/f/support-assata-harris-who-was-fired-by-350org/donate?member=18754807"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 alleged retaliation}}}\uc2  in the lead-up to unionizing and through bargaining efforts for a first union contract.\par \par \uc2 Defenders of Wildlife\uc2  has fought unionization since the campaign went public, according to workers, and has continued to do so through bargaining. The organization, which works to protect all native animals and plants throughout North America, rejected a request to voluntarily recognize the union, but workers successfully {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/region-05-baltimore/region-5-announces-results-in-defenders-of-wildlife-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won }}}\uc2  a union election in September 2021 with a vote of 70 to five to join the Office and Professional Employees Union Local 2.\par \par \uc2 One of the allegations the non-profit faces is that it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gofundme.com/f/i-stand-with-erica"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fired}}}\uc2  employee Erica Prather in retaliation for her role in the union campaign.\par \par \uc2 Prather was fired in February 2022 and the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/05-CA-290774"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued}}}\uc2  a complaint on her behalf, finding merit in the charge, which is expected to be adjudicated in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDefenders\u8217\'20\'19 goal with this isn\u8217\'20\'19t to do the right thing. They could have even looked like the hero and just apologized and reinstated me,\u8221\'20\'1D said Prather.\par \par \uc2 She argued the union drive was sparked by how management treats employees and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eenews.net/articles/defenders-of-wildlife-staffers-decry-culture-of-fear/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poor workplace culture}}}\uc2  that has driven {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/defenders_staff/status/1618283195308572672"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high turnover}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBeing able to put something on a piece of paper, to make yourself look good to funders is something different than living it and living it means you\u8217\'20\'19re gonna have to let workers have some autonomy,\u8221\'20\'1D Prather said. \u8220\'20\'1CAll the time, the money, the labor, could be fighting the climate crisis and the ecological crisis, which is what we\u8217\'20\'19re supposed to do. It\u8217\'20\'19s been just all that time, whether it\u8217\'20\'19s this trial or bargaining, like, what are you actually doing this work for?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A current employee at \uc2 Defenders of Wildlife\uc2  and member of the bargaining committee who requested to remain anonymous for fear of retaliation claimed the organization has refused to bargain with the union.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/defenders_staff/status/1604144392893186048"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued a complaint}}}\uc2  against \uc2 Defenders of Wildlife\uc2  for its refusal to bargain in good faith with the union.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have now had about seven proposals on the table and the only thing that is tentatively agreed upon is the preamble, which is a paragraph long and has nothing whatsoever controversial in it,\u8221\'20\'1D the worker said. \u8220\'20\'1CMy opinion as a union member is because they have no interest in giving us anything.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Defenders of Wildlife\uc2  did not comment on turnover or specific allegations of retaliation, citing privacy concerns around personnel. It denied allegations of discrimination or retaliation and claimed they\u8217\'20\'19re bargaining in good faith.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLike many organizations, we also recognize that there is more work to do. We are committed to supporting our staff and advancing diversity, equity and inclusion throughout our organization and the conservation movement,\u8221\'20\'1D said a spokesperson for \uc2 Defenders of Wildlife\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe are listening to staff feedback and working to make improvements that enhance our workplace and advance Defenders\u8217\'20\'19 important conservation work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 We have now had about seven proposals on the table and the only thing that is tentatively agreed upon is the preamble\par \par \uc2 Defenders of Wildlife\uc2  employee\par \par \uc2 Workers at the \uc2 National Audubon Society{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cwa-union.org/news/releases/more-120-national-audubon-society-workers-across-11-regions-vote-form-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 successfully unionized }}}\uc2  in 2021 with the \uc2 Communications Workers of America (CWA)\uc2 , but have claimed significant {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inthesetimes.com/article/audubon-society-staff-union-labor-birds-nonprofit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 union opposition}}}\uc2  from management through unionization and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cwa-union.org/news/bargaining-update-229"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bargaining}}}\uc2 . At {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/02/20/350org-mckibben-boeve-keystone-00009866"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 350.org}}}\uc2 , workers have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PWUnion1/status/1517891422074118146"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused }}}\uc2  the environmental non-profit of retaliating against workers for unionizing.\par \par \uc2 Other non-profits have sought to overturn union victories. At the \uc2 Center for Biological Diversity\uc2 , Kieran Suckling, co-founder and executive director, pushed for an effort to decertify the union, in a staff-wide email obtained by the Guardian entitled \u8220\'20\'1CVote to free the center from the \uc2 CWA\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDecertifying \uc2 CWA\uc2  will allow everyone at the center \u8211\'20\'13 from the bargaining unit, to managers, to the executive team \u8211\'20\'13 to regroup and craft a healing way forward,\u8221\'20\'1D Suckling wrote. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 CWA\uc2  has brought unprecedented divisiveness, insecurity and hostility to the Center.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The decertification election ultimately {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/28-RD-303851"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failed}}}\uc2  in October 2022.\par \par \uc2 After 68% of workers signed cards to join the \uc2 CWA\uc2  at \uc2 Center for Biological Diversity\uc2  the non-profit voluntarily recognized the union in 2021. Staff say that workers close to management then began criticizing the union and the decision to unionize on staff-wide listservs. They said they felt unable to push back for fear of being targeted, and that this animosity has continued through bargaining.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe tensions are very high. One thing leadership is trying to do is to make sure a contract never comes to fruition. They seem to push back on everything the bargaining committee is seeking to win,\u8221\'20\'1D said one worker who requested to remain anonymous for fear of retaliation. \u8220\'20\'1CLeadership wants to look like they\u8217\'20\'19re progressive, but don\u8217\'20\'19t want to release the reins.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another worker at the \uc2 Center for Biological Diversity\uc2  said the union campaign was driven by a desire to improve working conditions and support the important work the group does, including addressing pay inequities, more job security and the lack of a system to properly address grievances.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CImmediately there was a lot of backlash of this very one-sided attack on the unionization process, nitpicking, wild allegations and grandstanding on the side of management,\u8221\'20\'1D they said. \u8220\'20\'1COur executive director has a bad case of founder\u8217\'20\'19s syndrome, with weird power plays, actions not aligning with the values that upper management says they have, and power-tripping.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They argued management and some workers aligning with management against the union have mischaracterized union proposals, are pushing to make union dues voluntary, and revolved union opposition around \u8220\'20\'1Cstrawman\u8221\'20\'1D arguments that the \uc2 CWA\uc2  is not progressive enough as a national union, while purporting to not be anti-union.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey can\u8217\'20\'19t get over the fact that something significant at the Center isn\u8217\'20\'19t being controlled by them,\u8221\'20\'1D the worker added. \u8220\'20\'1CThe tears haven\u8217\'20\'19t stopped flowing and they have no self-awareness of how entitled they\u8217\'20\'19re coming off because they\u8217\'20\'19re so used to being centered all the time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Suckling said in an email that \u8220\'20\'1C90% of the union controversy at the Center has been within the bargaining unit, not between management and the union\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Center is, and has always been, pro-union and pro-unionization,\u8221\'20\'1D he added, claiming the primary reason behind opposition to \uc2 CWA\uc2  is the union\u8217\'20\'19s inclusion of police officer unions. He denied allegations of stoking and supporting union opposition. \u8220\'20\'1CWe had hoped that selecting a new or independent union would resolve the intense inter-bargaining unit conflicts. A majority (56%) of the unit members, however, believed the criticisms were not sufficient reason to start unionization over and voted to instead remain with \uc2 CWA\uc2 . We accept that without question and will continue to seek resolution of the conflicts with this framework.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They did not comment on an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/28-CA-292511"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unfair labor practice charge}}}\uc2  that workers allege was settled with the employee leaving the organization.\par \par \uc2 At the Public Interest Network, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://publicinterestnetwork.org/about/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 operates and supports}}}\uc2  numerous environmental organizations throughout the US, workers launched a union campaign in 2021 and received {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.publicinterestunion.org/press-releases/card-check-win-release"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voluntary recognition}}}\uc2  in March 2022 with OPEIU Local 2. But workers say the organization has dragged out bargaining a first contract and pushed out workers involved in organizing.\par \par \uc2 Zachary Barber, a field organizer for PennEnvironment, part of the Public Interest Network, claimed the organization has repeatedly rejected proposals in bargaining and recently went to arbitration to try to limit the size of the bargaining unit, which the union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TPINunion/status/1590751236923748353"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won}}}\uc2 . The union has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Vh0iUqByI1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}\uc2  several unfair labor practice charges against the organization, including allegations of retaliation against workers and making unilateral changes to employment without bargaining.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been told directly by senior members in leadership that if we don\u8217\'20\'19t agree with the way they\u8217\'20\'19re running the organization that we should just leave,\u8221\'20\'1D said Barber. \u8220\'20\'1CGenerally the point of the union is giving people the ability to come together and have a voice in their workplace, and that\u8217\'20\'19s the same thing we fight for every day as a grassroots organization.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Zach Szlezinger worked at the Public Interest Network as a higher education associate before he claims he was pushed out in Summer 2022 in retaliation for his heavy involvement in the union campaign as a bargaining committee member.\par \par \uc2 I had proven myself in the job and it was clearly retaliation from management\par \par \uc2 Zach Szlezinger\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was told that, with no reasoning, that there\u8217\'20\'19s no place for me this year, or in the future,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was very abnormal. I had proven myself in the job and it was clearly retaliation from management.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to acknowledge how incredibly hypocritical it is. It\u8217\'20\'19s almost crazy that an organization that fights for all of this could be so clearly anti-union and take these kinds of harmful actions,\u8221\'20\'1D added Szlezinger. \u8220\'20\'1CI know that unions are going to strengthen the environmental movement, and they\u8217\'20\'19re the only way to bring the movement into the future that\u8217\'20\'19s actually in touch with the problems that we\u8217\'20\'19re facing today.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Public Interest Network did not comment on Szlezinger\u8217\'20\'19s firing, unfair labor practice charges directly, or alleged comments made to workers by management. A spokesperson said in an email that after voluntarily recognizing the union, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe have negotiated in good faith since then in a general atmosphere of collegiality among all participants. Indeed, we have responded to all of the union\u8217\'20\'19s proposals and made counter-proposals to many of them, as well as initiating some proposals ourselves. We are waiting on the union to respond to many of our proposals.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 3 February 2023. The lead photograph was changed to a more relevant image.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230202ej22001jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
