\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 People in abortion-restrictive US states economically disempowered \u8211\'20\'13 study\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 700 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 January 2023\par 14:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wages, incarceration rates and unemployment benefits access worse when compared with states allowing the procedure\par \par \uc2 Wages, employment security, incarceration rates and access to unemployment benefits are all worse in US states where abortion is restricted or banned, compared with those where it is protected, a new report has found.\par \par \uc2 The report by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  also found that minimum wages are, on average, $3.75 an hour lower in abortion restrictive states compared with protective states ($8.17 compared with $11.92); and that restrictive states incarcerate people at 1.5 times the rate of protective states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is strong empirical evidence that abortion denial and abortion bans have negative economic consequences, from prolonged financial distress to lower wages and earnings, employment, educational attainment, and economic mobility,\u8221\'20\'1D said Asha Banerjee, an economic analyst at EPI who authored the report.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe states that have banned abortion rights are also the same states economically disempowering people through these other economic channels,\u8221\'20\'1D Banerjee added.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  last year scrapped federal abortion rights previously guaranteed under Roe vs Wade, a wave of Republican-run states have moved to limit or ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 The report analyzes data from 50 US states and the District of Columbia, listing 25 states as protective and 26 as abortion restrictive. Those restrictive states include states such as Alabama, Idaho and Tennessee, which all have full abortion bans; as well as states like Florida, Georgia and Utah, which have different gestational limits and other restrictions on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Calling restrictions a \u8220\'20\'1Csustained project of economic subjugation and disempowerment\u8221\'20\'1D, the report portrays a damning economic situation in the 26 states where abortion is severely restricted or banned.\par \par \uc2 With particular reference to differences in the minimum wage in restrictive and protective states, the report points out that \u8220\'20\'1Ckeeping the minimum wage purposefully and persistently low ensures that many people and families will struggle to cover their costs and get out of poverty \u8230\'20\'26 If the person denied an abortion is also working a minimum wage job, the negative economic effect is compounded.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The report also finds that, on average, abortion-restrictive states have half the rate of unionized workers compared with protective states \u8211\'20\'13 an important indicator of economic power and racial parity in wages.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to the availability and access to welfare benefits \u8211\'20\'13 such as unemployment and medicaid expansion \u8211\'20\'13 the report finds a similar relationship. In restrictive states, unemployed people access the benefits they are entitled to at a rate of 12 percentage points lower than unemployed workers in protective states.\par \par \uc2 A number of states in the restrictive category have also not expanded Medicaid under the Affordable Care Act, despite often having higher levels of parental poverty. \u8220\'20\'1CThe federal government has Medicaid funding coverage allocated, yet states choose not to take it \u8211\'20\'13 leaving millions without health insurance coverage,\u8221\'20\'1D the report says.\par \par \uc2 But it is the statistics on incarceration that show the steepest differences between restrictive and protective states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIncarceration and the criminalization of pregnancy and abortion have existed historically (recall the bans) and are seeing a troubling resurgence after the [supreme court decision] \u8230\'20\'26 Some states are threatening to imprison anyone suspected of \u8216\'20\'18aiding or abetting\u8217\'20\'19 an abortion, including medical professionals, acquaintances, family members, and even ride-share app drivers \u8230\'20\'26 \u8221\'20\'1D the report said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWomen of color are disproportionately likely to be and are overrepresented among people getting abortions in the states that recorded demographic data. Incarceration and abortion bans are intertwined with the long legacy of state-sanctioned anti-Black racism,\u8221\'20\'1D the report states.\par \par \uc2 Criminal justice reform advocates agreed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAbortion restrictions and incarceration are about the same thing: power and control. Power and control over people\u8217\'20\'19s bodies, lives and autonomy. Mass criminalization and incarceration over the last several decades have included the criminalization of pregnant people as collateral damage in the war on drugs and the criminalization of poverty,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dana Sussman, acting executive director of Pregnancy Justice.\par \par \uc2 Sussman added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe must understand that law enforcement and the carceral system target vulnerable people, including pregnant people, and those who help them and are a destabilizing and traumatizing force that inflicts harms on generations of families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230119ej1j000wa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
