\par\par \uc2 UK news\par \b \uc2 UN\uc2  agency and US labour secretary deny backing UK anti-strike bill\b0\par\par\uc2 Aletha Adu and Jessica Elgot \par \uc2 703 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 January 2023\par 15:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Remarks at odds with government claims that planned minimum service law has \u8216\'20\'18international seal of approval\u8217\'20\'19UK politics live \u8211\'20\'13 latest news updates\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UN\uc2  agency for workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights and the US labour secretary have distanced themselves from the \uc2 UK government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s claims that its strikes bill has the \u8220\'20\'1Cinternational seal of approval\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Number 10 hit back at the comments from Washington, saying far more restrictive rules were in place in many US states and that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself had blocked a national rail strike.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , and the business secretary, \uc2 Grant Shapps\uc2 , have repeatedly justified rolling out the anti-strike legislation, claiming the \uc2 International Labour Organization (ILO) \uc2 backs minimum service levels being provided during stoppages by workers, a key plank of the proposed new measures.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/jan/11/the-guardian-view-on-anti-strike-laws-bad-in-practice-wrong-in-principle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 law}}}\uc2  would apply across England, Scotland and Wales, and would mandate minimum service levels for critical industries even on strike days, meaning some workers must stay on duty in sectors such as health, transport, the fire service, border control, nuclear and education.\par \par \uc2 During prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s questions last Wednesday, Sunak told the Commons: \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 International Labour Organization\uc2  supports minimum service levels. They are present in France, in Italy, in Spain.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Shapps told parliament that the ILO believes \u8220\'20\'1Cminimum service levels are a proportionate way of balancing the right to strike with the need to protect the wider public\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But in an intervention, Gilbert Houngbo, the director general of the ILO, expressed surprise that his organisation was being used to endorse the legislation.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to the BBC at Davos, Houngbo said: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not aware of any bilateral discussion on this matter. We are very worried that workers may have to accept situations so they don\u8217\'20\'19t get themselves out of a job. They may have to accept a situation that is below par.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After hearing Houngbo\u8217\'20\'19s comments, the US labour secretary, Martin J Walsh, was cited as urging the broadcaster to ask him for his thoughts, clearly keen to express his viewpoint on whether he supported minimum service levels.\par \par \uc2 Walsh said: \u8220\'20\'1CNo. I don\u8217\'20\'19t know about the legislation. But I certainly will work with the ILO, [the United States] is a board member of the ILO as well \u8230\'20\'26 I would not support anything that would take away from workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sunak\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson underlined that Walsh had not seen the legislation. \u8220\'20\'1CThe steps we\u8217\'20\'19re taking to introduce minimum service levels on critical sectors including blue-light services \u8230\'20\'26 do not inhibit unions\u8217\'20\'19 ability to strike,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CIn the US, many states prohibit strikes by emergency services altogether. Fire services are banned from striking in most states. And in December, President Biden signed legislation to block a national rail strike.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, nurses returned to the picket line in a dispute over pay, as the head of the nursing union said patients did not get safe staffing levels \u8220\'20\'1Cany day of the week\u8221\'20\'1D in the \uc2 NHS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Angela Rayner\uc2 , the Labour deputy leader and shadow secretary for the future of work, said: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Grant Shapps\uc2  has been ludicrously claiming that his sacking nurses bill has the international seal of approval, but the ILO and the US labour secretary clearly beg to differ. The business secretary has been hiding behind warped and wilful misunderstandings of the \uc2 International Labour Organization\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s code in his desperate attempts to justify this shoddy, unworkable and vindictive piece of legislation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It comes as the immigration minister, \uc2 Robert Jenrick\uc2 , could not rule out the possibility of nurses continuing to strike for months.\par \par \uc2 He told LBC News: \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t want these strikes to proceed. We are approaching these discussions with the greatest spirit of reasonableness and collegiality, meeting with members of the \uc2 Royal College of Nursing\uc2  and other trade union bodies as regularly as we can.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut we don\u8217\'20\'19t want the strikes to continue for one day longer than is necessary. We\u8217\'20\'19re asking the unions to call them off because they will be harmful to patients, they will endanger the safety of the public and they will make it much harder to tackle the backlog of cases.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230118ej1i001bs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
