\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The US should break up monopolies \u8211\'20\'13 not punish working Americans for rising prices\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 930 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 January 2023\par 16:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Fed is putting people out of work to reduce workers\u8217\'20\'19 bargaining power and reduce inflation. They\u8217\'20\'19ve got it all wrong\par \par \uc2 Job growth and wages are slowing. Employers added 223,000 jobs in December, the labor department reported on Friday \u8211\'20\'13 lower than the average in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Average hourly wages rose by 4.6% in December, according to Friday\u8217\'20\'19s report. That\u8217\'20\'19s a slowdown from 4.8% in November.\par \par \uc2 All this is music to the ears of Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , because the Fed blames inflation on rising wages. The Fed has been increasing interest rates to slow the economy and thereby reduce the bargaining power of workers to get wage gains.\par \par \uc2 At his press conference on 14 December {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.federalreserve.gov/mediacenter/files/FOMCpresconf20221214.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announcing}}}\uc2  the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s latest interest rate hike, Powell warned that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe labor market remains extremely tight, with the unemployment rate near a 50-year low, job vacancies still very high, and wage growth elevated\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But aren\u8217\'20\'19t higher wages a good thing ?\par \par \uc2 The typical American worker\u8217\'20\'19s wage has been stuck in the mud for four decades.\par \par \uc2 Most of the gains from a more productive economy have been going to the top \u8211\'20\'13 to executives and investors. The richest 10% of Americans now own more than 90% of the value of shares of stock owned by Americans.\par \par \uc2 Powell\u8217\'20\'19s solution to inflation is to clobber workers even further. He {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.federalreserve.gov/mediacenter/files/FOMCpresconf20221214.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 says}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cthe labor market continues to be out of balance, with demand substantially exceeding the supply of available workers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But if the demand for workers exceeds the supply, isn\u8217\'20\'19t the answer to pay workers more?\par \par \uc2 Not according to Powell and the Fed. Their answer is to continue to raise interest rates to slow the economy and put more people out of work, so workers can\u8217\'20\'19t get higher wages. That way, \u8220\'20\'1Csupply and demand conditions in the labor market [will] come into better balance over time, easing upward pressures on wages and prices,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.federalreserve.gov/mediacenter/files/FOMCpresconf20221214.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 says}}}\uc2  Powell.\par \par \uc2 Putting people out of work is the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s means of reducing workers\u8217\'20\'19 bargaining power and the \u8220\'20\'1Cupward pressures on wages and prices \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Fed projects that as it continues to increase interest rates, unemployment will rise to 4.6% by the end of 2023 \u8211\'20\'13 resulting in more than 1m job losses.\par \par \uc2 But fighting inflation by putting more people out of work is cruel, especially when America\u8217\'20\'19s safety nets \u8211\'20\'13 including unemployment insurance \u8211\'20\'13 are in tatters.\par \par \uc2 As we saw at the start of the pandemic, because the US doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have a single nationwide system for getting cash to jobless workers, they have to depend on state unemployment insurance, which varies considerably from state to state.\par \par \uc2 Many fall through the cracks. When the pandemic began, fewer than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2020/04/24/not-all-unemployed-people-get-unemployment-benefits-in-some-states-very-few-do/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 30% }}}\uc2  of jobless Americans qualified for unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 The problem isn\u8217\'20\'19t that wages are rising. The real problem is that corporations have the power to pass those wage increases \u8211\'20\'13 along with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-08-25/us-corporate-profits-soar-taking-margins-to-widest-since-1950?sref=0KUfhQHv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record profit margins}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 on to consumers in the form of higher prices.\par \par \uc2 If corporations had to compete vigorously for consumers, they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be able to do this. Competitors would charge lower prices and grab those consumers away.\par \par \uc2 Corporations aren\u8217\'20\'19t even plowing their extra profits into new investments that would generate higher productivity in the future. They\u8217\'20\'19re buying back their shares to boost stock prices. Through the end of 2022, American firms announced stock buybacks {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-10-25/buyback-addiction-rages-among-companies-at-war-with-bear-market?sref=0KUfhQHv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exceeding $1tn}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A rational response to inflation, therefore, would not increase unemployment in order to reduce the bargaining power of workers to get higher wages.\par \par \uc2 It would be to reduce the pricing power of corporations to pass those costs along to consumers along with rising profit margins, by making markets more competitive.\par \par \uc2 Corporate pricing power is out of control because corporations face so little competition.\par \par \uc2 Worried about sky-high airline fares and lousy service? That\u8217\'20\'19s largely because {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/how-airlines-became-delta-united-american-233400004.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 airlines have merged}}}\uc2  from 12 carriers in 1980 to four today.\par \par \uc2 Concerned about drug prices? A handful of drug companies control the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.openmarketsinstitute.org/learn/drug-prices-monopoly"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pharmaceutical industry}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Upset about food costs? Four giants now control over 80% of meat processing, 66% of the pork market, and 54% of the poultry market.\par \par \uc2 Worried about grocery prices? \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  bought \uc2 Safeway\uc2  and now \uc2 Kroger\uc2  is buying \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 . Combined, they would control almost 22% of the US grocery market. Add in \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , and the three brands would control {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/14/business/kroger-albertsons-merger.html#:~:text=If%20the%20merger%20went%20through%2C%20she%20said%2C%20the%20combined%20Albertsons%2DKroger%20and%20Walmart%20would%20control%2070%20percent%20or%20more%20of%20the%20market%20in%20167%20cities%20in%20the%20United%20States."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 70% of the grocery market in 167 cities}}}\uc2  across the country.\par \par \uc2 And so on. The evidence of corporate concentration is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s getting worse. There were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wbur.org/onpoint/2022/12/27/more-than-money-microsoft-and-the-big-tech-question#:~:text=Just%20guess%20how%20many%20major%20corporate%20mergers%20or%20acquisitions%20took%20place%20globally%20last%20year%2C%20with%20a%20merger%20value%20of%20more%20than%20%24100%20million%3F%20Just%20guess.%20Take%20a%20second.%20Do%20you%20have%20a%20number%3F%20Was%20it%20more%20than%20a%20thousand%3F%20Because%20that%27s%20the%20answer.%20Total%20transaction%20value%3A%20%241.4%20trillion."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over a thousand}}}\uc2  major corporate mergers or acquisitions last year. Each had a merger value of $100m or more. The total transaction value was $1.4tn.\par \par \uc2 The government must stop putting the responsibility for fighting inflation on working people whose wages have gone nowhere for four decades.\par \par \uc2 Put the responsibility where it belongs \u8211\'20\'13 on big corporations with power to raise their prices.\par \par \uc2 One possibility: any large corporation in an industry dominated by five or fewer giant corporations that raises its prices more than the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s target of 2% should be presumed to have monopoly power, and slammed with an antitrust lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , a former US secretary of labor, is professor of public policy at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , and the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/saving-capitalism-for-the-many-not-the-few/9780345806222"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Saving Capitalism: For the Many, Not the Few}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-common-good-9780525436379/9780525436379"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Common Good}}}\uc2 . His new book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-system-who-rigged-it-how-we-fix-it/9780525659044"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The System: Who Rigged It, How We Fix It}}}\uc2 , is out now. He is a Guardian US columnist. His newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230116ej1g002fz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
