\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US adds 223,000 jobs in December, ending 2022 on high note\b0\par\par\uc2 Dominic Rushe in New York \par \uc2 380 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 January 2023\par 15:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Continued strength of jobs market comes as Fed struggles to cool hiring and bring down inflation by raising interest rates\par \par \uc2 The US jobs market ended 2022 on a high note, adding another 223,000 jobs in December, the department of labor reported on Friday. The unemployment rate dipped to 3.5%, back to its pre-pandemic low.\par \par \uc2 The continued strength of the jobs market comes as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has struggled to cool hiring and bring down inflation by raising interest rates at a pace unseen in a generation.\par \par \uc2 Jobs growth has slowed \u8211\'20\'13 the US added an average of 539,000 new positions per month in the first three months of 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 but over the year the economy added 4.5m jobs, the second strongest year on record.\par \par \uc2 The government jobs report comes a day after ADP, the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest payroll supplier, announced private employers had added 235,000 for the month, well ahead of the 153,000 Dow Jones estimate and the 127,000 initially reported for November.\par \par \uc2 The Fed has been raising rates since March last year in response to the US\u8217\'20\'19s cost of living crisis. The central bank raised rates seven times in 2022. While inflation has cooled from its peak of 9.1% in June, it remains high at 7.1%, well above the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s 2% target rate, and is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/dec/26/inflation-explainer-2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expected to remain elevated }}}\uc2  through 2023.\par \par \uc2 The annual rate of inflation is still running well ahead of wage gains but the Fed has argued that a tight labor market has led to inflation-fueling income gains. So far the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s hikes have done little to cool the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 Some sectors \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jan/05/amazon-to-axe-18000-jobs-citing-economic-uncertainty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 notably tech}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 have announced large layoffs. But the tech sector is a relatively small employer and the government\u8217\'20\'19s latest survey showed strong gains in leisure and hospitality, healthcare, construction, and social assistance.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe labor market is strong but fragmented, with hiring varying sharply by industry and establishment size,\u8221\'20\'1D ADP\u8217\'20\'19s chief economist, \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , said. \u8220\'20\'1CBusiness segments that hired aggressively in the first half of 2022 have slowed hiring and in some cases cut jobs in the last month of the year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230116ej1g002ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
