\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Uber\uc2  drivers strike in New York after company blocks raises and fare hikes\b0\par\par\uc2 Erum Salam in New York \par \uc2 584 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 January 2023\par 15:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 City agency approved raises for drivers by 7.42% per minute and 23.93% per mile but company filed lawsuit\par \par \uc2 At noon on Thursday, hundreds of \uc2 Uber\uc2  drivers gathered outside the company\u8217\'20\'19s headquarters in downtown New York, and chants echoed across the 9/11 Memorial Plaza: \u8220\'20\'1CShame on \uc2 Uber\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  drivers were engaging in a 24-hour strike that began first thing on Thursday in response to \uc2 Uber\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s move to sue New York\u8217\'20\'19s Taxi and Limousine Commission (TLC) for approving a raise and fare hike.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s opposition to the raise and fair hike for drivers comes as inflation has caused the cost of living to increase across the country.\par \par \uc2 In November, the TLC {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nyc.gov/assets/tlc/downloads/pdf/proposed-payment-of-high-volume-fhv-drivers-11-15-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approved}}}\uc2  raises for drivers by 7.42% per minute and 23.93% per mile. The raise would require that drivers be paid a minimum of $27.15 for a 30 minute, or 7.5 mile trip \u8211\'20\'13 an increase of more than $4.00 from original rates and more than $2.50 from current rates.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Uber\uc2  called TLC\u8217\'20\'19s proposed hike \u8220\'20\'1Ceconomically unjustifiable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The raise was expected to go into effect at the end of 2022, but \uc2 Uber\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit has blocked it. On Friday, a hearing will be held to determine whether to keep or lift the order blocking the raise.\par \par \uc2 Abdoul Kere, an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver since 2016 who began driving for the company a year after he arrived in the US from Burkina Faso, said that while he makes nearly $200 on an average day from driving, the cost of business leaves him with virtually nothing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you look at inflation, everything has gone up \u8211\'20\'13 food, rent, all the expenses. You have to maintain the car, pay the insurance, pay the tolls, fill the gas, and everything. So we are asking \uc2 Uber\uc2  just to help us a little bit. So if \uc2 Uber\uc2  stopped this [raise], that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make sense.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are partners. We are not slaves. \uc2 Uber\uc2  has to see how we are suffering to run this city every day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The New York Taxi Workers Alliance (NYTWA), the union that represents drivers of taxi cabs, black cars also offered their support at the protest.\par \par \uc2 Javed Tariq, NYTWA\u8217\'20\'19s co-founder, told the Guardian: \u8220\'20\'1CAt this time we have over 30,000 members. Half of them are yellow cab drivers, and half of them are app drivers from \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  \u8230\'20\'26 We struggle and fight very hard to get this fare increase.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDrivers are suffering, but \uc2 Uber\uc2  is making millions of dollars every day off the backbones of drivers. So we are here together because of \uc2 Uber\uc2  greed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In November 2021, after more than three years of protests, demonstrations, and even a hunger strike, the NYWTA was successful in negotiating financial relief with the city for taxi drivers with massive amounts of debt from purchasing medallions, the physical plate required to own and operate a taxi cab. The value of the medallion was artificially inflated and then dropped significantly, leaving medallion-owning drivers financially devastated.\par \par \uc2 Tariq said the NYTWA is prepared to put in the same amount of work in order to ensure \uc2 Uber\uc2  drivers receive this raise.\par \par \uc2 New Yorkers were asked by \uc2 Uber\uc2  drivers on strike to boycott the ride-hail app for the day. \uc2 Uber\uc2  said the strike would not affect service.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  did not respond to the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230116ej1g0026r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
