\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 There is no US labor shortage. That\u8217\'20\'19s a myth\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 January 2023\par 16:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 There is, however, a shortage of jobs paying sufficient wages to attract workers to fill job openings\par \par \uc2 When a public problem is wrongly described, the solutions posed often turn out to be irrelevant or inhumane.\par \par \uc2 A current example: America\u8217\'20\'19s so-called \u8220\'20\'1Clabor shortage\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Jerome Powell, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  chair, says the United States has a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nl.nytimes.com/f/a/GVWzkNMWpDdaO3w3yB0qbg~~/AAAAAQA~-Y8lmZytSE3JyZWljaEBiZXJrZWxleS5lZHVYBAAAAAA~"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 structural labor shortage}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D that\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely to be resolved anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2  claims there are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.uschamber.com/workforce/understanding-americas-labor-shortage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over 10 million job openings}}}\uc2  in the US for which employers can\u8217\'20\'19t find workers.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the truth: there is no labor shortage.\par \par \uc2 There is, however, a shortage of jobs paying sufficient wages to attract workers to fill job openings.\par \par \uc2 For most Americans, real (inflation-adjusted) wages continue to drop. Wages have been increasing less than prices.\par \par \uc2 Those price increases include the costs of food, energy, rent, childcare, eldercare and transportation (cars, gas and public transit) \u8211\'20\'13 all big expenses for working people.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the federal minimum wage continues to plummet. It hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been raised in 13 years \u8211\'20\'13 the longest period without a raise in its history. Adjusted for inflation, its real value is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/the-value-of-the-federal-minimum-wage-is-at-its-lowest-point-in-66-years/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lowest it\u8217\'20\'19s been in 66 years}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You don\u8217\'20\'19t have to be a financial wizard to see why some workers might say the hell with it.\par \par \uc2 Economists offered similar warnings of a \u8220\'20\'1Clabor shortage\u8221\'20\'1D after the financial crisis and recession of 2008-09. But when the economy strengthened and wages rose, the so-called \u8220\'20\'1Clabor shortage\u8221\'20\'1D magically disappeared.\par \par \uc2 So, what should be done about the difficulty employers are having finding workers?\par \par \uc2 Simple. If employers want more workers, they should pay them more.\par \par \uc2 Jerome Powell and his colleague at the Fed don\u8217\'20\'19t want to hear this. They\u8217\'20\'19re aiming to deal with the \u8220\'20\'1Clabor shortage\u8221\'20\'1D by slowing the economy so much that employers can find all the workers they need without raising wages.\par \par \uc2 Even with inflation slowing, central bankers still believe they need to slow the job market and tamp down wage gains. \u8220\'20\'1CThe biggest cost, by far, in [the service] sector is labor,\u8221\'20\'1D said Powell at his latest {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.federalreserve.gov/mediacenter/files/FOMCpresconf20221214.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 news conference}}}\uc2  in December. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we do see a very, very strong labor market \u8230\'20\'26 where wages are very high.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But slowing the economy will prevent millions of people from getting raises and cause millions more to lose their jobs \u8211\'20\'13 disproportionately low-wage workers, women and people of color.\par \par \uc2 Republicans and some corporate economists, meanwhile, are blaming the \u8220\'20\'1Clabor shortage\u8221\'20\'1D on overly generous jobless benefits. They say the way to get more people into jobs is to make their lives outside jobs less tolerable.\par \par \uc2 A recent \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://committeetounleashprosperity.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Paying-Americans-Not-to-Work.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D by Casey Mulligan and EJ Antoni claims that \u8220\'20\'1Cit pays not to work in Biden\u8217\'20\'19s America\u8221\'20\'1D, because unemployment and Affordable Care Act benefits are so generous that \u8220\'20\'1Cmany businesses {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nypost.com/2022/11/02/disturbing-rise-of-the-nilfs-men-not-in-the-labor-force/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 can\u8217\'20\'19t get workers back on the job}}}\uc2  almost three years after Covid-19 hit these shores\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Baloney. Apart from the non-working wealthy and their heirs, most unemployed people are hard up.\par \par \uc2 Pandemic benefits are over, and America\u8217\'20\'19s social safety nets are in tatters \u8211\'20\'13 as they were at the start of the pandemic. Before the pandemic, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2020/04/24/not-all-unemployed-people-get-unemployment-benefits-in-some-states-very-few-do/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fewer than 30%}}}\uc2  of unemployed Americans qualified for unemployment benefits, which last no longer than six months \u8211\'20\'13 the least generous of any other rich nation. Since then, we\u8217\'20\'19ve done nothing to fix this broken system.\par \par \uc2 Affordable Care Act subsidies, meanwhile, allow low-income people to afford health insurance. Without these subsidies, many wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t get the medical care they need and would face higher odds of getting seriously sick and hence unable to work.\par \par \uc2 Taken to its logical extreme, the corporate Republican argument might be correct. Eliminate all safety nets and at some point people without jobs will hurt so much they\u8217\'20\'19ll have to take any available job, at any wage, whatever the job demands.\par \par \uc2 But do this, and we end up with an economy that\u8217\'20\'19s even crueler than today\u8217\'20\'19s economy.\par \par \uc2 The reason people aren\u8217\'20\'19t working is that work doesn\u8217\'20\'19t pay them enough, given declining real wages and the increasing costs of what they depend on.\par \par \uc2 Both the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s solution \u8211\'20\'13 slow the economy so employers can find the workers they need without raising wages and the Republican corporate solution \u8211\'20\'13 slash safety nets so people are so desperate they must take any job available \u8211\'20\'13 are cruel. They would impose huge burdens on many of the most vulnerable people in our society.\par \par \uc2 If we want more people to take jobs and we wish to live in a decent society, the answer is to pay people more.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , a former US secretary of labor, is professor of public policy at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , and the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/saving-capitalism-for-the-many-not-the-few/9780345806222"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Saving Capitalism: For the Many, Not the Few}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-common-good-9780525436379/9780525436379"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Common Good}}}\uc2 . His new book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-system-who-rigged-it-how-we-fix-it/9780525659044"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The System: Who Rigged It, How We Fix It}}}\uc2 , is out now. He is a Guardian US columnist. His newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230116ej1g001tg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
