\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Do small businesses really need non-compete clauses? Not really\b0\par\par\uc2 Gene Marks \par \uc2 770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 January 2023\par 16:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 These types of restrictions serve a purpose, at least for some companies of a particular size \u8211\'20\'13 otherwise, let workers go where they please\par \par \uc2 Once upon a time a business would hire an employee and make them sign an employment agreement which would include a \u8220\'20\'1Cnon-compete\u8221\'20\'1D clause \u8211\'20\'13 a pro-business protection that serves one objective: prohibiting an employee from working for a competitor.\par \par \uc2 But in a period when employers are fighting for talent, workers have the upper hand and the tide is turning. Joe Biden, for one, is not a fan of non-compete clauses.\par \par \uc2 In 2021 he issued an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dwt.com/blogs/employment-labor-and-benefits/2021/07/biden-non-compete-executive-order"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 executive order}}}\uc2  aimed at curtailing the use of non-compete agreements because they limited worker mobility and made it harder for workers to change jobs or seek better working conditions. The order isn\u8217\'20\'19t law, but it directs the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  to investigate and potentially penalize companies that make use of these types of agreements when they have been deemed anti-competitive.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s unusual for the federal government to take such a provocative position in this matter. In the past, non-compete regulations have been left up to the states. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fisherphillips.com/news-insights/california-courts-weigh-in-on-non-compete-agreements.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California}}}\uc2  has banned non-compete clauses since 1872. But, seeing the writing on the wall in Washington, many other states are picking things up.\par \par \uc2 In 2021 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://vegasinc.lasvegassun.com/business/2022/jul/04/should-employers-revise-noncompete-agreements-in-l/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nevada}}}\uc2  voided the use of non-compete clauses for lower-wage employees. Oregon has made non-competes unenforceable if they\u8217\'20\'19re deemed too \u8220\'20\'1Cbroad\u8221\'20\'1D. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dwt.com/blogs/employment-labor-and-benefits/2021/07/biden-non-compete-executive-order"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maryland and Virginia}}}\uc2  recently restricted non-competes for lower-paid workers. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fargopatentlaw.com/blog/general-intellectual-property/are-non-compete-agreements-enforceable-in-north-dakota/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 North Dakota}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://shawnjroberts.com/is-my-non-compete-enforceable/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oklahoma}}}\uc2  ban any type of non-compete clauses except in very limited situations. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jdsupra.com/legalnews/new-non-compete-legislation-takes-4171150/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington DC}}}\uc2  just enacted a watered-down rule this past year. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://law.justia.com/codes/alabama/2021/title-8/chapter-1/article-10/section-8-1-190/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alabama}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.employmentlawinsights.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/36/2022/12/Rouses-Enterprises-LLC-v-Clapp.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Louisiana}}}\uc2  employers can only use non-compete agreements with existing employees.\par \par \uc2 Tech companies like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.engadget.com/microsoft-drops-non-compete-nda-075515490.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Microsoft}}}\uc2  have now discontinued the use of non-compete clauses. Big tech companies that compete with each other for talent hate it when workers switch jobs to a competitor, bringing their secrets with them.\par \par \uc2 There is still a ways to go. Between {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2021-07-12/biden-executive-order-on-non-competes-could-roil-tech."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 27.8% and 46.5%}}}\uc2  of private-sector workers in the US are still bound by them, according to one analysis, but it does look like the days for non-competes are coming to an end. At least for most workers.\par \par \uc2 Is that a problem for small businesses? Not really. Most non-compete clauses, at least for the great majority of my clients, are useless. The paragraph gets thrown into employment agreements by overzealous attorneys but I\u8217\'20\'19ve never, ever seen one enforced, and I\u8217\'20\'19ve been party to a number of acrimonious separations between key employees and companies. There have been lots of threats. But it dies there.\par \par \uc2 The reason? Pursuing non-compete violations is a costly, time-consuming, distracting and disruptive exercise. It\u8217\'20\'19s not really worth the bother because it\u8217\'20\'19s almost always the employee who\u8217\'20\'19s liable (and not their subsequent employer unless there\u8217\'20\'19s a really smart attorney involved and even then there\u8217\'20\'19s a fight) and so even if a former company prevails the payoff is never enough to be worth the effort. Plus, it\u8217\'20\'19s bad PR and doesn\u8217\'20\'19t reflect well on a company and its owners both with their outside community and current employees. Why is that big, bad company going after that poor little worker anyway?\par \par \uc2 And besides, who cares? Regardless of how \u8220\'20\'1Cimportant\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Ccritical\u8221\'20\'1D an employee is, just how damaging will it be if that person works for your competitor? Are you that afraid? Have you not protected your intellectual property? Do you not have other employees that will fill the gap? If that employee is so important to your small business, have you not done what you could to make them happy working for your company?\par \par \uc2 I realize that there are a few instances where a key employee could really hurt a former employer by going to a competitor. But rarely have I found such an employee with that kind of power or knowledge. Then again, I work in Philly, not Silicon Valley, and I mostly work with smaller companies, so maybe my viewpoint is too limited. But I don\u8217\'20\'19t think so.\par \par \uc2 So should we do away with non-compete clauses and agreements altogether? No. These types of restrictions do serve a purpose, at least for some companies of a particular size that have very senior people doing very confidential things. But it does make sense to severely restrict their use to that very limited group. Otherwise, I say let workers go where they please.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230104ej14001n4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
