\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Service workers left in the lurch as Americans cut back on tipping\b0\par\par\uc2 Wilfred Chan in New York \par \uc2 1,053 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2022\par 12:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 With lockdowns over and inflation rising, tips are shrinking in industries that depend on them \u8211\'20\'13 from gig work to restaurants\par \par \uc2 Tipping has been essential in the US for generations, but it\u8217\'20\'19s becoming increasingly rare to leave a few $1 bills out on the table \u8211\'20\'13 now when we pay for an \uc2 Uber\uc2 , buy a coffee or order takeout, a screen demands to know how much we want to tip, putting the ethical decision into clear focus. Thirty per cent? Twenty per cent? A measly 15%? Would anyone dare go lower than that?\par \par \uc2 Before the pandemic, Carlos Tavares says, he got as much as $75 in tips a week driving his gray Toyota Camry for \uc2 Uber\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 nothing life-changing, but a nice bonus after working long shifts in New York City. Now that\u8217\'20\'19s fallen sharply, he says, to $20 to $30 a week. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t really know why,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CMaybe people are just trying to save money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.poeticacoffee.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Poetica Coffee Shop}}}\uc2 , a chain of cozy cafes in Brooklyn, customers have been pulling back as well. Though nine out of 10 customers leave a tip, they\u8217\'20\'19re giving less than before, says Parviz Mukhamadkulov, the company\u8217\'20\'19s CEO and founder. \u8220\'20\'1CDuring the peak of Covid,\u8221\'20\'1D from May 2020 until June 2021, \u8220\'20\'1Ccustomers used to tip an average of 50% of the total amount. Everybody felt a need to support small businesses and local baristas that they knew. It has since then dropped to 15%.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When economic distress hits, tips are usually the first thing to go\par \par \uc2 Hildalyn Col\u243\'00\'F3n Hern\u225\'00\'E1ndez, Los Deliveristas Unidos\par \par \uc2 New research reflects this more miserly tipping trend, as the urgency of the pandemic fades and the pressures of inflation rise. According to a survey released this month by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2022/11/15/2555842/0/en/Fewer-U-S-Consumers-Are-Tipping-Restaurant-Staff-20-or-Higher-but-Majority-Expect-to-Boost-Gratuities-Over-the-Holidays.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Popmenu}}}\uc2 , 43% of consumers say they tip their servers at least 20% of the check, down from 56% of consumers who did the same last year. Thirty-two per cent of people reported tipping delivery workers 20% of their order, down from 38% a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Tipping service workers is a longstanding American social norm \u8211\'20\'13 one that comes with obvious risks: it can play into racism and sexism, and it puts workers at risk of wage theft. But while some US restaurants have tried to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/feb/13/us-tipping-restaurants-wages"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminate tipping}}}\uc2 , many have backtracked when earnings decreased for workers and the business.\par \par \uc2 Business owners say tipping is often an emotional response; studies have shown tips tend to increase during the holidays. Mukhamadkulov says Poetica Coffee recently saw an increase in tips after their baristas \u8211\'20\'13 some of whom are Ukrainian \u8211\'20\'13 hosted a donation drive to support the people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But tipping norms also diverge greatly from one industry to another, even if the effort involved is comparable. Data collected in 2021 by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.driversseat.co/post/data-insights-with-drivers-seat-lets-talk-about-tips"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Driver\u8217\'20\'19s Seat}}}\uc2 , a gig worker-focused platform, found that while 85.8% of customers tip for food delivery, only 24.8% do the same for \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers. And a survey last month by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.playusa.com/state-of-tipping-2022/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 PlayUSA}}}\uc2  found that while 98% of restaurant workers receive tips, only 39% of coffee shop workers do.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s frustrating for Tiana, an employee at a recently opened counter-service cafe that sells boba and freshly made sandwiches in the celebrity-studded neighborhood of Brooklyn Heights. She says that only about a third of her customers leave a tip because they don\u8217\'20\'19t see it as a tipping establishment. \u8220\'20\'1CI guess it\u8217\'20\'19s how the rich stay rich,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Mimo Guzman, a 28-year-old barista who works a few blocks away, says she watched tips nosedive at her previous employer, a busy \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in Manhattan, after the height of the pandemic. Thankfully, her current employer, Blank Street, offers a wage guarantee: the base pay is $16.50, but if the tips don\u8217\'20\'19t add up to $23 then the company makes up the difference. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t get by on minimum wage, not in this job. We depend on tips,\u8221\'20\'1D says her colleague, 20-year-old Masena Sadeghi.\par \par \uc2 The same applies to delivery workers, who are largely dependent on tips to survive. Gustavo Ajche, the founder of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://losdeliveristasunidos.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Los Deliveristas Unidos}}}\uc2 , a New York-based labor group, says that in a good 12-hour workday on \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , he might make $250. But out of that sum, almost $200 of it might be from tips. Even then, Ajche suspects that \u8220\'20\'1Cthey don\u8217\'20\'19t give us the real amount\u8221\'20\'1D: in 2020 \uc2 DoorDash{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mercurynews.com/2020/11/25/bay-areas-doordash-to-pay-2-5-million-after-being-accused-of-stealing-drivers-tips/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 admitted}}}\uc2  to using customer tips to subsidize workers\u8217\'20\'19 base pay. Though the company claims it has ended that practice, Ajche says he still sees strange discrepancies between the earnings the app promises and what he takes home.\par \par \uc2 Gig companies\u8217\'20\'19 lack of transparency also affects rideshare workers. Nicole Moore, an LA-based \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver and organizer at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.drivers-united.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rideshare Drivers United}}}\uc2  labor group, says platforms like \uc2 Uber\uc2  have been price-gouging passengers without passing additional earnings on to drivers. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause the passengers are paying such high fares, they don\u8217\'20\'19t think the drivers need tips,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut in fact drivers\u8217\'20\'19 earnings are even lower than they used to be.\u8221\'20\'1D A study commissioned by the labor group found that after accounting for the costs of doing business, rideshare workers in California take home just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wired.com/story/california-voted-for-cheaper-uber-rides-it-may-have-hurt-drivers/amp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $6.20 an hour}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s why gig workers are pushing for wage guarantees. The New York City council recently proposed a first-of-its-kind minimum wage for delivery workers of $23.82 an hour, excluding tips \u8211\'20\'13 though Los Deliveristas Unidos has asked for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gothamist.com/news/deliveristas-call-for-5-pay-bump-as-nyc-mulls-minimum-wage-hike-for-app-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $5 more}}}\uc2  to cover the cost of their electric bikes, safety gear and the risk of injury. It\u8217\'20\'19s also a hedge against unpredictable customer behavior: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen economic distress hits, tips are usually the first thing to go,\u8221\'20\'1D says Hildalyn Col\u243\'00\'F3n Hern\u225\'00\'E1ndez, the group\u8217\'20\'19s policy director. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why tips cannot be the income for these workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But there\u8217\'20\'19s at least one group of Americans who are determined to continue tipping well: tipped workers themselves. Tiana, the cafe worker, says she\u8217\'20\'19s recently started bumping up the tips she leaves whenever she makes a purchase \u8211\'20\'13 even if it\u8217\'20\'19s just takeout. She says it\u8217\'20\'19s simple: \u8220\'20\'1CI know what it\u8217\'20\'19s like on the other side.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221206eic6001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
