\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Largest LGBTQ+ health center in US midwest to lay off 15% of staff\b0\par\par\uc2 Mary Retta \par \uc2 888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2022\par 16:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Staff cuts at Chicago community health center come at a time of high-profile attacks on queer and trans healthcare across the US\par \par \uc2 Howard Brown Health, a nonprofit community health center in Chicago that is the largest provider of health and wellness care for the LGBTQ+ community and people living with HIV in the midwest, has announced it wants to lay off at least 100 employees, or about 15% of staff.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs were proposed as voluntary in the first instance, but the company says \u8220\'20\'1Ca reduction in workforce is required\u8221\'20\'1D, suggesting that if 100 volunteers are not found then layoffs will be compulsory.\par \par \uc2 They come at a time of high-profile attacks on queer and trans healthcare across the country. Jobs lost would include roles in behavioral health, health education, PrEp navigation, Covid testing and other teams. Many of the roles are filled by union members, following Howard Brown employees\u8217\'20\'19 decision to form a union in August of this year.\par \par \uc2 After hearing rumors of layoffs, the union raised its concern with management and was told in a written reply on 9 November that \u8220\'20\'1Cthere is no current plan or decision to layoff any union members\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The first indication otherwise was 10 days later, on 19 November. In a letter to employees from the board of directors citing financial strain, Howard Brown outlined a goal \u8220\'20\'1Cto recover $12m in our annual operating budget\u8221\'20\'1D and said it would \u8220\'20\'1Cconsider all available measures\u8221\'20\'1D in order to do so. Union members said they met with management later that day, which is when the organization proposed a \u8220\'20\'1Cvoluntary separation\u8221\'20\'1D package of one week\u8217\'20\'19s severance pay per year of service for employees to leave their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Despite Howard Brown claiming rough financial straits, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/display_990/362894128/download990pdf_07_2022_prefixes_27-39%2F362894128_202106_990_2022071120205028"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data from ProPublica}}}\uc2  shows that in 2021 the organization brought in $30m more than it spent and that the chief executive, David Munar, earned a salary of more than $300,000.\par \par \uc2 Many employees argue that Howard Brown should cut funds elsewhere before considering losing employees. They point to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blockclubchicago.org/2022/03/25/howard-brown-breaks-ground-on-larger-facility-bringing-more-health-care-services-to-northalsted/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 construction of a new $19.5m clinic in North Halsted}}}\uc2 , where Howard Brown already has a clinic. Union members said they are refusing to negotiate until management reveals the 2022 financial books.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs a union member, I would never accept that workers need to quit their jobs if it is not necessary for the organization to stay alive,\u8221\'20\'1D said Margo Gislain, a lead organizer of the Howard Brown Union.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHoward Brown refuses to show us their 2022 financial books and therefore has been unable to convince us that these layoffs are financially necessary. We want to be able to see the organization\u8217\'20\'19s finances so we can make suggestions of other budget cuts that don\u8217\'20\'19t involve losing workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several Howard Brown employees said they find the timing of the layoffs suspicious, coming mere months after the organization unionized. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no doubt in my mind this is retaliatory,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gislain, saying that many of the potential cuts would affect union members, \u8220\'20\'1Cmany of whom were highly involved as organizers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The past few years have seen widespread cuts to queer and trans healthcare across the nation as states ban or limit access to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.teenvogue.com/story/what-gender-affirming-care-actually-means"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gender affirming care}}}\uc2 . As the largest public health center for queer people in the midwest, Howard Brown serves not only the LGBTQ+ population of Chicago but those in surrounding states who travel to receive care.\par \par \uc2 Employees said they are already overworked and understaffed, and fear a smaller workforce would be overwhelmed and unable to provide swift patient care. Shakia Flowers, a behavioral health provider and a member of the union\u8217\'20\'19s bargaining committee representing Englewood\u8217\'20\'19s 63rd Street clinic, said that during her four years at Howard Brown there has always been a wait list for behavioral health treatment. The layoff strategy would cut the behavioral health team in half, leaving only four specialists to serve 30,000 annual patients.\par \par \uc2 Erik Roldan, director of marketing and communications for Howard Brown, blamed \u8220\'20\'1Cdrastic reductions in federal dollars\u8221\'20\'1D for what he said was a $12m revenue gap. \u8220\'20\'1CSpecifically, the agency\u8217\'20\'19s revenue shortfall since the beginning of the new fiscal year July 1 comes from changes to the 340B pharmacy program,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince 2020, 18 pharmaceutical manufacturers \u8211\'20\'13 including \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2 , Merck, and Gilead \u8211\'20\'13 have restricted or eliminated access to 340B priced drugs through contract pharmacy partners, with 8 manufacturers specifically targeting FQHC contract pharmacies. These changes are putting a strain on the agency\u8217\'20\'19s finances, requiring a reduction in operating expenses.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Roldan said the organization was exploring other ways of cutting spending at the same time as layoffs, that no layoffs would occur before 1 January and that they would be subject to bargaining with the union.\par \par \uc2 Howard Brown employees have planned a public protest for Saturday and are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gofundme.com/f/solidarity-fund-hbh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accepting solidarity donations}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese layoffs would deeply impact the communities we\u8217\'20\'19re serving, which are already vulnerable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Flowers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re talking about queer people, Black and brown people, the uninsured, the impoverished. It\u8217\'20\'19s so vitally important for these patients to come here and see providers they can relate to, who can understand their plight, and who can be their support system when they don\u8217\'20\'19t have one.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221201eic100107\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
