\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US House approves bill to block rail strike and mandate paid sick leave\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 729 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2022\par 15:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawmakers vote to impose tentative contract deal on a dozen unions as Bernie Sanders calls for sick-day amendment\par \par \uc2 The House of Representatives on Wednesday voted to approve a bill to block a potentially crippling US rail strike \u8211\'20\'13 but also to mandate paid sick time for the workers.\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , Bernie Sanders, the Vermont independent who caucuses with Democrats, announced that he would object to fast-tracking \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s proposal that Congress impose an industrial settlement, until he can get a roll-call vote on the amendment that would guarantee seven paid sick days for rail workers.\par \par \uc2 The House voted 290-137 to impose a tentative contract deal that had been reached in September, but which four key unions had refused to join, on a dozen unions representing 115,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 The US president, who built a reputation on being pro-labor and put himself {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/30/us-rail-strike-unions-decry-biden-proposal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at loggerheads}}}\uc2  with the unions after asking Congress to avert a strike, had warned of the catastrophic impact of a rail stoppage that could begin as early as 9 December and could cost the US economy about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aar.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/AAR-Rail-Shutdown-Report-September-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $2bn a day}}}\uc2  by some estimates, with chaos hitting freight and passenger traffic.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , and Senate majority leader, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , indicated they would attempt to push through a bill to impose the settlement, albeit expressing reluctance.\par \par \uc2 Workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/30/us-rail-strike-unions-decry-biden-proposal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expressed dismay}}}\uc2  at the stance of Biden and his administration.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the House passed the bill to block the strike and, separately, voted 221-207 to give seven days of paid sick leave to railroad employees, a plan that faces an uncertain fate in the evenly split Senate. Democrats and some Republicans have expressed outrage over the lack of paid short-term sick leave for railroad workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know much more needs to be done for railroad workers,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said ahead of the votes. \u8220\'20\'1CNo one should be at risk of losing his or her job by staying home when sick, needing to see a doctor or getting lifesaving surgery.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A rail strike could freeze almost 30% of US cargo shipments by weight, stoke already surging inflation, cause widespread job losses and strand millions of long-distance \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  passengers and commuter rail services.\par \par \uc2 After the vote, Biden called on the Senate to act \u8220\'20\'1Curgently\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWithout the certainty of a final vote to avoid a shutdown this week, railroads will begin to halt the movement of critical materials like chemicals to clean our drinking water as soon as this weekend,\u8221\'20\'1D he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Asked if Biden supported the separate House measure to require sick leave, the White House press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , said that the president broadly supported paid sick leave for all Americans \u8220\'20\'1Cbut he does not support any bill or amendment that would delay getting this bill to his desk\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Railroad companies and the \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2  oppose amending the contract deal that was struck in September largely on the recommendations of an emergency board appointed by Biden.\par \par \uc2 The influential business lobby group said the sick leave, if passed and signed into law, \u8220\'20\'1Cwould impose an unworkable, one-sided modification to a labor agreement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Biden on Monday praised the proposed contract that includes a 24% compounded pay increase over five years and five annual $1,000 lump-sum payments, and had asked Congress to impose the agreement without any modifications.\par \par \uc2 There are no paid short-term sick days under the tentative deal, after unions asked for 15 and railroads settled on one personal day.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis all could have been avoided had the railroads been willing to provide their employees with a basic protection and what so many Americans already have: paid sick time,\u8221\'20\'1D the House transportation committee chair, \uc2 Peter DeFazio\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 Ian Jefferies, chief executive of the \uc2 Association of American Railroads\uc2 , said House action on sick leave could undermine future collective bargaining and argued the unions had historically bargained for higher overall wages and a more generous long-term leave policy.\par \par \uc2 The contracts cover workers at carriers including \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2 , \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 BNSF\uc2 , \uc2 CSX\uc2 , \uc2 Norfolk Southern Corp\uc2  and \uc2 Kansas City Southern\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The transportation secretary, Pete Buttigieg, and labor secretary, \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2 , are expected to speak to Senate Democrats on Thursday about the rail labor issue.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221201eic10028n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
