\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Congress expected to impose contract on US railroad workers to avert strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 584 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2022\par 13:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Citing \u8216\'20\'18catastrophic\u8217\'20\'19 risk to US economy, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  announces impending vote to bind unions to September negotiations\par \par \uc2 The US House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , has announced that her fellow members of Congress plan to vote this week on imposing a new contract for railroad workers to avert a looming labor strike.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi made the announcement late on Monday afternoon just after \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/nov/28/biden-us-congress-railroad-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called on }}}\uc2  Congress to intervene to prevent a strike, a possibility if an agreement between the freight rail industry and unions is not made by 9 December.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.speaker.gov/newsroom/112822"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  referring to the president\u8217\'20\'19s request, Pelosi said that Democrats were \u8220\'20\'1Creluctant to bypass\u8221\'20\'1D negotiations but \u8220\'20\'1Cwe must act to prevent a catastrophic nationwide rail strike, which would grind our economy to a halt\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The agreement that would be imposed if passed by both congressional chambers comes from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/sep/15/biden-tentative-agreement-freight-rail-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 negotiations}}}\uc2  that were made in September between the rail companies, several unions and the Biden administration. It would entail a 24% raise by 2024, $1,000 in annual bonuses and a cap on healthcare premiums.\par \par \uc2 Four unions \u8211\'20\'13 including the largest rail union, the International Association of Sheet Metal, Air, Rail and Transportation Workers (Smart), which represents more than 28,000 rail workers \u8211\'20\'13 rejected the agreement and had been negotiating with rail companies over the last several weeks. Smart {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/11/21/smart-td-votes-down-labor-deal-as-rails-move-closer-to-national-strike.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 turned down}}}\uc2  the tentative deal with rail management on 21 November, inching closer to a potential strike in December.\par \par \uc2 The dozen rail unions, including those who voted in favor of the September deal, agreed to strike if just one union rejects any agreement and takes the dispute to the picket line. The rail industry has estimated a strike would cost the economy $2bn a day as ground transportation for goods and passengers would be halted.\par \par \uc2 Congress has the ability to impose an agreement on the rail workers to avert a strike, something Democrats have been holding off on to give more room for unions to negotiate with management.\par \par \uc2 The deadlock between management and the unions is mostly over paid sick leave. The union argues that workers should get at least six days of paid sick leave. They are currently expected to use vacation time if they call out sick and are penalized if they take time off without using vacation days. The agreement Congress is considering does not include a sick leave provision.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Biden said that he was \u8220\'20\'1Ca proud pro-labor president\u8221\'20\'1D but that the effects of a strike would be too severe on the US economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere the economic impact of a shutdown would hurt millions of other working people and families \u8211\'20\'13 I believe Congress must use its powers to adopt this deal,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said in his statement. \u8220\'20\'1CSome in Congress want to modify the deal to either improve it for labor or for management. However well-intentioned, any changes would risk delay and a debilitating shutdown.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Railroad Workers Union on Tuesday issued a statement responding to Biden, saying that the president \u8220\'20\'1Cblew it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe had the opportunity to prove his labor-friendly pedigree to millions of workers by simply asking Congress for legislation to end the threat of a national strike on terms more favorable to workers,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement said.\par \par \uc2 In her statement, Pelosi said that the House will take up the agreement \u8220\'20\'1Cwith no poison pills or changes to the negotiated terms\u8221\'20\'1D and will soon send it to the Senate if passed.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221129eibt003eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
