\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Congress to take up bill to avert rail strike as Biden and unions clash \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Joanna Walters in New York \par \uc2 6,497 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 November 2022\par 21:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President urges Congress to impose deal on rail workersSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 As we wrap up this US politics blog for the day, the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  is debating proposed amendments to the bipartisan Respect for Marriage Act that seeks to codify in legislation the right to same sex and interracial marriages. A final vote is expected soon and will be covered in a news story. The politics blog will be back tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where things stand:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 US Senate{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/29/congress-rail-strike-same-sex-marriage-democrats-biden-republicans-live-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-638672d48f082ef70a241523#block-638672d48f082ef70a241523"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to vote}}}\uc2  on legislation codifying federal rights to same-sex and interracial marriage in the US. The upper chamber is debating a bill right now.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A bill{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/29/congress-rail-strike-same-sex-marriage-democrats-biden-republicans-live-updates?page=with:block-638638a68f08ec68b6014727#block-638638a68f08ec68b6014727"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to avert}}}\uc2  the looming US passenger and freight rail strike will be brought to the floor of the House of Representatives early tomorrow, Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said after a meeting at the White House with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the other congressional leaders.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Record early voting is happening in Georgia. The number of people {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/29/congress-rail-strike-same-sex-marriage-democrats-biden-republicans-live-updates?page=with:block-63862d578f08f5eb2e31375d#block-63862d578f08f5eb2e31375d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 casting their ballots}}}\uc2  during early voting in the run-off election for one of the state\u8217\'20\'19s seats in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  is already on its way to half a million since the process got under way at the weekend. Polls close 6 December.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Natoforeign ministers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/29/congress-rail-strike-same-sex-marriage-democrats-biden-republicans-live-updates?page=with:block-6386287f8f08ec68b601459e#block-6386287f8f08ec68b601459e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pledged}}}\uc2  to step up support to Ukraine and help repair its energy infrastructure amid a wave of Russian attacks that have repeatedly knocked out power supplies and heating for millions of Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/29/congress-rail-strike-same-sex-marriage-democrats-biden-republicans-live-updates?page=with:block-638611108f08ec68b6014435#block-638611108f08ec68b6014435"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has urged}}}\uc2  the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  to intervene to prevent the rail strike that is looming across America and could bring passenger and freight trains screeching to a halt as early as next week. This puts the pro-labor president at loggerheads with some of the key rail unions.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.10pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US Senate\uc2  to vote on legislation codifying right to gay and interracial marriage in US\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Senate\uc2  is currently debating proposed amendments to the bipartisan Respect for Marriage Act that seeks to codify in legislation the right to same-sex and interracial marriages in the US.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s expected to pass when it comes to a final vote a bit later this afternoon, from whence it will go back to the House, where it is also expected to pass, and speed its way to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s desk to be signed into law in December.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, 12 Republican senators voted with all Senate Democrats to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/16/same-sex-marriage-bill-us-senate-latest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advance the bill}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bill has Democratic and Republican sponsors and was spearheaded by \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  of Wisconsin, the first openly lesbian or gay senator in the US.\par \par \uc2 Democratic senator from Wisconsin \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , the first out gay US Senator, speaks to the media on the bill to protect same sex marriage in the US Capitol in Washington on Tuesday. Photograph: Jim Lo Scalzo/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 The expected passage of the legislation with support from both parties is an extraordinary sign of the shifting politics on the issue and a measure of relief for the hundreds of thousands of same-sex couples who have married since the Supreme Court\u8217\'20\'19s 2015 Obergefell v Hodges decision that legalized gay marriage nationwide, the Associated Press writes.\par \par \uc2 The bill has gained steady momentum since the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s June decision that overturned the federal right to an abortion, and comments from Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  at the time that suggested same-sex marriage could also come under threat.\par \par \uc2 Bipartisan Senate negotiations kick-started this summer after 47 Republicans unexpectedly voted for a House bill and gave supporters new optimism.\par \par \uc2 The legislation would not codify the Obergefell decision or force any state to allow same-sex couples to marry. But it would require states to recognize all marriages that were legal where they were performed, and protect current same-sex unions, if Obergefell were to be overturned.\par \par \uc2 It would also protect interracial marriages by requiring states to recognize legal marriages regardless of \u8220\'20\'1Csex, race, ethnicity, or national origin\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  today wrestled with a partisan-tinged dispute over a Biden administration policy that would prioritize deportation of people in the country illegally who pose the greatest public safety risk , the Associated Press writes.\par \par \uc2 It was not clear after arguments that stretched past two hours and turned highly contentious at times whether the justices would allow the policy to take effect, or side with Republican-led states that have so far succeeded in blocking it.\par \par \uc2 At the center of the case is a September 2021 directive from the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  that paused deportations unless individuals had committed acts of terrorism, espionage or \u8220\'20\'1Cegregious threats to public safety\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The guidance, issued after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became president, updated a Trump-era policy that removed people in the country illegally regardless of criminal history or community ties.\par \par \uc2 Today, the administration\u8217\'20\'19s top supreme court lawyer told the justices that federal law does \u8220\'20\'1Cnot create an unyielding mandate to apprehend and remove\u8221\'20\'1D every one of the more than 11 million immigrants living in the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 Solicitor General \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2  said it would be \u8220\'20\'1Cincredibly destabilizing on the ground\u8221\'20\'1D for the high court to require that.\par \par \uc2 Congress has not given DHS enough money to vastly increase the number of people it holds and deports, the Biden administration has said.\par \par \uc2 But Texas Solicitor General Judd Stone told the court that the administration violated federal law requiring the detention of people who are in the US illegally and who have been convicted of serious crimes.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  was among the conservative justices who pushed back strongly on the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s arguments.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s our job to say what the law is, not whether or not it can be possibly implemented or whether there are difficulties there, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t think we should change that responsibility just because Congress and the executive can\u8217\'20\'19t agree on something... I don\u8217\'20\'19t think we should let them off the hook,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Yet Roberts, in questioning Stone, also called Prelogar\u8217\'20\'19s argument compelling.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 , made clear they believed that Texas and Louisiana, which joined Texas in suing over the directive, weren\u8217\'20\'19t even entitled to bring their case.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is dependent on Congress to avoid a government shutdown on December 16, the president wants a government funding bill passed to provide additional money for the Covid-19 response and to bolster US support for Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s economy and defense against Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion, the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers are months behind on passing funding legislation for the current fiscal year, relying on stop-gap measures that largely maintain existing funding levels, that federal agencies have warned leaves them strapped for cash.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re going to work together, I hope, to fund the government,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden told lawmakers, emphasizing the importance of Ukraine and pandemic funding as well.\par \par \uc2 Meeting in the Roosevelt Room at the White House earlier, Biden sat at the head of the conference table, flanked on either side by Democratic House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , the two smiling brightly at the start of the meeting.\par \par \uc2 Republican House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy sat next to Schumer, and Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was next to Pelosi and appeared more reserved.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe 2022 election, summed. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4ZyxVUcigO"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/4ZyxVUcigO}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sahil Kapur (@sahilkapur) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sahilkapur/status/1597644594443079685?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 29, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 As the meeting began, Biden quipped, \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m sure this is going to go very quickly\u8221\'20\'1D to reach agreement on everything.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers spent a bit more than an hour with the president, who was joined by Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and senior aides.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy is working to become speaker in January, though he must first overcome dissent within the GOP conference to win a floor vote on January 3.\par \par \uc2 All the leaders said their preference was to pass a comprehensive spending bill for the fiscal year, rather than a continuing resolution (CR) that largely maintains existing funding levels.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we don\u8217\'20\'19t have an option we may have to have a yearlong\u8221\'20\'1D stop-gap bill, Pelosi added.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy, who has promised to look more critically at the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s requests for Ukraine aid, told reporters that, \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not for a blank check for anything.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He said he wasn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily opposed to more funding, but wanted to ensure \u8220\'20\'1Cthere\u8217\'20\'19s accountability and audits.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Schumer and Pelosi popped out of the west wing after the meeting to take questions from reporters and were followed shortly afterwards by McCarthy who did the same.\par \par \uc2 On a spending bill, Pelosi said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to have a bipartisan agreement on what the top line is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 CNN reported that McConnell eschewed such an appearance and returned directly to Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 enltrSCHUMER calls the White House meeting among Hill leaders "a very productive discussion about funding the government \u8212\'20\'14 we all agreed that it should be done this year."\par \par \uc2 PELOSI says if they can't reach a deal, "we may have to have a year-long CR." She says they don't want that. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/fEFiBOiQgY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/fEFiBOiQgY}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sahil Kapur (@sahilkapur) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sahilkapur/status/1597634356293402624?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 29, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Interim summary\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been a lively morning in US politics and there\u8217\'20\'19s more to come. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is en route to Michigan to tour a factory and talk about the economy and the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  is poised to vote on a bill codifying same-sex and interracial marriage.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s where things stand:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A bill{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/29/congress-rail-strike-same-sex-marriage-democrats-biden-republicans-live-updates?page=with:block-638638a68f08ec68b6014727#block-638638a68f08ec68b6014727"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to avert}}}\uc2  the looming US passenger and freight rail strike will be brought to the floor of the House of Representatives early tomorrow, Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said after a meeting at the White House with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the other congressional leaders.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Record early voting is happening in Georgia. The number of people {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/29/congress-rail-strike-same-sex-marriage-democrats-biden-republicans-live-updates?page=with:block-63862d578f08f5eb2e31375d#block-63862d578f08f5eb2e31375d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 casting their ballots}}}\uc2  during early voting in the run-off election for one of the state\u8217\'20\'19s seats in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  is already on its way to half a million since the process got under way at the weekend. Polls close 6 December.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Natoforeign ministers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/29/congress-rail-strike-same-sex-marriage-democrats-biden-republicans-live-updates?page=with:block-6386287f8f08ec68b601459e#block-6386287f8f08ec68b601459e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pledged}}}\uc2  to step up support to Ukraine and help repair its energy infrastructure amid a wave of Russian attacks that have repeatedly knocked out power supplies and heating for millions of Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/29/congress-rail-strike-same-sex-marriage-democrats-biden-republicans-live-updates?page=with:block-638611108f08ec68b6014435#block-638611108f08ec68b6014435"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has urged}}}\uc2  the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  to intervene to prevent the rail strike that is looming across America and could bring passenger and freight trains screeching to a halt as early as next week. This puts the pro-labor president at loggerheads with some of the key rail unions.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is on his way to Michigan, aboard Air Force One right now, to tour the SK Siltron CSS semiconductor facility in Bay City, on the shore of Lake Huron.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s part of his agenda to promote progress in rebuilding the US manufacturing sector.\par \par \uc2 A local ABC channel described how \uc2 SK Siltron\uc2  recently completed a $300m expansion. The firm makes semiconductor wafers \u8220\'20\'1Cused in power system components for electric vehicles and 5G cellular technology,\u8221\'20\'1D the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.abc12.com/news/politics/president-biden-coming-to-bay-city-on-tuesday-for-speech-at-sk-siltron-css/article_d77c86e6-6f3f-11ed-9786-c773306c340f.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outlet reported}}}\uc2  ahead of the president\u8217\'20\'19s visit this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The ABC report noted that \u8220\'20\'1Clocal, state and federal leaders hailed the project as an example of the US bringing semiconductor manufacturing back home during a crippling supply shortage of the devices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s due to speak about the US economy a bit later. He\u8217\'20\'19s being accompanied on the factory tour by newly-reelected Michigan governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  and others.\par \par \uc2 A serious-faced US president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  waves while boarding Air Force One at Joint Base Andrews in Maryland today, en route to Michigan. Photograph: Jim Watson/AFP/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  compared \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to a gargoyle \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Hats off to Politico for gathering this reporting. The outlet reports that senior Republicans \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and \uc2 John Thune\uc2  all directly or obliquely criticized Trump\u8217\'20\'19s meeting with the far right\u8217\'20\'19s Nick Fuentes last week, as senators returned to Capitol Hill after the Thanksgiving break.\par \par \uc2 But it noted this choice comment, that Utah Republican Senator and former presidential candidate \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  was \u8220\'20\'1Cparticularly sharp\u8221\'20\'1D on Trump, in general, and noted that he was not a fan of the former president running for office again, as he intends to in 2024 and said: \u8220\'20\'1CI certainly don\u8217\'20\'19t want him hanging over our party like a gargoyle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One of Washington National Cathedral\u8217\'20\'19s 112 whimsical and fearsome gargoyles, which serves as a drain spout to push rain water away from the building, Photograph: Manuel Balce Ceneta/AP\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s NBC:\par \par \uc2 enltrRomney on Trump: \u8220\'20\'1CI voted to remove him from office twice\u8230\'20\'26 I don't think he should be president of the United states. I don't think he should be the nominee of our party in 2024. And I certainly don't want him hanging over our party like a gargoyle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a character issue.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sahil Kapur (@sahilkapur) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sahilkapur/status/1597361249532071940?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 28, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 House Republican leader and would-be next speaker Kevin McCarthy has spoken out for the first time to condemn the meeting between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and blatant white supremacist and antisemite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/28/donald-trump-shied-away-from-criticising-nick-fuentes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nick Fuentes}}}\uc2  last week.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy spoke about several topics as he emerged from the west wing of the White House a little earlier, following a meeting called there by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with Democratic and Republican congressional leaders to talk about urgent legislative business before the year end.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think anybody should be spending any time with Nick Fuentes. He has no place in this Republican Party,\u8221\'20\'1D McCarthy told reporters at the White House.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy is the latest GOP figure to speak out, following a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/27/republican-condemns-trump-dinner-with-white-supremacist"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 series of senior Republicans}}}\uc2  and pressure group leaders condemning the fact that Trump had dinner last week with Ye, the rapper formerly known as Kanye West, who is in deep controversy for antisemitic remarks, and Fuentes, who accompanied Ye.\par \par \uc2 As the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/edwin-rios"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Edwin Rios}}}\uc2  noted it was just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/26/trump-nick-fuentes-dinner-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the latest}}}\uc2  in a long line of incidents involving the former US president and the far right.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy did stumble though. He said that Trump had four times condemned Fuentes and did not know who he was.\par \par \uc2 Reporters on the scene immediately pounced to note, accurately, that Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/28/donald-trump-shied-away-from-criticising-nick-fuentes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has not condemned}}}\uc2  Fuentes and his racist views.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy responded: \u8220\'20\'1CWell, I condemn.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, Arkansas governor \uc2 Asa Hutchinson{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/27/republican-condemns-trump-dinner-with-white-supremacist"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  the meeting between Trump, Ye and Fuentes \u8220\'20\'1Cwas not accidental.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Moments earlier, when asked if it was appropriate for Trump to meet with Ye, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mediaite.com/news/watch-kevin-mccarthy-denounces-trump-meeting-with-kanye-literally-two-seconds-after-saying-kanye-fine-fuentes-bad/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 McCarthy said}}}\uc2  Trump could have meetings \u8220\'20\'1Cwith who he wants.\u8221\'20\'1D Then went onto criticize Fuentes.\par \par \uc2 enltrWATCH: Kevin Mccarthy Denounces Trump Meeting With Kanye Literally Two Seconds After Saying Kanye Fine, Fuentes Bad {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/hnT6lpKWc1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/hnT6lpKWc1}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Mediaite (@Mediaite) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Mediaite/status/1597647872522321923?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 29, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 But also Ye, sort of?\par \par \uc2 enltrMcCarthy: "The president can have meetings with who he wants. I don't think anybody though should have meetings with Nick Fuentes. And his views are nowhere within the R Party or within this country itself."\par \par \uc2 And Kanye?\par \par \uc2 "I don't think he should have associated with him as well."\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sahil Kapur (@sahilkapur) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sahilkapur/status/1597637976808640512?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 29, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bill to avert looming rail strike to reach House floor\par \par \uc2 Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  just emerged from the White House to talk about their meeting just now with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to talk about legislation in the lame duck session and, most urgently, his request that Congress intervene to stop the looming rail strike.\par \par \uc2 Schumer signaled the Senate would support the move.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi said: \u8220\'20\'1CTomorrow morning we will have a bill on the floor, it will come up as early as 9am.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden wants Congress to impose the agreement tentatively reached in September, but which four unions didn\u8217\'20\'19t sign on to, forcing the president and the labor unions to be at loggerheads.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi said the original elements of the agreement, on pay, etc, would be included in the bill and some \u8220\'20\'1Cadditional benefits\u8221\'20\'1D agreed to by Biden and labor secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She said the agreement \u8220\'20\'1Cis not everything I would like to see, I would like to see paid sick leave \u8211\'20\'13 every [leading democratic] country in the world has it. I don\u8217\'20\'19t like going against the ability of a union to strike but, weighing the equities, we must avoid a strike.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Assuming the House votes for the bill, it will then move to the Senate for a vote there.\par \par \uc2 Schumer said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe will try to get it done \u8230\'20\'26 we are going to try to solve this ASAP.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Both leaders warned of job losses and further supply chain problems affecting ordinary goods and essential things such as chlorine for safe public water supplies.\par \par \uc2 enltrSchumer and Pelosi, speaking to reporters call it a \u8220\'20\'1Cproductive meeting,\u8221\'20\'1D Finding a solution to rail strike a top priority, they say. \u8220\'20\'1CWe must avoid a strike,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi says. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/cK0HwSCcXy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/cK0HwSCcXy}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Myah Ward (@MyahWard) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MyahWard/status/1597633755342307328?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 29, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 House minority leader Kevin McCarthy emerged from the West Wing a few minutes after Schumer and Pelosi spoke to gathered reporters and indicated that he expected a resolution on the rail strike.\par \par \uc2 Schumer had earlier noted that he had minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s support in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 All 100 senators must agree to hold a quick vote like this and it\u8217\'20\'19s unclear yet if all are on board, especially \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrAsked if he will allow a vote on legislation to avert the rail strike to happen by the Dec. 9 deadline, \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  just told me: \u8220\'20\'1CWe will have more to say about that later.\u8221\'20\'1D He criticized the deal for lack of paid sick leave. \u8220\'20\'1CThat is outrageous.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Manu Raju (@mkraju) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mkraju/status/1597613906159181827?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 29, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Despite the extensive efforts of progressive organizers in Georgia, the state\u8217\'20\'19s early voting operation has run into some significant issues.\par \par \uc2 Many voters reported long lines at polling places over the weekend, as they tried to cast ballots in Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s Senate runoff election.\par \par \uc2 One of the candidates in that race, Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , the incumbent, waited in line for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LaleeIbssa/status/1596964522828656641"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about an hour on Sunday}}}\uc2  to cast his vote.\par \par \uc2 A coalition of progressive groups has launched a massive canvassing operation to help ensure that voters know how and when they can cast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 Voters line up to cast their ballots on November 26, 2022 in Decatur, Georgia. Photograph: Justin Sullivan/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 Hillary Holley, executive director of the progressive group Care in Action, said that canvassers have encountered a lot of misunderstanding among voters as they knock on doors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery time basically our canvassers reach a voter at their house, they\u8217\'20\'19re saying, \u8216\'20\'18Thank you so much because we are so confused about when we can go vote,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Holley said on a Monday press call.\par \par \uc2 Part of that confusion stems from a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/11/18/georgia-senate-runoff-saturday-voting-ruling/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 judge\u8217\'20\'19s last-minute ruling}}}\uc2  that counties could allow early voting to occur on the Saturday after the Thanksgiving holiday.\par \par \uc2 Georgia election officials had initially said that early voting could not take place on that day, but the Warnock campaign won a legal challenge to expand voting hours.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Jackson Ali, policy director of the progressive group New Georgia Project, said: \u8220\'20\'1COur call is for counties to continue the fight to get more locations open, to continue the fight to keep your counties open late, and for our voters to stay in line.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This combination of photos shows, Sen. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , D-Ga., speaking to reporters on Capitol Hill in Washington, Aug. 3, 2021, left, and Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  speaking in Perry, Ga., Sept. 25, 2021. Photograph: Amanda Andrade-Rhoades/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Progressives in Georgia are leaving nothing to chance with just one week to go before polls close in the state\u8217\'20\'19s Senate runoff election.\par \par \uc2 A coalition of progressive groups has launched a massive canvassing effort for the Democratic party since election day, when neither Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  nor Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  were able to win enough support to avoid a runoff.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the coalition, known as Georgia Organizers for Active Transformation, said on a Monday press call that they now have 2,500 canvassers knocking on 200,000 doors a day.\par \par \uc2 In total, the canvassers have knocked on more than 2.5 million doors since the Thursday after election day, which was three weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Progressives\u8217\'20\'19 work already appears to be paying dividends. More than 150,000 Georgians cast their ballots over the weekend, as early voting got underway, and there was record turnout on Monday, the first day of statewide early voting.\par \par \uc2 According to the progressive group Progress Georgia, African Americans and women are currently outpacing their high turnout levels in the 2020 general election.\par \par \uc2 Given that those constituencies lean toward Democrats, the early voting data could provide some reassurance to the camp of Warnock as he defends the seat he has only held since 2021.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGeorgia voters know exactly what\u8217\'20\'19s going on,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hillary Holley, a coalition leader and the executive director of Care in Action. \u8220\'20\'1CThey know what the stakes are, and they want Warnock to remain representing them for six additional years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  in Decatur, Georgia, at the weekend. Photograph: Justin Kase Photography/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Record early voting in Georgia Senate seat runoff\par \par \uc2 The number of people casting their ballots during early voting in the run-off election for one of Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s seats in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  is already on its way to half a million since the process got under way at the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Monday saw a record turnout, with reports varying from more than 250,000 voters to in excess of 300,000 people voting on the first day of state-wide early operation of the polls (some counties began earlier).\par \par \uc2 Incumbent Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and his Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  are neck-and-neck ahead of the election on 6 December.\par \par \uc2 Deputy secretary of state \uc2 Gabriel Sterling\uc2  said it was the largest in-person early voting day in Georgia history.\par \par \uc2 enltrJust...WOW! GA voters, facilitated through the hard work of county election & poll workers, have shattered the old Early Vote turnout, with 300,438 Georgians casting their votes today. They blew up the old record of 233k votes in a day. Way to go voters & election workers. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/gapol?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #gapol}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/rYbmpjAs43"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/rYbmpjAs43}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Gabriel Sterling\uc2  (@GabrielSterling) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/GabrielSterling/status/1597414255523160064?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 29, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Neither candidate got above the 50% threshold in the midterm elections earlier this month and under Georgia rules the fierce race went to a runoff.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A quick pool report from Washington: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  have been meeting with congressional leaders in the Roosevelt Room at the White House, moments ago.\par \par \uc2 The president made brief remarks but did not take shouted questions from the pool, your pooler notes.\par \par \uc2 I asked for top leaders in Congress to come in and talk about what we\u8217\'20\'19re going to do between now and Christmas. There\u8217\'20\'19s a lot to do, including resolving the train strike,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said.\par \par \uc2 He also mentioned funding the federal government, the ongoing coronavirus response and the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And we\u8217\'20\'19re going to find other areas of common ground,\u8221\'20\'1D the president added.\par \par \uc2 Pate-spotters may or may not be able to identify all the people in the room from this pic tweeted by pooler. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  next to Veep, surely? At right, the inscrutable \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrBiden meets with congressional leaders. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/6E1biP5g0U"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/6E1biP5g0U}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 alexander nazaryan (@alexnazaryan) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/alexnazaryan/status/1597616834513145863?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 29, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US and Nato allies pledge continued support for Ukraine\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/nato"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nato}}}\uc2  foreign ministers today pledged to step up support to Ukraine and help repair its energy infrastructure amid a wave of Russian attacks that have repeatedly knocked out power supplies and heating for millions of Ukrainians, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s aggression, including its persistent and unconscionable attacks on Ukrainian civilian and energy infrastructure, is depriving millions of Ukrainians of basic human services,\u8221\'20\'1D the foreign ministers said in a statement after a first day of talks in Bucharest, Romania.\par \par \uc2 They condemned what they called Russia\u8217\'20\'19s cruelty against Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s civilians and promised to assist the country as it repairs its energy infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 We will continue and further step up political and practical support to Ukraine as it continues to defend its sovereignty and territorial integrity...and will maintain our support for as long as necessary,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement noted.\par \par \uc2 Foreign ministers also confirmed a 2008 Nato summit decision that Ukraine will eventually become a member of the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2  defense alliance.\par \par \uc2 Since then, however, leaders have not taken any concrete steps such as giving Kyiv a membership action plan that would lay out a timetable for bringing the country closer to Nato.\par \par \uc2 A woman is looking at her mobile phone during the Nato foreign ministers meeting held at Parliament Palace in Bucharest, Romania, on Tuesday. Photograph: Robert Ghement/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, much as we would hate to lose any eyeballs from the US politics blog, we must point out two other Guardian staples that readers may wish to follow or toggle between.\par \par \uc2 For updates on the war, our global {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/series/ukraine-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ukraine live blog}}}\uc2  is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/nov/29/russia-ukraine-war-live-nato-foreign-ministers-meet-in-romania-us-to-announce-substantial-aid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian flag flatters at half mast near the Ukrainian Motherland monument in Kyiv, Ukraine, on Tuesday. Photograph: Bernat Armangu\u233\'00\'E9/AP\par \par \uc2 And it has not escaped our attention that at 2pm ET today, the US is playing Iran in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/world-cup-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 soccer World Cup}}}\uc2  in Qatar, amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/nov/29/berhalter-denies-gio-reyna-conspiracy-claims-as-usa-iran-match-approaches"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 diplomatic hoo-ha}}}\uc2 . That match will be liveblogged whistle by whistle by our sports team closer to kick-off. Read about the build-up in the general competition blog {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2022/nov/29/world-cup-2022-countdown-to-wales-v-england-plus-iran-v-usa-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Talking up labor unions as a senator or a candidate or when you\u8217\'20\'19re president and opening a factory is one thing but facing a national rail strike as the festive seasons looms is quite another \u8211\'20\'13 especially for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s some interesting perspective directly from Politico. The outlet writes in this morning\u8217\'20\'19s, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/newsletters/playbook/2022/11/29/why-union-joe-put-the-screws-to-rail-workers-00071088?cid=hptb_primary_0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Politico Playbook dispatch}}}\uc2  from the front lines in Washington:\par \par \uc2 In 1992, two days into a crippling railroad strike , then-Senator \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came to the Senate floor and decried the lopsided nature of federal labor laws dealing with the rail industry \u8211\'20\'13 laws, he argued, that essentially allowed corporations, regulators and, ultimately, Congress to run roughshod over workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to restore a measure of balance to these negotiations,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, before voting with just five other senators against halting the strike.\par \par \uc2 Thirty years later, as president, Biden is turning to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://go.politicoemail.com/?qs=4c3b91b36a4e47f50fef902f3481644cd2ff54a3d3647439231625138a1857da9a4b01056fa97a236f272e1d9d6bd0c0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 those very same laws}}}\uc2  to prevent another strike and impose a tentative contract agreement that his administration brokered but multiple rail unions voted to reject.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C As a proud pro-labor president, I am reluctant to override the ratification procedures and the views of those who voted against the agreement,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CBut in this case \u8211\'20\'13 where the economic impact of a shutdown would hurt millions of other working people and families \u8211\'20\'13 I believe Congress must use its powers to adopt this deal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Translation: It\u8217\'20\'19s a lot easier to be \u8220\'20\'1CUnion Joe\u8221\'20\'1D as one senator among 100 than it is as president of the United States \u8211\'20\'13 especially during holiday shopping season and a persistent bout of inflation.\par \par \uc2 People familiar with the process told us last night that Biden and his advisers determined that the risk to the economy was just too great. A strike would disrupt supply chains \u8211\'20\'13 including such critical goods as motor fuels and water treatment chemicals \u8211\'20\'13 and could ultimately {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://go.politicoemail.com/?qs=4c3b91b36a4e47f59d218157cd6236852a95b63ca0afb4b4e28a1162a319366a4d8bde2a4c26d37e7ba656df57ac8cb7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cost the U.S. economy $1 billion}}}\uc2  within a week, according to analysis from the Anderson Economic Group \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26 Until Monday, administration officials remained engaged in resolving the dispute. But Biden [he was advised by Cabinet folks that] there is no path to resolve the dispute at the bargaining table and recommended he ask Congress to impose the September deal \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26 One former [unnamed] labor department official told Politico:\par \par \uc2 There is a sentiment among some railroad workers that they thought the president was going to absolutely battle all the way to the end to get them exactly what they wanted. He\u8217\'20\'19s going to have some unhappy people. I think there\u8217\'20\'19s going to be some grumbling in the labor movement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The former official was hopeful that Biden\u8217\'20\'19s pro-union history couldn\u8217\'20\'19t and wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be overshadowed by this one decision. That remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 Freight train cars sit in a \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  rail yard on 14 September in Atlanta. Businesses are increasingly worried about the renewed threat of a railroad strike after two unions rejected their deals, and they want the White House and Congress to be ready to intervene. Photograph: Danny Karnik/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Workers in four unions, including the largest, have rejected the tentative rail industry deal, while workers in eight unions have approved it, Reuters\u8217\'20\'19 reporting continues.\par \par \uc2 Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2 , Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  and Agriculture Secretary Tom Vilsack have been involved in discussions with the rail industry, unions and agriculture industry stakeholders.\par \par \uc2 File photo: U.S. Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  (L) speaks as Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Transport Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  and Director of the White House Office of Public Engagement \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2  look on at the first meeting of the White House Task Force on Worker Organizing and Empowerment in Washington in May 2021. Photograph: Jonathan Ernst/Reuters\par \par \uc2 Senator Roger Wicker, the top Republican on the commerce committee, praised Biden\u8217\'20\'19s call to Congress to act and said no one side was fully happy with the compromise contract deal \u8220\'20\'1Cbut the responsible thing to do is avoid the strike.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Association of American Railroads\uc2  said \u8220\'20\'1Ccongressional action to prevent a work stoppage in this manner is appropriate... No one benefits from a rail work stoppage \u8211\'20\'13 not our customers, not rail employees and not the American economy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a letter yesterday, the US Chamber of Commerce, National Association of Manufacturers, \uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2 , \uc2 American Petroleum Institute\uc2 , \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2 , \uc2 American Trucking Associations\uc2  and other groups warned that impacts of a potential strike could be felt as soon as 5 December.\par \par \uc2 Biden said Congress\par \par \uc2 Should set aside politics and partisan division and deliver for the American people. Congress should get this bill to my desk well in advance of December 9th so we can avoid disruption.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The letter to congressional leaders, first reported by Reuters, warned that a strike could halt passenger railroad \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  and commuter rail services that \u8220\'20\'1Cwould disrupt up to 7 million travelers a day\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It added:\par \par \uc2 The risks to our nation\u8217\'20\'19s economy and communities simply make a national rail strike unacceptable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s Presidential Emergency Board in August released the framework for the tentative deal forged in September between major railroads and a dozen unions representing 115,000 workers. Those carriers include \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2 , \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway Inc\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 BNSF\uc2 , \uc2 CSX\uc2 , \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  and \uc2 Kansas City Southern\uc2 . Unions and railroads have until Dec. 9 to resolve differences. If they do not, workers could strike or railroads could lock out employees - unless Congress intervenes. But railroads would halt hazardous materials shipments at least four days ahead of a strike deadline.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  last night called on Congress to intervene to avert a potential rail strike that could occur as early as 9 December , warning of a catastrophic economic impact if railroad service ground to a halt, Reuters writes.\par \par \uc2 Biden asked lawmakers to adopt the tentative deal announced in September \u8220\'20\'1Cwithout any modifications or delay - to avert a potentially crippling national rail shutdown\u8221\'20\'1D and added that up to 765,000 Americans \u8220\'20\'1Ccould be put out of work in the first two weeks alone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said lawmakers would take up legislation this week \u8220\'20\'1Cto prevent a catastrophic nationwide rail strike, which would grind our economy to a halt\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , leaves the 18th annual Interfaith Thanksgiving Prayer Service at St Mark\u8217\'20\'19s Lutheran church after speaking earlier this month in San Francisco. Photograph: Lea Suzuki/AP\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, more than 400 groups called on Congress to intervene in the railroad labor standoff that threatens to idle shipments of food and fuel and strand travelers while inflicting billions of dollars of economic damage.\par \par \uc2 A rail traffic stoppage could freeze almost 30% of US cargo shipments by weight, stoke inflation and cost the American economy as much as $2bn a day by unleashing a cascade of transport woes affecting US energy, agriculture, manufacturing, healthcare and retail sectors.\par \par \uc2 A rail shutdown would devastate our economy. Without freight rail, many U.S. industries would shut down... Communities could lose access to chemicals necessary to ensure clean drinking water. Farms and ranches across the country could be unable to feed their livestock,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said in a statement last night.\par \par \uc2 The president hailed the contract deal that includes a 24% compounded wage increase over a five-year period from 2020 through 2024 and five annual $1,000 lump-sum payments.\par \par \uc2 A view of the Daniel Patrick Moynihan Train Hall in New York City after negotiators reached a tentative deal in September in an attempt to resolve plans for passenger and freight rail strikes. But with some key unions not signing up, strikes now loom and the White House wants Congress to intervene. Photograph: Anadolu Agency/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is convening congressional leaders to discuss legislative priorities through the end of the year, according to the White House.\par \par \uc2 There are no further details yet from a snap report from the Reuters news agency but we\u8217\'20\'19ll bring you the news as it develops.\par \par \uc2 The urgent rail industry matter notwithstanding, our Washington colleague {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/lauren-gambino"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lauren Gambino}}}\uc2  examines what the still-Democratic controlled Congress can get done in this \u8220\'20\'1Clame duck\u8221\'20\'1D session before Republicans take charge of the House with a slim majority early next year.\par \par \uc2 Lauren writes that: As a new era of divided government looms in the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  are rushing to complete a lengthy legislative to-do list that includes landmark civil liberties legislation, a routine but critical spending package and a bill to prevent another January 6.\par \par \uc2 There are only a handful of working days left before the balance of power in Congress shifts and Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 unified control of government in Washington ends. In January, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  will claim the gavel in the House, giving them veto power over much of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s agenda.\par \par \uc2 You can read the rest of her analysis report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/26/congress-democrats-lame-duck-debt-ceiling-election-legislation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Balcony overlooking the South Portico sports holiday decorations at the White House. Photograph: Michael Brochstein/SOPA Images/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Biden at loggerheads with unions over push to stop rail strike\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has urged the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  to intervene to prevent the rail strike that is looming across America and could bring passenger and freight trains screeching to a halt as early as next week.\par \par \uc2 The US president warns that a rail strike will put more than three quarters of a million Americans out of work very quickly \u8211\'20\'13 and one expert analysis estimates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/11/23/economy/us-freight-railroad-strike-costs/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it would cost}}}\uc2  the economy $1bn in the first week.\par \par \uc2 But asking Congress to force through a tentative agreement reached in September that\u8217\'20\'19s supported by some \u8211\'20\'13 but very much not by all \u8211\'20\'13 the rail unions involved puts him at loggerheads with organized labor in the US.\par \par \uc2 That goes sharply against his track record as a vociferous supporter of unions through thick and thin over decades and a reputation as perhaps {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/02/joe-biden-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the most pro-labor president}}}\uc2  in US history.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will support action from Congress, to avert strikes from December 9, while House majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has not placed his cards down yet. Labor secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  helped mediate negotiations between rail companies and unions earlier in the fall. Four key unions refused to sign up.\par \par \uc2 Will Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s socialist and independent US Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , who caucuses with the Democrats, support a government-imposed resolution?\par \par \uc2 enltrAt this critical moment for our economy, in the holiday season, we cannot hurl the U.S. into a devastating rail freight shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Congress should get this bill to my desk as soon as possible so we can avoid disruption.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden (@POTUS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1597383594372128768?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 29, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Biden clashes with unions as he urges Congress to derail train strike\par \par \uc2 Good Morning, US politics live blog readers, the White House is a hive of activity, it\u8217\'20\'19s early voting a go-go in Georgia and there\u8217\'20\'19s a lively day in store on Capitol Hill, so stay with us for all the news as it happens.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what afoot:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has always championed labor unions in America, whether fashionable or not, but now that he\u8217\'20\'19s president he\u8217\'20\'19s staring at a holiday-season rail strike that he says threatens the economy \u8211\'20\'13 and he\u8217\'20\'19s urged Congress to intervene and prevent it by imposing a deal some unions hate.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The US president is traveling to Michigan today to tour a semiconductor facility as he seeks to promote his agenda, especially in swing states, of boosting manufacturing in the US and his stewardship of the economy.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 More than 400,000 voters have already cast their ballots since early voting began in the Georgia midterms run-off for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  seat held since the 2020 election by Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . He\u8217\'20\'19s neck-and-neck with Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . The election\u8217\'20\'19s next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Mike Lindell, he of foamy pillows and foaming-at-the-mouth promotion of the lie that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the 2020 presidential election, is running to become the chair of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  ( \uc2 RNC\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  has compared Trump to a gargoyle.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221129eibt00236\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
