\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Biden asks \uc2 US Congress\uc2  to block railroad strike that could \u8216\'20\'18devastate economy\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Dominic Rushe and agencies \par \uc2 728 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 November 2022\par 13:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 With 9 December deadline fast approaching, business groups also push \uc2 US government\uc2  to intervene in labor dispute before holidays\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  called on Congress to intervene and block a railroad strike before next month\u8217\'20\'19s deadline in the stalled contract talks, saying a strike would \u8220\'20\'1Cdevastate our economy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s move comes as business groups have warned that the looming strike would hit just before the holiday season and worsen the US\u8217\'20\'19s inflation problems.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLet me be clear: a rail shutdown would devastate our economy,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CWithout freight rail, many US industries would shut down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The strike comes after long-running negotiations reached an impasse and both sides agreed to a cooling-off period that ends next week.\par \par \uc2 Congress has the power to impose contract terms on the workers, but it\u8217\'20\'19s not clear what lawmakers might include if they do. They could also force the negotiations to continue into the new year.\par \par \uc2 Both the unions and railroads have been lobbying Congress while contract talks continue. Four rail unions that represent more than half of the 115,000 workers in the industry have rejected the deals that Biden helped broker before the original strike deadline in September and are back at the table trying to work out new agreements. Eight other unions have approved their five-year deals with the railroads and are in the process of getting back pay for their workers for the 24% raises that are retroactive to 2020.\par \par \uc2 Last month the Biden administration said it was up to unions and the rail companies to reach an agreement. In his statement Biden said that as \u8220\'20\'1Ca proud pro-labor president\u8221\'20\'1D he was reluctant to override the views of people who voted against the agreement. \u8220\'20\'1CBut in this case \u8211\'20\'13 where the economic impact of a shutdown would hurt millions of other working people and families \u8211\'20\'13 I believe Congress must use its powers to adopt this deal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s remarks came after a coalition of more than 400 business groups sent a letter to congressional leaders on Monday urging them to step into the stalled talks because of fears about the devastating potential impact of a strike that could force many businesses to shut down if they cannot get the rail deliveries they need. Commuter railroads and \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  would also be affected in a strike because many of them use tracks owned by the freight railroads.\par \par \uc2 The business groups, led by the \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2 , the National \uc2 Association of Manufacturers\uc2  and the \uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2 , said even a short-term strike would have a tremendous impact and the economic pain would start to be felt even before the 9 December strike deadline. They said the railroads would stop hauling hazardous chemicals, fertilizers and perishable goods up to a week beforehand to keep those products from being stranded somewhere along the tracks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA potential rail strike only adds to the headwinds facing the US economy,\u8221\'20\'1D the businesses wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CA rail stoppage would immediately lead to supply shortages and higher prices. The cessation of \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  and commuter rail services would disrupt up to 7 million travelers a day. Many businesses would see their sales disrupted right in the middle of the critical holiday shopping season.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \uc2 Association of American Railroads\uc2  (\uc2 AAR\uc2 ) trade group praised Biden\u8217\'20\'19s action. \u8220\'20\'1CNo one benefits from a rail work stoppage \u8211\'20\'13 not our customers, not rail employees and not the American economy,\u8221\'20\'1D said the \uc2 AAR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s president and chief executive, Ian Jefferies. \u8220\'20\'1CNow is the appropriate time for Congress to pass legislation to implement the agreements already ratified by eight of the 12 unions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The unions have asked the railroads to consider adding paid sick time to what they already offered to address some of the workers\u8217\'20\'19 quality-of-life concerns. But so far, the railroads, which include Union Pacific, \uc2 BNSF\uc2 , \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 , \uc2 CSX\uc2  and \uc2 Kansas City Southern\uc2 , have refused to consider that.\par \par \uc2 The railroads want any deal to closely follow the recommendations a Biden-appointed special board of arbitrators made this summer that called for the 24% raises and $5,000 in bonuses but did not resolve workers\u8217\'20\'19 concerns about demanding schedules they say make it hard to take a day off and other working conditions.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press contributed\par \par \uc2 reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221129eibt001uw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
