\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  worker\u8217\'20\'19s grisly foundry death blamed on training and work conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,162 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 November 2022\par 09:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers say conditions at Illinois facility haven\u8217\'20\'19t changed since Steven Dierkes was incinerated in pot of molten iron in June\par \par \uc2 Just nine days into his new job at \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s foundry in Mapleton, Illinois, Steven Dierkes, a 39-year-old father of three, fell into an 11ft-deep pot of molten iron and was incinerated.\par \par \uc2 Now workers at the plant are blaming lack of training, poor safety protections and grueling working conditions for his death and are threatening strike action at the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest construction equipment manufacturer.\par \par \uc2 Dierkes\u8217\'20\'19 death in June was the subject of a report issued by the \uc2 US Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  (Osha) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.osha.gov/news/newsreleases/region5/11092022-0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earlier this month}}}\uc2 . The report determined that \u8220\'20\'1C??if required safety guards or fall protection had been installed, the 39-year-old employee\u8217\'20\'19s ninth day on the job might not have been their last\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Osha said workers at \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s foundry were \u8220\'20\'1Croutinely exposed\u8221\'20\'1D to unprotected {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.osha.gov/fall-protection"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fall hazards}}}\uc2  and has proposed a fine of $145,027. The decision does not go far enough for Jessica Sutter, Dierkes\u8217\'20\'19 fiancee.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy children are left without their father, I am left without my fiancee, my partner, my best friend, all because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to take better safety precautions for that type of work,\u8221\'20\'1D Sutter, who had two daughters with Dierkes, said.\par \par \uc2 She claimed \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  has not provided any assistance or support to her and her daughters. She is now trying to find additional work to save enough money to find a new place to live with her children because her landlord won\u8217\'20\'19t conduct needed repairs on her home. She said they were already suffering financially because Dierkes had been out of work for two months before starting at \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sutter criticized \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  for putting her fiance in a dangerous position without adequate safety protections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs far as \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 , I feel that they are murderers. It\u8217\'20\'19s a slaughterhouse. No one should have to lose their life like this,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey do not have any compassion for human decency at all, they are a company of no humanity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Former and current workers at the foundry also raised concerns about safety. One former employee at the Mapleton foundry, who requested anonymity for fear of retaliation from prospective employers, quit in late 2021 due to unsafe working conditions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou breathe in smoke and dust six, seven days a week,\u8221\'20\'1D the former employee said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a lack of concern whenever we brought up a safety issue there. Most of the time it was overlooked or their fix created a whole new safety issue or multiple issues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A current employee at the foundry, who also requested anonymity for fear of retaliation, explained in detail the working conditions and lack of safety protections that contributed to Dierkes\u8217\'20\'19 death. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20221109-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Over 800 workers}}}\uc2  are employed at the foundry.\par \par \uc2 The foundry is divided by into two sides by \u8220\'20\'1Cthe wall\u8221\'20\'1D, a term used by workers to characterize the separation of environments and job types. Machining takes place on one side of the wall, and iron melting on the other side.\par \par \uc2 Dierkes was working as a melt deck operator and fell into a melter while trying to obtain a sample.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen he died they only had us off work for two days and then told everyone to come back. The air literally still smelled like his burning body,\u8221\'20\'1D a worker said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere were no guard rails, no harness procedures and nothing to ensure you wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t fall into the massive holes filled with iron. As he was collecting a sample of iron with the spoon, he fell in and churned up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m very surprised this is the first time it\u8217\'20\'19s ever happened. When I worked up there, there were numerous times I thought, \u8216\'20\'18Man, are they really gonna have me do this?\u8217\'20\'19 For instance, if the iron level was low, they wanted you to try to get a sample or temp anyways, which would require you to lean over the hole a bit to be able to reach the iron. The melters are always around 2,400-2,600F, so if you fall in one there is zero chance of survival.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The worker also claimed the process of tapping out the iron was dangerous, the cranes used to haul the iron also posed safety risks, and there were significant risks of getting burned by backsplash while working in excessive heat.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the summer the melt deck reaches upward of 120F. You\u8217\'20\'19re expected to be in full long sleeves to protect you from the iron, but the fire-resistant clothing you wear doesn\u8217\'20\'19t protect from anything \u8211\'20\'13 the iron burns right through it,\u8221\'20\'1D the worker added.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to breathe because of the heat, and you\u8217\'20\'19re always drenched in sweat. They have heat advisory days in the summer where security passes out bottles of water. But it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t really matter how much water you drink up there, you\u8217\'20\'19re losing so much sweat you almost always feel cruddy when leaving work and your ears and nose are filled with black soot every single day, and that obviously gets in your lungs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The worker also claimed that \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  had not done anything for Dierkes\u8217\'20\'19 family and that co-workers had tried to raise money for the family themselves after the incident. They noted that the money Osha has proposed to fine \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  for the safety violations won\u8217\'20\'19t go to Dierkes\u8217\'20\'19 family.\par \par \uc2 Another worker, 50-year-old contractor Scott Adams, fell to his death at the foundry in 2021. Osha {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pjstar.com/story/news/accident/2022/06/27/peoria-contractors-fined-after-2021-death-caterpillar-foundry/7749162001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blamed the contractors}}}\uc2  he was working for on \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s premises for failing to protect him from the fall.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, the latest year of available data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/opub/ted/2022/a-look-at-workplace-deaths-injuries-and-illnesses-on-workers-memorial-day.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 4,764 workers }}}\uc2  in the US suffered fatal injuries in the workplace. But the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the largest federation of unions in the US, says that number {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/reports/death-job-toll-neglect-2021"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 severely undercounts}}}\uc2  the real problem because of the lack of funding for regulatory oversight.\par \par \uc2 Workers at the foundry are represented by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , and the employee claimed that workers are now being asked to train their replacements in anticipation of a possible strike in March 2023, when the current union contract expires.\par \par \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  declined to comment on Dierkes\u8217\'20\'19 or Adams\u8217\'20\'19 deaths or on workers\u8217\'20\'19 claims they are being asked to train possible replacements.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  said in an email: \u8220\'20\'1CWe continue to be deeply saddened by the death of an employee who was involved in a serious incident at our Mapleton, Illinois, facility on June 2. Our thoughts remain with this employee\u8217\'20\'19s family, friends and colleagues. The safety of our employees, contractors and visitors is our top priority at all \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  locations around the world. Regarding the serious safety incident that occurred, we will continue to engage with Osha to seek an appropriate resolution to its review.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221126eibq000rw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
