\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The success is inspirational\u8217\'20\'19: the Fight for $15 movement 10 years on\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,404 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 November 2022\par 14:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal lawmakers failed to increase the minimum wage, but US workers made other gains, and they are setting their sights on new goals\par \par \uc2 Ten years ago next week, 200 fast-food workers walked out at 20 New York City restaurants, demanding $15 an hour in pay. At the time, many observers scoffed at $15 as an absurd, pie-in-the-sky demand. As the movement\u8217\'20\'19s anniversary approaches, the Fight for $15 movement has proven the naysayers wrong.\par \par \uc2 Congress has failed to increase the federal minimum wage, which has been stuck at $7.25 an hour since 2009. But across the country, states and companies have raised wages in the wake of Fight for $15\u8217\'20\'19s efforts. While, for many, $15 an hour is still too low, the increases have been especially important in the current era of rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 Twelve {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.govdocs.com/how-many-states-have-a-15-minimum-wage/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states and Washington DC have adopted a $15 minimum}}}\uc2  hourly wage, although in many states it\u8217\'20\'19s being phased in. Even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Business/nebraska-voters-approve-15-minimum-wage/story?id=92985074"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deep-red Nebraska has embraced a $15 minimum}}}\uc2 , while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hawaiinewsnow.com/2022/04/29/lawmakers-decide-whether-raise-hawaiis-minimum-wage-18-by-2026/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hawaii has approved an impressive $18 minimum}}}\uc2  to be phased in between now and 2028.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe movement\u8217\'20\'19s success is inspirational,\u8221\'20\'1D said Yannet Lathrop, a policy analyst at the National Employment Law Project. \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nelp.org/publication/quantifying-the-impact-of-the-fight-for-15-150-billion-in-raises-for-26-million-workers-with-76-billion-going-to-workers-of-color/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 It has helped 26 million workers}}}\uc2  across the US win $150bn per year in additional pay. Its impact for workers of color is significant. About 12 million workers of color have benefited and their additional earnings are $76bn a year.\u8221\'20\'1D For workers whose wages rose, this means an average raise of roughly $6,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  (\uc2 SEIU\uc2 ) helped create and underwrite the Fight for $15 movement because it believed that the US was paying far too little attention to the plight of low-wage workers. Fight for $15 began in New York and relied on a series of one-day strikes that inspired fast-food workers in other cities. It {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2016/nov/29/fight-for-15-protest-minimum-wage-fast-food-airport-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ultimately expanded to staging strikes in 340 US cities}}}\uc2  on the movement\u8217\'20\'19s fourth anniversary in 2016.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe key accomplishment was that the Fight for $15, which was led by black, brown and immigrant workers all across the country, taught all workers that when you join together you can make changes in your jobs and in your lives,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Mary Kay Henry\uc2 , the \uc2 SEIU\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s president, told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTwenty-six million workers have seen their wages go up to $15. That is a standard that was scoffed at when it started, but that standard is now getting challenged for demands for an ever-higher minimum, which is a real indicator of success.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Fight for $15\u8221\'20\'1D, Henry added, \u8220\'20\'1Chas changed the way the country thinks about wages, the way elected officials think about wages, and the way economists think about wages.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Frances Holmes, a fast-food worker in St Louis, Missouri, is glad that her pay has jumped from $7.75 an hour a decade ago to $15 today. \u8220\'20\'1CI was able to move from an apartment to a house,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI was able to afford to buy bacon again, but it\u8217\'20\'19s still a struggle living on $15. They don\u8217\'20\'19t give you enough hours to work each week. Our biggest hope is to have a union. That would help us get more money and more hours.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Not all of the campaign\u8217\'20\'19s initial aims have panned out. Its original goal was \u8220\'20\'1C$15 and a union\u8221\'20\'1D, and while it has racked up major successes in raising wages, it has failed to unionize any fast-food workers. One reason is that its pressure campaign against \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s has not gotten the fast-food giant to agree not to oppose unionization efforts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Fight for $15 has lifted up the whole issue of the pathetic state of the minimum wage,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ruth Milkman, a sociologist at the City University of New York\u8217\'20\'19s School of Labor and Urban Studies. \u8220\'20\'1CIn terms of pushing the envelope to make the utopian real, it was extremely successful, at least in blue cities and states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs for its stated goal of \u8216\'20\'18$15 and a union\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D Milkman added, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe second half of that has not taken place.\u8221\'20\'1D She said the movement\u8217\'20\'19s assumption that it could get the federal government to declare that heavily franchised companies like \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s were joint employers \u8220\'20\'1Cturned out to be wrong\u8221\'20\'1D. If the government considered \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s to be a joint employer with its franchisees, it would make it far easier for employees of the fast-food giant to unionize and win contracts that would cover many of its locations, rather than having to negotiate many individual contracts with individual franchisees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s pretty tough to unionize in the fast-food sector without the joint employer thing,\u8221\'20\'1D Milkman said.\par \par \uc2 With their successes on the wage front and lack of success on the unionization front, workers and strategists behind Fight for $15 are trying to figure out their next steps. One ambitious new initiative has been to set up a new group, the Union of Southern Service Workers, to help workers across the southern region of the US, where the Fight for $15 movement has had the least success in getting employers and states to raise pay.\par \par \uc2 Last weekend, more than 100 fast-food, retail, healthcare, warehouse and other workers from the south\u8217\'20\'19s service economy met in Columbia, South Carolina, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oShUps4sU_U"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to launch this new, non-traditional union}}}\uc2 . The organizers said the effort would seek to unite \u8220\'20\'1Cworkers across workplaces plagued by low pay, high turnover and a legacy of racism\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Terrence Wise, a \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2  worker and Fight for $15 leader who attended the South Carolina meeting that founded the new union, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to do whatever it takes: go on strike, do civil disobedience, do union elections, target the employer in any way possible. That\u8217\'20\'19s what\u8217\'20\'19s unique about this. It\u8217\'20\'19s across different industries.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that wages are lowest in the south, and we know the history of the south: slavery, plantations, union-busting,\u8221\'20\'1D Wise continued. \u8220\'20\'1CRacial inequality is baked into the country and especially the southern states. We know we can win in California and New York. Don\u8217\'20\'19t you want to take the fight where it\u8217\'20\'19s hardest to win, where it\u8217\'20\'19s most challenging? That means the south.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This new union may push for union elections at nursing homes in Mississippi or stage protests at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouses in Georgia over safety problems there.\par \par \uc2 Wise said another goal of the South Carolina meeting was to help develop new leaders, people like Lizett Aguilar, a McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s worker in Los Angeles. Aguilar said the biggest effect of Fight for $15 for her hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been that her pay rose to $16 an hour from $8.50 a decade ago, but that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Fight for $15 has helped me lose my fear about speaking out against injustice\u8221\'20\'1D. Aguilar has helped lead marches and knocked on legislators\u8217\'20\'19 doors to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seiu1021.org/article/gov-newsom-signs-ab-257-law-labor-day-major-victory-fast-food-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 win passage of California\u8217\'20\'19s Assembly Bill 257}}}\uc2  (AB 257), which has set up a type of sectoral bargaining for more than 550,000 California fast-food workers to help them get raises and reduce wage theft and sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 Fight for $15 leaders say their movement helped inspire the union drives at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . Indeed, some activists say the Fight for $15 movement should seek to help the unionization wave grow faster and further by copying the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  campaign and pushing to unionize hundreds of \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s restaurants, despite the lack of joint employer status.\par \par \uc2 But some Fight for $15 leaders don\u8217\'20\'19t see that as a formula for success. \uc2 SEIU\uc2  president Henry said: \u8220\'20\'1CBargaining store by store in a franchised industry is not going to give workers the power they need. That\u8217\'20\'19s why we will fight to pass legislation like AB 257 in other states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Terrence Wise feels considerable pride when he hears that \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  announced a $15 minimum wage in 2017 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newsroom.bankofamerica.com/content/newsroom/press-releases/2022/05/bank-of-america-accelerates-us-minimum-hourly-wage-to--22-as-nex.html#:~:text=Share%3A-,Bank%20of%20America%20Accelerates%20US%20Minimum%20Hourly%20Wage%20to%20%2422,Step%20to%20%2425%20by%202025&text=Today%2C%20Bank%20of%20America%20announced,increase%20to%20%2425%20by%202025."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a $22 minimum wage recently}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWhenever I hear about wages being increased \u8211\'20\'13 just the other week in Nebraska, or when we hear about \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers forming a union or \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers challenging \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 that kind of change is real, and we know it was inspired by the Fight for $15 and those workers in New York City walking out 10 years ago,\u8221\'20\'1D Wise said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221123eibn001e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
