\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Victoria election launch shows Labor\u8217\'20\'19s strategy is all about a return to (70s-style) power\b0\par\par\uc2 Benita Kolovos \par \uc2 974 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2022\par 20:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Analysis: At first glance, the choice of Sing It Back for \uc2 Daniel Andrews\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 campaign launch was odd \u8211\'20\'13 but it soon made senseFollow our Australia news live blog for the latest updatesGet our morning and afternoon news emails, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 At first glance, \uc2 Daniel Andrews\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 choice to use a 1998 club hit, Sing It Back, for his big entrance to Labor\u8217\'20\'19s campaign launch on Sunday was a little odd, given it is laced with sexual innuendo and the premier is a renowned 1980s rock fan.\par \par \uc2 But as he walked on to the stage to the lyrics \u8220\'20\'1Cbring it back\u8221\'20\'1D, it became clear it was a nod to Labor\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/oct/22/daniel-andrews-plans-to-revive-the-state-electricity-commission-what-will-it-mean-for-victorias-power"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  bring back the State Electricity Commission (SEC)}}}\uc2  if elected on 26 November.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/oct/29/email-newsletters-guardian-australia-best-daily-news-emails-newsletter-free-sign-up-inbox-subscribe-headlines?CMP=copyembed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sign up for Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s free morning and afternoon email newsletters for your daily news roundup}}}\par \par \uc2 The commitment was central to the launch, with members of the \uc2 Electrical Trades Union\uc2  arriving at Cranbourne Community Theatre in T-shirts featuring the SEC\u8217\'20\'19s old sun-ray logo. The deputy premier, \uc2 Jacinta Allan\uc2 , dedicated her entire speech to it.\par \par \uc2 Allan told the crowd her father began his 50-year career in the electricity sector with an apprenticeship with the SEC, which at the time was Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s sole provider of electricity generation, transmission and networks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor a young person back then, working for the SEC meant a job for life. Secure, stable work, the kind of work you could raise a family on, build a life with,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBack then, each community and country town had its own SEC depot, each local depot employed its own local workers and each year a couple of extra apprentices would be taken on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Allan went on to criticise its privatisation by the former Kennett Liberal government in the 1990s, a decision she said had failed workers and country communities and driven up power bills for Victorians. The latest federal budget predicted electricity prices were expected to rise by 56% over the next two years and gas prices by 40%.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/nov/13/power-bill-relief-all-round-as-major-parties-launch-victorian-election-campaigns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labor also announced another power-saving bonus of $250 for households}}}\uc2  who seek a better power deal via a government website.\par \par \uc2 The other new announcement was a $207m package for specialist schools.\par \par \uc2 But the SEC dominated Andrews speech, with the premier describing its revival as \u8220\'20\'1Can opportunity for generations of Victorians\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 6,000 jobs for the commission will be dedicated to apprentices and local content requirements would guarantee them a pipeline of work \u8211\'20\'13 while framing the election as a choice between privatisation and public ownership.\par \par \uc2 The opposition leader, \uc2 Matthew Guy\uc2 , on Sunday mocked the government\u8217\'20\'19s SEC commitment as taking Victoria \u8220\'20\'1Cback to 1975\u8221\'20\'1D with \u8220\'20\'1Cno evidence it will actually lower energy bills\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Strategists, however, have said the policy is getting the most cut-through of all of Labor\u8217\'20\'19s election commitments so far. It appeals to older voters, who remember the simpler times that Allan revisited in her speech, and younger voters, who in an era of job instability and short-term employment contracts would find the possibility of working for one company for decades an attractive proposition.\par \par \uc2 This was embodied in a slick campaign video produced for the launch, featuring \u8220\'20\'1Cmates\u8221\'20\'1D Graham and Mick, who spoke of the \u8220\'20\'1Cgood old days\u8221\'20\'1D of the SEC \u8211\'20\'13 both worked more than 20 years for the commission before it was privatised \u8211\'20\'13 and then Damien, James and Shannon, who work in the industry now.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe go from one contract to another and it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t really enable you to plan and be secure as far as your financial situation goes,\u8221\'20\'1D Damien says in the video.\par \par \uc2 It also appeals to those {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/oct/20/victoria-sets-target-of-95-of-its-electricity-sourced-from-renewable-energy-by-2035"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who want a faster transition to renewables}}}\uc2  (read: voters who may be considering backing the Greens in Labor-held seats in the inner city) as well as workers, who are being told they won\u8217\'20\'19t be left behind.\par \par \uc2 Coalition focuses on Andrews government\u8217\'20\'19s weaknesses\par \par \uc2 The central theme at the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s launch was less clear. Guy spoke about rebuilding Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s burdened healthcare system, easing cost-of-living pressures on families, the state\u8217\'20\'19s growing debt and the integrity cloud over the government.\par \par \uc2 Guests were handed \u8220\'20\'1Cfrequent-liar\u8221\'20\'1D cards, a nod to the several Ibac investigations that have played out during Andrews\u8217\'20\'19 time as premier. But outside, a small group of protesters in lobster suits sought to remind voters the Coalition also hasn\u8217\'20\'19t covered itself in glory on the issue of integrity \u8211\'20\'13 referring to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2017-08-08/vic-opposition-leader-dined-with-alleged-mafia-head-madafferi/8782824"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2017 revelation Guy dined at Beaumaris Lobstercave with an alleged mafia boss}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Guy announced the Coalition would cover electricity supply charges on power bills for the first six months of 2023, saving households an estimated $235, and promised that all new gas produced in Victoria would be quarantined for Victorian use. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/aug/20/railing-against-rail-loop-put-matthew-guy-back-on-track-but-theres-a-long-way-to-go"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his focus was on the government weaknesses and the challenge ahead}}}\uc2 . To win outright, the Coalition needs a net gain of 18 seats. Adding to its woes, it will enter the race in a worse position than it was then, thanks to an electoral redistribution that sees Labor gain two net seats, based on the 2018 vote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s like climbing Mount Everest without oxygen and here in Victoria, Labor have been in government for 19 of the past 23 years, it\u8217\'20\'19s like doing it all in a blizzard \u8211\'20\'13 all backwards,\u8221\'20\'1D Guy said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut for the sake of all those people impacted by \uc2 Daniel Andrews\uc2  and his world-record lockdowns, we must win this election.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Guy said there was a \u8220\'20\'1Cgrowing wave of anger and resentment\u8221\'20\'1D against the premier in the state that makes him \u8220\'20\'1Cmore and more confident\u8221\'20\'1D that the Coalition will win the election.\par \par \uc2 He has two more weeks to make his case.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221113eibd000us\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
