\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Graduate workers across University of California system to strike for better pay\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 805 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2022\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Students, researchers and scholars are negotiating contracts in what could be the largest strike in US\u8217\'20\'19s higher education history\par \par \uc2 Graduate workers including teaching assistants and student researchers, academic researchers and postdoctoral scholars in the University of California system have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/uaw2865/status/1588009831020961792"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted}}}\uc2  to authorize a strike which could be the largest in higher education history in the US.\par \par \uc2 Their unions \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 UAW\uc2  5810, \uc2 UAW\uc2  2865 and SRU-\uc2 UAW\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 representing 48,000 workers are currently negotiating union contracts for four separate bargaining units. They are coordinating to push {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fairucnow.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/BargainingSummary.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased}}}\uc2  compensation, childcare reimbursements, job security protections, sustainable transit incentives, eliminating fees for international scholars and stronger disability accommodations.\par \par \uc2 A strike date has been set for 14 November.\par \par \uc2 Bernard Remollino, a researcher and teaching assistant at UCLA, spent the majority of the 2018-2019 academic year living out of his car because he couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford spending 85% or more of his income on rent.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe academic year 2018 to 2019, I spent most of it living out of my car because the rent situation in Los Angeles was untenable both in the graduate student housing and in the private market apartments in LA,\u8221\'20\'1D said Remollino. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is physically draining trying to scrimp and save and feeling that those efforts were in vain. There had to be more dignity to the work than that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He voted in favor of the strike authorization so no other graduate workers have to experience the same economic precarity he did \u8211\'20\'13 experiences that drive and deter people out of academia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis action is happening because of UC\u8217\'20\'19s failure to support a diverse workforce. That failure undermines the quality of research and education,\u8221\'20\'1D added Remollino. \u8220\'20\'1CUC works because we do. It functions off the intellectual, emotional and physical labor of its academic workers, but despite our contributions, we work in difficult conditions while being severely undercompensated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The strike authorization vote was initiated after 6,000 academic workers in the University of California system called for action after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fairucnow.org/ulp/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filing }}}\uc2  more than 20 unfair labor practice charges against the university.\par \par \uc2 The charges include claims that the University of California is refusing to bargain in good faith, has refused to furnish information to the unions, refused to discuss certain subjects, unilaterally changed working conditions and engaged in direct dealing with departments and workers rather than with the unions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C [What] we want is the opportunity to just sit down and settle a contract without UC engaging in unfair labor practices,\u8221\'20\'1D said Janna Haider, a graduate researcher at \uc2 UC Santa Barbara\uc2  and bargaining team member for \uc2 UAW\uc2  2865.\par \par \uc2 She currently spends 49% of her income on rent and has frequently had to take on side jobs to make ends meet. Haider criticized the classification of graduate workers as part-time workers capped at 20 hours a week, as graduate and research work demands workers devote much more time than that to progress.\par \par \uc2 The unions are calling for a minimum annual salary of $54,000 for all grad workers and $70,000 salary for postdoctoral scholars, citing the increasing costs of living, especially in regards to high inflation and soaring rent. A membership {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fairucnow.org/cola/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey }}}\uc2  by the unions found 92% of graduate workers and 61% of postdoctoral scholars are rent burdened, spending more than one-third of their income on monthly rent.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re fighting so those of us who do the majority of teaching and research do not have to live with severe rent burdens and debt, while highly paid administrators live in publicly funded mansions,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rafael Jaime, a graduate worker at UCLA in the english department and president of \uc2 UAW\uc2  2865.\par \par \uc2 Some 11,000 postdoctoral scholars and academic researchers participated in the strike authorization vote, many of them international researchers.\par \par \uc2 Dr Hannah Waterhouse, a postdoctoral scholar in soil science at \uc2 UC Berkeley\uc2 , currently commutes over four hours round trip daily by train from Sacramento to Berkeley for work because of how unaffordable rent is in the area near campus. Even living so far away, she and her partner still need to live with a roommate in order to afford an apartment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just feeling barely sustainable at this point and I actually personally know a few people who\u8217\'20\'19ve passed up on applying to UC jobs, because the pay doesn\u8217\'20\'19t match the cost of living,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Waterhouse.\par \par \uc2 Dr Neal Sweeney, a postdoctoral scholar in molecular biology at UC Santa Cruz and president of UAW Local 5810, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe have an opportunity here to really transform the university in really positive, progressive ways. With the University of California being one of the largest university systems in the US, what we\u8217\'20\'19re able to achieve will really impact people elsewhere.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The University of California did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221112eibc000pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
