\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Inadequate US state unemployment systems leave millions vulnerable\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 812 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2022\par 11:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unprepared system leaves unemployed Americans vulnerable to poverty, losing homes and struggling to pay bills or feed families\par \par \uc2 The onset of the Covid-19 pandemic left millions of Americans jobless in the US, overwhelmingly state unemployment benefit systems that did not have the staffing, technology or resources to handle the influx of those who lost their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Federal pandemic relief added an additional $600 a week to unemployment benefits which was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/08/10/pandemic-unemployment-insurance-ends-in-septemberheres-who-loses-aid.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cut }}}\uc2  to $300 a week, as most state benefits provide only a fraction of the value of weekly wages for the average worker.\par \par \uc2 The benefits {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/business/business-news/pandemic-unemployment-benefits-just-expired-what-will-families-do-now-n1278495"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  expired }}}\uc2  in September 2021, with several states cutting them off early, affecting an estimated 7.5 million people. Proponents for cutting off benefits claimed the benefits were resulting in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2022/09/16/worker-shortage-strikes-economy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 labor shortages,}}}\uc2  though the US still has more job openings than unemployed workers and research {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/cutting-off-unemployment-hurts-states-did-not-help-employment-research-2021-9#:~:text=Research%20has%20continually%20found%20that,not%20opt%20out%20of%20benefits."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found }}}\uc2  cutting off the benefits had little to no impact on job growth and employment.\par \par \uc2 Since the expiration of federal benefits, state unemployment benefit systems have been left again as primary benefit providers. But many are still lagging and seem unprepared to deal with any future wave of job losses as high inflation rates have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/fed-aggressively-hikes-interest-rates-fight-inflation-amplifying-recession-concerns-2022-11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inciting }}}\uc2  concerns of a looming economic recession, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tcf.org/content/report/a-pandemic-lifeline-nations-unemployment-system-is-inadequate-and-unprepared-for-next-downturn/?session=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new report}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 Century Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That has left many vulnerable unemployed Americans vulnerable to poverty, losing homes and struggling to pay bills or feed their families.\par \par \uc2 About two months ago, Melinda Sayre\u8217\'20\'19s partner James filed for unemployment in Pennsylvania after losing his job when his employer ended his contract after working there for a few months. He was approved for benefits and has claimed weekly for over a month without receiving anything yet.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe expected to find another job right away, but a lot of employers that act like they really need someone and are \u8216\'20\'18urgently hiring\u8217\'20\'19 are taking a long time contacting potential employees and it\u8217\'20\'19s making it hard when he just wants to get back to work right away,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sayre. \u8220\'20\'1CIf our local ministry hadn\u8217\'20\'19t paid rent for us, we would probably be homeless.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sayre said her partner had contacted their local state representative for assistance, but had not been helped yet and they had already fallen behind enough on bills to lose his phone because they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford the bill, and were behind on utility and internet bills.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are struggling to have enough money for food and gas,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 In the 12 months ending August 2022, only 26.8% of jobless Americans were receiving state unemployment benefits, compared to 76% through early 2021. The benefits are also paltry compared to average wages, with average weekly benefits in the first quarter of 2022 at $347.22, replacing just 39% of the average weekly wages by those receiving benefits, compared with 79% of pre-layoff wages when pandemic benefits were still supplementing state benefits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of eligible people don\u8217\'20\'19t apply for benefits,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Andrew Stettner\uc2 , the director of workforce policy and senior fellow at the \uc2 Century Foundation\uc2 , who co-authored the report.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://time.com/nextadvisor/in-the-news/unemployment-survey-benefits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Surveys }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/opub/ted/2019/most-unemployed-people-in-2018-did-not-apply-for-unemployment-insurance-benefits.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data }}}\uc2  from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  that the majority of jobless workers do not apply for unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 The report also notes that states that struggled to pay out benefits and resolve issues throughout the pandemic have largely not resolved the administration problems plaguing state unemployment systems.\par \par \uc2 As of August 2022, only 15 states met a federal requirement to pay out at least 87% of eligible claims within 21 days and workers are waiting nearly four months to have appeals of benefit denials settled. The report also noted these shortcomings, delays and insufficient benefits {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tcf.org/content/report/a-pandemic-lifeline-nations-unemployment-system-is-inadequate-and-unprepared-for-next-downturn/?session=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disproportionately affect }}}\uc2  Black workers.\par \par \uc2 Kim Lytton of Tennessee was relying on unemployment benefits after losing her job as a personal assistant when her benefits were suddenly cut off in May 2021 as she was wrongfully deemed ineligible.\par \par \uc2 She spent months filing appeals, struggling to get a hold of someone in the unemployment system to resolve her issues and finally received back pay in August 2022, though she said it was short of the benefits she was actually owed but gave up in trying to fight for the correct amount.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt put me in a really bad position. I begged and pleaded with them, I confirmed my identity over 20 times,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lytton. \u8220\'20\'1CWe lived in my car for a few weeks. My car was up for repossession, I got several jobs, but with my daughters, working a regular nine-to-five as a single mother is really hard for me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lytton said she was eventually told an error was made by an unemployment agency worker who denied her initial appeal when her benefits were first flagged.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s supposed to be there to help you, but they\u8217\'20\'19re actually making things harder,\u8221\'20\'1D added Lytton.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221111eibb001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
