\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Pittsburgh newspaper workers go on strike over unfair labor practice\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 684 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2022\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Strike began after \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s management, \uc2 Block Communications\uc2 , cut off health insurance\par \par \uc2 More than 100 workers represented by five labor unions at the \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2  are currently on an unfair labor practice strike, including production, distribution, advertising and accounts receivable staff.\par \par \uc2 The strike initially began after the newspaper\u8217\'20\'19s management, \uc2 Block Communications\uc2  owned by the Block family, cut off health insurance for employees on 1 October after refusing to pay an additional $19 a week per employee to maintain the existing coverage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHaving health insurance is one of the most important parts of the middle class. It was the final straw for us \u8211\'20\'13 we were able to say this was an unfair labor practice and we walked off the job,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kitsy Higgins, an advertising representative who\u8217\'20\'19s been at the Post-Gazette for one year.\par \par \uc2 Higgins said the newspaper\u8217\'20\'19s management had tried to get workers to accept a healthcare plan with a $14,000 deductible that they rejected because it was unaffordable to use.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a shame that Block Communications, a multimillion-dollar company, isn\u8217\'20\'19t putting their money into the people creating this prize-winning newspaper. They\u8217\'20\'19re putting money into attorneys who are greatly affecting the outcome of that. If they would have invested their money into workers, we wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be having this strike and I\u8217\'20\'19d be at work,\u8221\'20\'1D added Higgins. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is unfair and we will continue being on strike until we get a fair agreement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The strike is unfolding in a US media industry that has seen widespread layoffs over the past decade with newspapers hit especially hard.\par \par \uc2 Workers at the Post-Gazette have been working without a union contract since March 2017, claiming they haven\u8217\'20\'19t received any pay raises in 16 years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe had no alternative but to strike,\u8221\'20\'1D said James VanLandingham, a mailer at the Post-Gazette for 28 years. \u8220\'20\'1COver the years, they\u8217\'20\'19ve always paid for our healthcare. Once Block senior passed away, the two nephews took over and everything went downhill. They\u8217\'20\'19ve totally disregarded all of their employees.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 VanLandingham noted the influx of community support for the strike, with local unions pulling advertisements from the newspaper and support for a call for subscribers and advertisers to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cwa-union.org/support-striking-pittsburgh-post-gazette-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 boycott}}}\uc2  the newspaper until workers\u8217\'20\'19 demands are met and management bargains in good faith for a new union contract.\par \par \uc2 Workers have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.unionprogress.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 launched}}}\uc2  a strike publication, Pittsburgh Union Progress, as an alternative to the Post-Gazette during the strike.\par \par \uc2 In Washington DC, the newspaper\u8217\'20\'19s DC bureau chief, Ashley Murray, led a picket protest with dozens of supporters at the US Capitol on 25 October.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI would much rather be in the halls of Congress today covering our lawmakers or working on stories about the midterm elections that are in two weeks,\u8221\'20\'1D Murray told the crowd of supporters. \u8220\'20\'1CInstead of reporting or taking photographs or designing the pages of our daily newspaper, my colleagues are out on the picket line in Pittsburgh because the owner of our news outlet refuses to come to the bargaining table and work out a deal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The newspaper recently won an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://triblive.com/local/regional/butler-county-judge-backs-injunction-against-striking-post-gazette-workers-their-supporters/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 injunction}}}\uc2  in Butler county court against the striking workers, limiting the number of picketers outside the Butler Eagle, a local newspaper that has crossed the strike line to continue a reduced service of \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s operations during the strike.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Blocks haven\u8217\'20\'19t responded to anything. Our unions have reached out to them numerous times since the strike began,\u8221\'20\'1D said VanLandingham. \u8220\'20\'1CWe just want the public to know how disrespectful the Blocks are. In my family, I\u8217\'20\'19m a third generation employee, my family has over 100 years of combined loyal employment to this newspaper. We enjoy putting out the newspaper, but the Blocks don\u8217\'20\'19t care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2  said in an emailed statement on 1 November praising the court injunction against picketers: \u8220\'20\'1CThe Post-Gazette contacted a federal mediator yesterday in an effort to move forward with negotiations with the union.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221111eibb001bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
