\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Colorado gig drivers make an average of just $5.49 an hour, study finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Kari Paul in San Francisco \par \uc2 988 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2022\par 19:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Although the report focuses on drivers in Denver, gig worker drivers across the US face similar issues, experts say\par \par \uc2 Drivers for platforms like \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  in Denver, Colorado, on average make significantly less than minimum wage when accounting for expenses and time spent on the app, a new study found, the latest research to highlight the struggles of such workers across the US.\par \par \uc2 Research from workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights group Colorado Jobs With Justice in partnership with drivers\u8217\'20\'19 union Colorado Independent Drivers United surveyed hundreds of gig workers in the Denver area regarding their work between 1 November 2021 and 31 January 2022, and found drivers on average took home $5.49 (\u163\'00\'A34.75) an hour after expenses. That average is roughly 35% of Denver\u8217\'20\'19s 2022 minimum wage of $15.87, and falls significantly below the federal minimum wage of $7.25 an hour.\par \par \uc2 The study marks the latest attempt to quantify wages for gig workers, which can vary widely due to the range of ways to calculate costs, time and miles spent on the job. A 2018 study from the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  showed \uc2 Uber\uc2  drivers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/press/uber-drivers-earn-the-equivalent-of-9-21-in-hourly-wages-uber-and-other-gig-platforms-account-for-far-less-of-the-economy-than-many-estimates-suggest/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 make}}}\uc2  an average of $9.21 an hour, while another study that year found \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2018/mar/01/uber-lyft-driver-wages-median-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 made}}}\uc2  a median of $8.55 each hour before taxes.\par \par \uc2 Those numbers have been sinking {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/10/gig-workers-gas-prices-california-uber-lyft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as gas prices soar}}}\uc2  across the US and inflation rises. As of June, gas prices across the US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/06/10/inflation-continues-to-rise-gas-prices-up-nearly-50percent-since-last-year.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had increased}}}\uc2  50% over the course of the year. In Denver, where the study was conducted, the median cost of rent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/local/denver/2022/09/26/denver-no-9-most-expensive-metro-renters#:~:text=The%20median%20rent%20in%20all,a%20median%20rent%20of%20%242550."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased more}}}\uc2  than 45% in the past year to $2,550. Some workers in the study reported driving as many as 18 hours a day to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 Although the Colorado Jobs With Justice study focused on drivers in Denver, gig worker drivers across the US face similar issues, said Sofia Solano, organizing director with Colorado Jobs With Justice.\par \par \uc2 The findings come as legislation is being considered in various states of the US to better protect gig economy workers. The Biden administration has also proposed a rule that would more easily classify gig workers as employees, granting them access to benefits and federal labor protections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe hope this study can shed light on what it\u8217\'20\'19s really like to be a gig worker,\u8221\'20\'1D Solano said. \u8220\'20\'1CAs legislation is considered, no solution is going to be a good one for these workers unless they are collectively given a seat at the table.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to the study, drivers reported a median of 38 hours worked each week. The majority of drivers \u8211\'20\'13 61% \u8211\'20\'13 said they relied on gig work as a primary means of income. Companies such as \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and \uc2 Uber\uc2  have long argued drivers are contract workers who prefer flexibility and don\u8217\'20\'19t require full-time protections or benefits.\par \par \uc2 Spokespeople from \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  strongly disputed the findings of the study. Alix Anfang, a spokesperson for \uc2 Uber\uc2 , disputed the calculation of costs for drivers and said the company\u8217\'20\'19s internal data found median earnings in Colorado were more than $37 an hour for time spent engaged on the platform. Anfang added that \uc2 Uber\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s own data showed 80% of drivers nationwide drive less than 20 hours a week.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson from \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  also said the company\u8217\'20\'19s own data showed drivers work less than full time and spend fewer than four hours each week on a delivery. \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  added that the company\u8217\'20\'19s data showed Dashers across all markets earn $25 an hour while on delivery.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson from \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said the platform \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinues to provide drivers with the ability to earn on their own terms\u8221\'20\'1D and that its own data showed drivers earned more than $35 an hour including tips.\par \par \uc2 Activist groups and researchers in the past {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/uber-drivers-55000-year-claim-criticism/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have disputed}}}\uc2  earnings calculations for drivers coming from gig economy firms. Increasing costs like car maintenance, rent and gas are making it impossible to make ends meet, said Jennifer, a driver who participated in the study and asked to use a pseudonym for fear of retaliation.\par \par \uc2 She said she began driving with \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and \uc2 Uber\uc2  so she could have flexibility to take care of her family, including her mother who was ill. According to the Colorado Jobs With Justice study, the majority of drivers reported that they supported someone else with their income, including 23% who said they support two or more children.\par \par \uc2 In addition to rising costs, Jennifer said she lost more than $1,000 in wages after her account was temporarily shut down when the company investigated her report of being assaulted by a passenger. During the investigation, she said, she had little knowledge of when her account would return. She ultimately was able to return to work after a week.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson from \uc2 Uber\uc2  confirmed the platform restricts access to both drivers\u8217\'20\'19 and passengers\u8217\'20\'19 accounts when a \u8220\'20\'1Cserious incident\u8221\'20\'1D is reported while agents internally investigate it as part of its safety policy.\par \par \uc2 Driver safety has long been a concern on the platforms. Over one-fifth of drivers in the study (21.7%) reported facing violence or threatened violence while on the job; a similar number (19.7%) reported being discriminated against on the basis of their identity. Companies like \uc2 Uber\uc2  have said driver safety is a top priority, and continue to invest in technology and policies that mitigate risk.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese companies need to be better regulated,\u8221\'20\'1D Jennifer said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey get away with so much, and it\u8217\'20\'19s unfair to their workers who need these wages to survive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This story was amended on 9 November 2022. The study referenced was conducted in partnership with Colorado Independent Drivers United and not Gig Workers Rising as a previous version said. Also, the $5.49 average hourly wage cited is 35% of Denver\u8217\'20\'19s minimum not 35% below Denver\u8217\'20\'19s minimum.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221109eib9001p6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picsc