\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  union workers go on strike over pay \u8216\'20\'18for as long as it takes\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Alaina Demopoulos \par \uc2 812 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2022\par 18:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers at the only \u8216\'20\'18big four\u8217\'20\'19 publisher to have a union authorize indefinite strike over low wages and diversity\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Guerdan started working in the children\u8217\'20\'19s book department of \uc2 HarperCollins Publishers\uc2  six years ago. It was a dream job \u8211\'20\'13 just not a dream paycheck. The $33,500-a-year (\u163\'00\'A328,750) salary was well below a livable wage in New York City, but Guerdan didn\u8217\'20\'19t ask for more. \u8220\'20\'1CI was terrified that I was not going to get that job if I negotiated,\u8221\'20\'1D they said. \u8220\'20\'1CPublishing is very much an industry where they tell you, \u8216\'20\'18If you don\u8217\'20\'19t want this, there are 500 people in line behind you who do.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Publishing has for decades has been known for its low pay and overwhelmingly white staff. But workers at \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2 , the only member of the \u8220\'20\'1Cbig four\u8221\'20\'1D publishing houses to have a union, have had enough and authorized an indefinite strike. Work stopped at the downtown Manhattan offices on the sunny morning of 10 November. Employees like Guerdan, who is a shop steward at the union, spilled on to the streets to picket.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to create a workplace that is more financially sustainable for employees and accessible to people from a variety of backgrounds,\u8221\'20\'1D said Olga Brudastova, president of Local 2110 United Auto Workers, the union that \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  workers are part of.\par \par \uc2 More than 250 \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  employees are unionized, including workers in the editorial, sales, publicity, design, legal and marketing departments. The strike was authorized by a vote of 95.1% last month. It comes after 11 months of negotiations with \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  management over a new contract, and a one-day strike that occurred on 20 July.\par \par \uc2 According to the union, the average salary at the company is $55,000 annually, and the majority of employees are women. \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2 , which is owned by Rupert Murdoch\u8217\'20\'19s NewsCorp, reported record profits in 2021.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re willing to stay out as long as it takes,\u8221\'20\'1D Guerdan said. \u8220\'20\'1CAfter about a week, the company is really going to feel the loss of all our essential labor. I\u8217\'20\'19ve talked to friends who are not in the union, and their assessment of it has been, \u8216\'20\'18I don\u8217\'20\'19t think the company knows what\u8217\'20\'19s going to hit them.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  strike follows a national surge in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/26/striketober-unions-strikes-us-october"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 support for labor unions}}}\uc2 , which at 71% is the highest it\u8217\'20\'19s been since 1965.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, a spokesperson for the company said: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  has agreed to a number of proposals that the \uc2 United Auto Workers Union\uc2  is seeking to include in a new contract. We are disappointed an agreement has not been reached and will continue to negotiate in good faith.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Authors published by the firm showed their support for the strike on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CUnlivable wages and untenable conditions result in burnout and turnover,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/allie_therin"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  romance author Allie Therin. \u8220\'20\'1CI support fair contracts for workers.\u8221\'20\'1D The artist and graphic novelist Bree Paulsen {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/breebird33/status/1590703919491354625?s=20&t=ohER8Yeng2bYoYInQ4nMVQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeted}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1C@hcpunion has my full support.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Laura Harshberger, senior production editor at HarperCollins Children\u8217\'20\'19s Books and the union\u8217\'20\'19s unit chair, said she was only able to afford working in the publishing industry though her 20s by living with her parents and working retail on the side. \u8220\'20\'1CI would average five to 10 hours a week freelancing, which helped compensate for the fact that my day job did not pay me enough to live on,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Harshberger met her partner working in publishing. When her partner left the industry, she immediately made double the salary. \u8220\'20\'1CI can keep working in publishing right now because of her,\u8221\'20\'1D Harshberger said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat doesn\u8217\'20\'19t feel great. That\u8217\'20\'19s the privilege side of it: it shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t only be the people who have wealthy partners or parents who can stay in the industry. When you\u8217\'20\'19re not paying people fairly, that narrows the perspective of who\u8217\'20\'19s making decisions about what books we publish. Readers lose out in that, and so does the company.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Staffers on the picket line said they were both nervous and anxious. They led chants and encouraged passing drivers to honk their horns in support. Some carried protest signs poking fun at their more famous titles, like a Maurice Sendak-inspired \u8220\'20\'1CWhere The Wild Things Are Underpaid\u8221\'20\'1D banner, and one, riffing on the children\u8217\'20\'19s book written by Laura Numeroff, that read: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you give a mouse a fair contract.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers got together to make the signs during a brainstorm session in Brooklyn\u8217\'20\'19s Prospect Park. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re doing is challenging and very scary, but when we all come together, we end up feeling better about it,\u8221\'20\'1D Harshberger said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe feed off of each other\u8217\'20\'19s energies, we\u8217\'20\'19re all smart, talented people, so you see it sometimes reflected in our signs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221110eiba00336\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
