\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republicans want working-class voters \u8212\'20\'14 without actually supporting workers\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par 17:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 GOP courts blue collar voters but most favor anti-union \u8216\'20\'18right to work\u8217\'20\'19 laws and reject laws that would protect right to organize\par \par \uc2 After years of struggle, America\u8217\'20\'19s labor unions enjoy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/30/americans-support-labor-unions-highest-60-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 greater public approval}}}\uc2  than at any time in more than 50 years. Yet even as the Republican party seeks to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/04/13/986549868/top-republicans-work-to-rebrand-gop-as-party-of-working-class"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rebrand}}}\uc2  itself as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://talkingpointsmemo.com/news/gop-memo-bearhugs-trump-in-bid-for-working-class-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 party of the working class}}}\uc2 , its lawmakers, by and large, remain as hostile as ever toward organized labor. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t look like that situation is about to change.\par \par \uc2 With the midterm elections approaching, and many polls indicating that the Republicans will win control of the House, nearly all Republican lawmakers in Congress oppose proposals that would make it easier to unionize. One hundred and eleven Republican House members and 21 senators are co-sponsoring {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/1275/cosponsors?q=%7B%22search%22%3A%5B%22National+Right+to+Work+Act%22%2C%22National%22%2C%22Right%22%2C%22to%22%2C%22Work%22%2C%22Act%22%5D%7D&r=1&s=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a bill that would weaken unions by letting workers in all 50 states opt out}}}\uc2  of paying any fees to the unions that represent them. And at a time when many young workers \u8211\'20\'13 among them, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers, \uc2 Apple\uc2  store workers, museum workers, grad students \u8211\'20\'13 are flocking into unions, Republican lawmakers often deride unions as woke, leftwing and obsolete.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats \u8211\'20\'13 seeing the surge in unionization drives along with the aggressive anti-union campaigns by \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , Amazon and other companies \u8211\'20\'13 say there is increased urgency to enact the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/842/actions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Protecting the Right to Organize Act }}}\uc2  (Pro Act), which would make it easier for workers to unionize. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/03/09/975259434/house-democrats-pass-bill-that-would-protect-worker-organizing-efforts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pro Act passed the House last year}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202170"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 205 Republicans voting against and five in favor}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 but it faces an uphill battle in the Senate, largely because of a GOP filibuster, and will almost certainly fail to pass if Republicans gain Senate seats in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 The Pro Act remains the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 overwhelming legislative priority for helping unions \u8211\'20\'13 it would, among other things, ban employers\u8217\'20\'19 captive audience meetings and create substantial penalties for corporations that break the law when fighting unionization. Republicans denounce the legislation, vigorously opposing a provision that would override the right-to-work laws enacted in 27 states, laws that allow workers to opt out of paying union dues. The Senate Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rpc.senate.gov/policy-papers/the-pro-act-bad-for-workers-bad-for-businesses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 policy committee has slammed the Pro Act}}}\uc2 , saying it would undermine worker freedom, \u8220\'20\'1Cheavily tilt the scales in favor of labor\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Ccurb workers\u8217\'20\'19 choices, threaten jobs and increase costs on employers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t always this way. Two decades ago, there were 30 union-friendly Republicans in the House, but that number has dwindled to a handful, partly because many of the party\u8217\'20\'19s billionaire and corporate donors frown on pro-union Republicans. These donors see unions as bothersome institutions that favor Democrats and reduce corporate profits. Indeed, many Republican lawmakers treat unions and their leaders as enemies.\par \par \uc2 Virginia Foxx, the senior Republican on the House Education and Labor Committee, scoffed at the idea that there is a union resurgence and said Democrats \u8220\'20\'1Care in the pocket of Big Labor\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CUnions are hitting the panic button and praying that Democrats can gin up a PR campaign to cover up the declining numbers and lack of interest in union membership,\u8221\'20\'1D Foxx told the Guardian, noting that union membership has sunk to just 6% of the private-sector workforce. Foxx, who often serves as Congress\u8217\'20\'19s chief spokesperson on labor matters, belittled unions\u8217\'20\'19 recent gains, saying that only a tiny percentage of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2  stores have been unionized.\par \par \uc2 Foxx, a nine-term House member from North Carolina, said: \u8220\'20\'1CIf Democrats genuinely believe that union popularity is soaring and that union campaigns and strikes are resonating with American workers, then they truly have a tortured relationship with both math and reality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even as the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) reported a 53% jump over the past year in the number of workplace petitions for union elections, Foxx and many other Republicans are backing bills that would make it harder to unionize. With corporations prohibiting union organizers from setting foot on company property to speak with workers, unions rely on \uc2 NLRB\uc2  rules requiring employers to give them workers\u8217\'20\'19 home addresses, phone numbers and email addresses so they can communicate with them. But the Employee Privacy Protection Act, a Republican-sponsored bill re-introduced last March, shortly after the recent union surge began, would limit unions to obtaining just one of those three ways to contact workers. Foxx said workers should \u8220\'20\'1Cnever have to hand over their personal contact information\u8221\'20\'1D to \u8220\'20\'1Ca union to which they object\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Bill Samuel, legislative director of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the nation\u8217\'20\'19s main union federation, said he has seen no sign of Republicans warming up to unions despite their increased popularity \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/398303/approval-labor-unions-highest-point-1965.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 71% of Americans approve of unions}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI haven\u8217\'20\'19t seen any change\u8221\'20\'1D among Republicans, Samuel said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s been no outreach. We haven\u8217\'20\'19t been getting calls from Republicans asking, \u8216\'20\'18How can we help workers organize?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bobby Scott\uc2 , a Virginia Democrat who is chairman of the House education and labor committee, agreed, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans are pretty much as hostile as ever toward unions \u8211\'20\'13 pretty much down the line.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Scott said Democrats should rush to enact the Pro Act in light of the many daunting obstacles that workers face in seeking to unionize at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , Amazon and other companies due to intense corporate opposition and a flurry of alleged illegalities by management. In Scott\u8217\'20\'19s view, especially important is a provision that would for the first time allow the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  to impose substantial fines against companies that violate the law when battling union drives. \u8220\'20\'1CThe biggest improvement we need is to have some meaningful sanctions for unfair labor practices,\u8221\'20\'1D Scott said. \u8220\'20\'1CRight now, there is no meaningful deterrent.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Oren Cass, executive director of American Compass, a thinktank for conservative economics, said that many Republicans have grown more interested in worker issues. Cass acknowledged, however, that most Republican lawmakers remain hostile to organized labor because \u8220\'20\'1Cunions are predominantly financing mechanisms for the Democratic party.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He said some Republicans are open to the idea of increasing worker power, but only if it\u8217\'20\'19s done largely outside the framework of traditional unions. Nevertheless, whether with or without unions, hardly any Republicans are pushing to expand worker power \u8211\'20\'13 an idea that would irk corporate Republicans. Many GOP lawmakers instead emphasize worker choice and worker freedom \u8211\'20\'13 part of their decades-long effort to enact state right-to-work laws that allow workers to opt out of paying any dues or fees to the unions that represent them.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.paul.senate.gov/news/dr-rand-paul-reintroduces-national-right-work-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senator Rand Paul of Kentucky and Representative Joe Wilson}}}\uc2  of South Carolina are co-sponsoring the National Right to Work Act, which would let workers in all 50 states opt out of union dues. Wilson told the Guardian that the bill would \u8220\'20\'1Celiminate forced-dues clauses\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Callow workers to choose for themselves\u8221\'20\'1D. He said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats were on \u8220\'20\'1Ca mission to force unionization\u8221\'20\'1D on \u8220\'20\'1Cworkers by eliminating employee choice\u8221\'20\'1D. Senator Paul said their bill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OYxai0Tt7XY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 would \u8220\'20\'1Cput bargaining power where it belongs}}}\uc2 , in the hands of American workers\u8221\'20\'1D. Unions assert, however, that workers have far more bargaining power by bargaining collectively, rather than as individuals.\par \par \uc2 Cass, who worked in \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2012 presidential campaign, supports steps to give workers more power and said it\u8217\'20\'19s a good time for Republicans to push to increase worker power. Their \u8220\'20\'1Cconstituents are significantly and increasingly working class\u8221\'20\'1D, Cass noted, adding that Republicans might be more willing to distance themselves from corporations now that more business executives \u8220\'20\'1Care on the other side\u8221\'20\'1D, having endorsed Democrats.\par \par \uc2 For years, most Republicans lawmakers have opposed any increase in the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s budget; that agency oversees private-sector union elections and cracks down on employers that break the law in fighting unions. The labor board\u8217\'20\'19s budget hasn\u8217\'20\'19t increased since 2014, a budget freeze that has angered union leaders because they say it hampers the board\u8217\'20\'19s ability to move quickly against law-breaking, anti-union employers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has been flat-funded for a long time,\u8221\'20\'1D said Scott, chair of the House labor committee. \u8220\'20\'1CWith the popularity of unions increasing, the work of the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has increased. In order to get their work done, the board needs significant increases in funding.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Foxx called increasing \uc2 NLRB\uc2  funding {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/17/us-labor-agency-union-activity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Can inherently stupid idea\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , asserting that the labor board tilts in favor of unions, just as Democrats asserted that President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s labor board was far too anti-union.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Samuel voiced dismay that many Republicans seem implacably opposed to anything that would help unions expand. \u8220\'20\'1CAll this,\u8221\'20\'1D Samuel said, \u8220\'20\'1Cillustrates their hostility to make it easier for workers to enjoy what is supposed to be their basic right under the law: to come together to form a union.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221025eiap001e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
