\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republicans in Arizona push measures to curtail citizen-led initiatives\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Leingang in Phoenix \par \uc2 1,462 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2022\par 18:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The option to bypass the legislature, which allowed for marijuana legalization and minimum wage increases, is under threat\par \par \uc2 In November, voters in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/11/us-midterms-congress-senate-house-races-to-watch"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona}}}\uc2  will decide whether to limit citizens\u8217\'20\'19 ability to get measures on the ballot, a change that could severely curtail direct democracy.\par \par \uc2 Three upcoming measures, referred to the ballot by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/11/us-midterms-candidates-who-threaten-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican}}}\uc2  state lawmakers, would require a supermajority to approve any taxes on the ballot, limit initiatives to one topic and allow lawmakers to alter ballot measures if any language is illegal or unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 In total, the measures represent a final step in the Arizona legislature\u8217\'20\'19s years-long attempt to hamper citizen initiatives, which allow voters to circumvent the legislature altogether by collecting signatures from their fellow residents on issues to send to the ballot. With Republicans in control of the legislature, progressive causes \u8212\'20\'14 like minimum wage increases in 2016 and marijuana legalization in 2020 \u8212\'20\'14 have used the initiative process to bypass lawmakers in recent years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen, since minimum wage passed, a very deliberate effort to make direct democracy more difficult and more expensive. This is the knockout punch,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Stacy Pearson\uc2 , a spokesperson for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://willofthepeopleaz.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Will of the People Arizona}}}\uc2 , a campaign against the three measures. \u8220\'20\'1CSo we\u8217\'20\'19re in the last round of the boxing match. And this would be the end.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Arizona is a Petri dish. We have all this outside money coming in\par \par \uc2 Tim Dunn\par \par \uc2 The citizens\u8217\'20\'19 initiative process is enshrined in Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s constitution, and previously led to the state implementing mandatory sick time and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2016/11/08/arizona-minimum-wage-proposition-206-election-results/92970650/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 higher minimum wage}}}\uc2  , legalizing both {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://edition.cnn.com/2010/POLITICS/11/14/arizona.medical.marijuana/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medical}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/11/03/arizona-marijuana-proposition-207-election-results/5997553002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recreational}}}\uc2  marijuana and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azdhs.gov/preparedness/epidemiology-disease-control/smoke-free-arizona/index.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning indoor smoking}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But it\u8217\'20\'19s become costly and legally complicated to run measures here in the past decade, as stricter laws pushed by a Republican legislature threaten to derail citizen-led campaigns. It now costs as much as $10m to collect signatures, not including the additional millions needed to withstand court challenges and front a successful campaign to win.\par \par \uc2 The new measures have support not only from Republican-elected officials, but heavy hitters like the \uc2 Arizona chamber of commerce\uc2  and industry and the state GOP.\par \par \uc2 They join a national trend of GOP-initiated attempts to curtail citizen-led ballot measures. In Arkansas, voters will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/local/nw-arkansas/2022/10/03/arkansas-constitutional-vote-change"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 weigh a measure}}}\uc2  to require a 60% majority for most ballot measures. And in South Dakota in June, voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.argusleader.com/story/news/2022/06/07/amendment-c-primary-election-results-no-south-dakota-tax-threshold/7441949001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected a 60% threshold for tax measures}}}\uc2 , which would have made it more difficult for voters to approve Medicaid expansion at the ballot this November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing is red states trying to curtail this tool that citizens have used really successfully to move policies that are otherwise stuck for, usually, political reasons,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hannah Ledford, deputy executive director and campaigns director for the Fairness Project.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are \u8216\'20\'18watering down their own rights\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Opponents to the three measures caution that the groups that now want to hinder initiatives may have to use the process someday too, if the Republicans lose control of the governor\u8217\'20\'19s office and legislature \u8211\'20\'13 a prospect that\u8217\'20\'19s increasingly likely as the electorate moves closer to the center. Over the years, the pendulum on which party uses the ballot process has swung back and forth multiple times.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat they\u8217\'20\'19re really doing is, they\u8217\'20\'19re watering down their own rights and eventually, it all comes around,\u8221\'20\'1D said Drew Chavez, the owner of Arizona-based signature-gathering firm Petition Partners. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re going to need the process, and what\u8217\'20\'19s gonna happen then?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Danny Seiden, president and CEO of the \uc2 Arizona chamber of commerce\uc2  and industry, said the chamber\u8217\'20\'19s position on these measures would be the same regardless of whether it had strong relationships with lawmakers and the governor, as it does now.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe believe that the best mode of government is to have a higher threshold to pass a tax increase or to require single subjects. I think we\u8217\'20\'19re always going to think that,\u8221\'20\'1D Seiden said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut we could be in a position of needing to use the referral and ballot initiative process \u8211\'20\'13 but if we do, I\u8217\'20\'19d be grateful for [it] being as simple and transparent as possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The three proposals from Republican lawmakers would hinder citizen initiatives in different ways, each of which will affect whether groups will decide to run measures in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the most major change, and one similar to the measure that recently failed in South Dakota, is contained in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://azsos.gov/sites/default/files/prop_132_ballot_format_language.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Proposition 132}}}\uc2 , which would require 60% of voters in order to pass a ballot measure that \u8220\'20\'1Capproves a tax\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Arizona state representative Tim Dunn, the sponsor of the referral, said it was inspired by \u8220\'20\'1Coutside influences\u8221\'20\'1D trying to raise taxes at the ballot box. Lawmakers need a two-thirds vote to pass tax increases, so this measure aligns initiatives with that requirement, he said. He initially wanted all ballot measures to meet a supermajority threshold, but then changed it to tax-related measures, he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://azpbs.org/horizon/2022/09/what-proposition-132-will-mean-for-arizona/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told Arizona PBS}}}\uc2  during a debate on the measure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CArizona is a Petri dish. We have all this outside money coming in,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Under the current law, Prop 132 will only need a simple majority of voters to approve it to become law.\par \par \uc2 But opponents of the measure and people who have run initiatives say they think the measure could apply more broadly. Ballot measures have to be revenue-neutral, and they typically include taxes or fees to fund themselves to ensure they don\u8217\'20\'19t cost the state money.\par \par \uc2 Marijuana legalization, which included taxes on products, received 60% of the vote in November 2020. But it\u8217\'20\'19s not unusual for measures to pass with just a few percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Arizona is \u8216\'20\'18one of the hardest states\u8217\'20\'19 for ballot initiatives\par \par \uc2 Another referral, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://azsos.gov/sites/default/files/prop_129_ballot_format_language.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Proposition 129}}}\uc2 , calls for ballot measures to focus on a single subject, which must be reflected in the title of the measure.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers must follow a single-subject rule. The state budget process, which frequently jammed tons of unrelated provisions into budget bills, was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2022/01/06/court-makes-statement-rules-against-lawmakers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recently struck down}}}\uc2  by the Arizona supreme court for violating single-subject and title laws. The sponsor of the referral, Arizona Republican representative John Kavanagh, said an initiative could still have many provisions, as long as they all relate directly to the subject and title of the measure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo the idea is somebody couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have, in the same measure, some sorely needed roads, but also include [a] provision either for or against teaching critical race theory, because they\u8217\'20\'19re different subject areas,\u8221\'20\'1D Kavanagh said.\par \par \uc2 Pearson, who opposes the campaign, said their side believes measures that modify many parts of law \u8211\'20\'13 like the marijuana measure that included taxes and expungement, or an indoor smoking ban that affected many types of settings \u8211\'20\'13 could be removed from the ballot.\par \par \uc2 The third proposal, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://azsos.gov/sites/default/files/prop_128_ballot_format_language.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Proposition 128}}}\uc2 , relates to measures that contain \u8220\'20\'1Cillegal or unconstitutional language\u8221\'20\'1D, as determined by the courts. If measures have such language, lawmakers can amend or repeal it \u8211\'20\'13 something they cannot do now, because the Voter Protection Act prevents lawmakers from tinkering with voter-approved laws unless they have a three-fourths majority and further the intent of the measure.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s also become more common in recent years for citizen initiatives to face multiple court challenges over their wording and signatures before voters ever see them on the ballot. The implementation of a single-subject rule likely would factor into these court cases.\par \par \uc2 The Arizona supreme court, which expanded to seven justices in 2016, all of whom are Republicans, recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azmirror.com/2022/03/11/voter-approved-invest-in-education-act-ruled-unconstitutional/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aided in striking down a tax}}}\uc2  on wealthy people to fund schools and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/08/30/us/politics/arizona-election-reform-voting.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prevented a broad progressive election measure}}}\uc2  from reaching the ballot.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CArizona is already one of the hardest states in the country to run ballot initiatives,\u8221\'20\'1D Ledford said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I think adding this extra layer of uncertainty around how many votes do you need to win, what subjects can it encompass, and will the legislature undo it immediately on the back end, I think just adds an extra layer of risk \u8211\'20\'13 and certainly \u8211\'20\'13 cost.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Chavez, from the signature-gathering firm, thinks about 80% of those who would want to run a measure wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be able to anymore. Pearson thinks a funder would need a \u8220\'20\'1Climitless budget and a limitless appetite for risk with this court\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Dunn, the sponsor of the supermajority referral, simply said: \u8220\'20\'1CIf it\u8217\'20\'19s harder, it\u8217\'20\'19s harder.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Those opposed to the three measures believe voters will see through the attempt to limit their own power and reject all three.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey seem to have done a really good job in putting together progressives on the merits and libertarians on the government overreach, which \u8211\'20\'13 when you combine that \u8211\'20\'13 we have enough votes to defeat these,\u8221\'20\'1D Pearson said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221014eiae001p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
