\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Alabama prisoners strike over \u8216\'20\'18horrendous\u8217\'20\'19 conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,166 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par 01:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Prisoners refuse to work for no pay in prison jobs in food service, laundry and maintenance\par \par \uc2 The inmate does not want to give their name for fear of being retaliated against, but they do not hold back when it comes to describing the awful conditions that have prompted a widespread jail strike in Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s brutal prisons.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a humanitarian crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D said the inmate currently incarcerated at Limestone correctional facility in Harvest, Alabama. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re not providing us with any adequate medical treatment [or] mental health treatment. We\u8217\'20\'19re not getting proper diets, we\u8217\'20\'19re not getting any kind of rehabilitation in these places.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Prisoners at all 13 prisons in the Alabama department of corrections (Adoc) system started striking from their prison service jobs on 26 September, demanding improvement to conditions, and reforms to harsh sentencing laws and surging parole denials that have long plagued the prison system in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 The prison conditions are horrendous\par \par \uc2 Diyawn Caldwell\par \par \uc2 Prisoners have refused to work prison service jobs such as food service, laundry, maintenance and janitorial jobs that they receive {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.uchicago.edu/story/us-prison-labor-programs-violate-fundamental-human-rights-new-report-finds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 no pay }}}\uc2  to do.\par \par \uc2 The strike has continued with the \uc2 Adoc\uc2  canceling weekend visits and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/news/2022/09/striking-alabama-prisoners-receiving-only-2-daily-meals-movement-restricted.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reducing}}}\uc2  the number of daily meals provided to prisoners from three to two, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/news/2022/09/alabama-prison-work-strike-continues-adoc-cancels-weekend-visits.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claiming }}}\uc2  the measure isn\u8217\'20\'19t retaliatory but a logistical requirement and stating Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s prisons are still fully operational.\par \par \uc2 Prisoners have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FREEALAMOVEMENT/status/1575670572247179264"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked}}}\uc2  photos of the cold, inadequate meals they have been provided with during the strike and a prisoner has publicly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.alreporter.com/2022/09/30/incarcerated-man-alleges-he-was-forced-to-work-against-prison-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  they were transported from a work release center to a higher security prison and forced to work against the strike.\par \par \uc2 The Limestone inmate explained that prison conditions have only worsened in recent years, with parole denials surging in Alabama over the past three years, pushing prisoners to organize a strike to demand improved conditions, and reforms to parole board hearings and harsh sentencing laws that are contributing to significant overcrowding in prisons.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re being warehoused and worked in order to generate more for the state of Alabama,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 As of July 2022, the Alabama department of corrections currently holds {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.doc.state.al.us/docs/MonthlyRpts/July%202022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 20,000 prisoners}}}\uc2  in facilities designed to hold at maximum 12,115 people. In Alabama, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.alreporter.com/2021/02/18/report-alabama-ranks-4th-highest-in-percentage-of-inmates-serving-harshest-sentences/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26% of prisoners}}}\uc2  are serving life sentences or sentences of at least 50 years.\par \par \uc2 While the state\u8217\'20\'19s prisons have been overcrowded, the system is struggling with severe understaffing of correctional officers, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aldailynews.com/adoc-541-correctional-officer-vacancies-across-prisons/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 541 vacancies }}}\uc2  as of August 2022. Parole denials also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.waff.com/2022/03/26/parole-denials-skyrocket-alabama/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly doubled}}}\uc2  to 84% in 2021 compared with 46% in 2017. In fiscal year 2022, parole board denials also hit a historic high of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.alabamasmartjustice.org/reports/report-parole-denied"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 89%}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been campaigning on these issues and trying to get somebody to do something. When the DoJ came out with their report in 2019, we became optimistic and we sat around and sat around and sat around. We\u8217\'20\'19re still sitting around and nobody has done nothing,\u8221\'20\'1D the inmate added. \u8220\'20\'1CAlabama is never gonna do the right thing because it\u8217\'20\'19s the right thing to do, they will always have to be compelled by force to do the right thing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They noted that Alabama had been required by the federal government to change racist laws throughout its history. Even the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution \u8211\'20\'13 adopted in 1901 \u8211\'20\'13 was created \u8220\'20\'1Cto establish white supremacy\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/opinion/2017/06/alabama_all_are_not_created_eq.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to John B Knox, who presided over the Alabama constitutional convention.\par \par \uc2 These interventions include the federal government forcing Alabama to eliminate slavery during the civil war in the 1860s, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/wlulr/vol72/iss4/3/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  debt peonage}}}\uc2  in the early 20th century, closing convict leasing loopholes in 1941 under President Franklin Roosevelt, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/opinion/2014/08/post_105.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 school segregation}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/opinion/2014/08/post_105.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 discriminatory voting laws}}}\uc2  in the 1960s as Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s leadership opposed it.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the federal government began the process of forcing Alabama to fix its prison system, when the \uc2 US Department of Justice{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-alleges-conditions-alabama-mens-prisons-violate-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 alleged}}}\uc2  the Alabama prison system of violating the US constitution due to poor conditions. It even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-files-lawsuit-against-state-alabama-unconstitutional-conditions-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}\uc2  a lawsuit against the state over prison conditions in 2020. In 2021, the justice department issued a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/news/2021/11/new-department-of-justice-complaint-says-alabama-has-not-improved-prison-conditions-since-2019-allegations.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 complaint}}}\uc2  alleging Alabama had not improved conditions.\par \par \uc2 Alabama is still fighting those allegations in court against the federal government, disputing constitutional violations. The complaints and allegations include overcrowding, understaffing and a failure to protect prisoners from violence, sexual abuse and excessive force from staff.\par \par \uc2 The trial is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/news/2022/05/alabama-justice-department-headed-toward-trial-over-prison-conditions-in-2024.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scheduled}}}\uc2  to begin in November 2024, but prisoners and advocates have noted conditions have only gotten worse while the state builds {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/coronavirus-pandemic-business-prisons-montgomery-kay-ivey-8a7d30c43f4e61987051368a9604fda9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/news/2021/10/gov-kay-ivey-calls-alabama-prison-building-plan-major-step-forward.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  new prisons}}}\uc2 , diverting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.splcenter.org/news/2022/07/01/alabama-diverts-400-million-covid-funds-to-prisons"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $400m }}}\uc2  (\u163\'00\'A3354m) in Covid-19 relief funds from the federal government toward construction.\par \par \uc2 Adoc\uc2  recently went into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.waaytv.com/news/judge-orders-alabama-department-of-corrections-into-mediation-to-solve-mental-health-housing-issues/article_a62911aa-231c-11ed-950b-872458052f1d.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mediation}}}\uc2  after being sued for improperly housing prisoners with serious mental health illnesses. It has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.waaytv.com/news/judge-orders-alabama-department-of-corrections-into-mediation-to-solve-mental-health-housing-issues/article_a62911aa-231c-11ed-950b-872458052f1d.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ordered}}}\uc2  to finalize plans to create an external monitoring team to review and implement changes of \uc2 Adoc\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s mental health staffing ratios.\par \par \uc2 Frank Ozment, a Birmingham-based attorney leading the case, said the lack of staffing has worsened numerous problems within the \uc2 Adoc\uc2 , including an inability to properly provide mental health or medical care to prisoners.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, a federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/2021/12/27/judge-orders-alabama-prison-system-hire-more-correctional-officers/9031122002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled}}}\uc2  Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s mental healthcare in prisons was in violation of the US constitution\u8217\'20\'19s ban on cruel and unusual punishment and ordered the \uc2 Adoc\uc2  to improve staffing.\par \par \uc2 They will get retaliated against for the smallest things\par \par \uc2 Diyawn Caldwell\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are so few correctional officers that they can\u8217\'20\'19t get inmates to mental health appointments, even when the appointments are scheduled to occur inside of the prison,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ozment, who has filed several prison reform lawsuits. \u8220\'20\'1CI got an affidavit from one of the people in charge of keeping up with the mental health appointments inside the system, and she said they have about 600 mental health appointments a day inside the system and roughly 400 of them go unfulfilled \u8211\'20\'13 are missed \u8211\'20\'13 because they don\u8217\'20\'19t have enough correctional officers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.alabamasmartjustice.org/reports/documents-reveal-a-grim-outlook-for-parole-eligible-people-incarcerated-in-alabamas-violent-overcrowded-prisons"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appointed}}}\uc2  new leaders to the state\u8217\'20\'19s parole board in 2019, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2021/12/01/alabama-prisons-humanitarian-crisis-523548"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 coinciding}}}\uc2  with a significant drop in parole board hearings and the number of paroles granted. A disproportionate majority of inmates granted parole are white while the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prisonpolicy.org/profiles/AL.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 majority of Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s prison population is black.}}}\par \par \uc2 Prison reform advocates have criticized Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s harsh laws and sentencing for years. Alabama has a history of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/news/federal-court-orders-alabama-to-preserve-evidence-of-botched-attempted-execution-of-alan-miller#:~:text=A%20federal%20district%20court%20has,Miller%20on%20September%2022%2C%202022."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent botched executions}}}\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://eji.org/issues/alabama-death-penalty/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest death penalty sentences}}}\uc2  per capita in the US. In 2021, Alabama prisons {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.alabamasmartjustice.org/reports/2021-marks-deadliest-year-for-alabama-prisons"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  a record number of deaths of prisoners due to violence and drug overdoses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe prison conditions are horrendous,\u8221\'20\'1D said Diyawn Caldwell, founder of the advocacy group Both Sides of the Wall and organizer of the strike. \u8220\'20\'1CThey do not provide the basic necessities, a lot of times they have to beg for toiletries and the basics that they need, and they still won\u8217\'20\'19t get them,\u8221\'20\'1D said Caldwell, whose husband is currently incarcerated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey will get retaliated against for the smallest things. The culture of the \uc2 Adoc\uc2  is to bully, retaliate and place fear into people to get what they want and stop what they don\u8217\'20\'19t want.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221006eia6001gt\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0